window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["ansible"]=[{"id":"ansible-f0001","card":"q-ansible-001-what-ansible-is-and-how-it-differs-from","sec":"ansible-s002","q":"What is Ansible, and why is it used?","a":"<p>Ansible automates configuration management, application deployment and ad-hoc tasks. It is agentless (uses SSH), simple (YAML-based), cuts manual errors and keeps systems consistent.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0002","card":"q-ansible-001-what-ansible-is-and-how-it-differs-from","sec":"ansible-s002","q":"How does Ansible differ from Puppet or Chef?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Ansible</strong>: agentless (SSH), push-based, YAML playbooks.</li>\n  <li><strong>Puppet/Chef</strong>: agent-based, pull-based, use a DSL or Ruby.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ansible is simpler and lighter, with no master-agent complexity.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0003","card":"q-ansible-001-what-ansible-is-and-how-it-differs-from","sec":"ansible-s002","q":"What pain point does Ansible solve compared with configuring servers by hand or with shell scripts?","a":"<p>Hand configuration drifts: servers end up slightly different and nobody can say why. Shell scripts are written as steps, so running them twice can break things. Ansible describes the desired state in YAML and its modules only change what differs, so the same playbook can be re-run safely on one server or a thousand and keeps them consistent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0004","card":"q-ansible-001-what-ansible-is-and-how-it-differs-from","sec":"ansible-s002","q":"In Ansible, what are the practical advantages and drawbacks of being agentless?","a":"<p>Advantages: nothing to install or patch on managed nodes (only SSH and Python), a smaller attack surface, and quick onboarding of a new host. Drawbacks: every run opens connections from the control node, which gets slower at very large scale. Nothing enforces state between runs, so drift is only corrected when you run a playbook (or schedule it).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0005","card":"q-ansible-001-what-ansible-is-and-how-it-differs-from","sec":"ansible-s002","q":"How does Ansible's push model differ from the pull model of Puppet and Chef, and how do you get pull behaviour with Ansible?","a":"<p>In push, the control node connects to the targets when you run a playbook. In pull, an agent on each node checks in with a server on a schedule and applies its configuration. Ansible has <code>ansible-pull</code>, which runs on the node, fetches a playbook from a Git repository and applies it locally, usually from cron. Use that when nodes are behind NAT or autoscale and cannot be reached by SSH.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0006","card":"q-ansible-001-what-ansible-is-and-how-it-differs-from","sec":"ansible-s002","q":"Ansible versus Terraform: which one provisions infrastructure and which one configures it, and can they be combined?","a":"<p>Terraform is built for provisioning and tracking infrastructure (VMs, networks, load balancers) through a state file. Ansible is built for configuring what runs on machines (packages, files, services) and for orchestrating deployments. They are often combined: Terraform creates the instances and Ansible configures them. Ansible can also create cloud resources through collections, but it keeps no state file to detect drift or destroy things cleanly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0007","card":"q-ansible-001-what-ansible-is-and-how-it-differs-from","sec":"ansible-s002","q":"Ansible runs tasks in the order they are written, while Puppet builds a dependency graph. What does that mean when you design a playbook?","a":"<p>In Ansible you control order explicitly by task position, so a playbook reads top to bottom and is easy to follow. Puppet resolves ordering from declared relationships. The consequence for Ansible is that you must put prerequisites first (install a package before configuring its service) and cannot rely on the tool to infer dependencies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0008","card":"q-ansible-002-architecture","sec":"ansible-s003","q":"What is the architecture of Ansible?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Control node</strong>: runs Ansible.</li>\n  <li><strong>Managed nodes</strong>: the targets; need SSH and Python.</li>\n  <li><strong>Inventory</strong>: lists hosts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Modules</strong>: the task executors.</li>\n  <li><strong>Playbooks</strong>: YAML workflows.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0009","card":"q-ansible-002-architecture","sec":"ansible-s003","q":"In Ansible, where do the modules actually execute: on the control node or on the managed node?","a":"<p>On the managed node. Ansible copies the module to a temporary directory on the target (over SSH), runs it with the target's Python, collects its JSON result and deletes the file. The control node only orchestrates and renders templates. This is why managed nodes need Python for most modules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0010","card":"q-ansible-002-architecture","sec":"ansible-s003","q":"Why does Ansible need Python on managed nodes, and how do you manage a host that has none?","a":"<p>Most modules are Python programs that run on the target, and fact gathering also needs Python. For a host without Python (a fresh minimal image or a network device), use the <code>raw</code> module, which just sends a command over SSH, to install Python first. Run that play with <code>gather_facts: no</code>, because fact gathering itself needs Python.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0011","card":"q-ansible-002-architecture","sec":"ansible-s003","q":"What is the difference between the <code>ansible-core</code> package and the <code>ansible</code> community package?","a":"<p><code>ansible-core</code> contains the engine, the CLI tools and the <code>ansible.builtin</code> modules and plugins. The <code>ansible</code> package bundles <code>ansible-core</code> plus a curated set of collections (for AWS, Kubernetes, network and many more). Since Ansible 2.10, most modules live in collections, so a minimal install may need <code>ansible-galaxy collection install</code> for what you use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0012","card":"q-ansible-002-architecture","sec":"ansible-s003","q":"Can the control node be a Windows machine?","a":"<p>Not natively: the control node needs a POSIX system (Linux or macOS), and on Windows you run it inside WSL. Windows hosts can still be managed nodes, using WinRM (or SSH) instead of SSH with Python.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0013","card":"q-ansible-003-playbooks-roles-modules","sec":"ansible-s004","q":"What is an Ansible Playbook?","a":"<p>A YAML file defining tasks and workflows for automation (for example install Nginx, then start it). It is the blueprint for what happens on which hosts.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0014","card":"q-ansible-003-playbooks-roles-modules","sec":"ansible-s004","q":"Difference between Playbooks and Roles?","a":"<p>A playbook is a full automation script. A role is a modular, reusable chunk (tasks, vars, templates) that playbooks call for structure.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0015","card":"q-ansible-003-playbooks-roles-modules","sec":"ansible-s004","q":"What are Ansible Modules? How do they work?","a":"<p>Modules are scripts that perform tasks (file operations, package installs). Ansible ships them to the hosts over SSH, executes them, and cleans up. They are idempotent by design.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0016","card":"q-ansible-003-playbooks-roles-modules","sec":"ansible-s004","q":"What is an Inventory file and what is its purpose?","a":"<p>It lists the hosts (IPs, names, groups) Ansible targets, static or dynamic, for example <code>[webservers] web1</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0017","card":"q-ansible-003-playbooks-roles-modules","sec":"ansible-s004","q":"What is the difference between a play and a playbook in Ansible?","a":"<p>A playbook is a YAML file containing a list of plays. A play maps a group of hosts to a list of tasks (plus variables, handlers and settings such as <code>become</code>). One playbook can hold several plays, for example one for database hosts and one for web hosts, run in order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0018","card":"q-ansible-003-playbooks-roles-modules","sec":"ansible-s004","q":"In Ansible, what is the difference between a module and a plugin?","a":"<p>A module is a unit of work that runs on the managed node (install a package, copy a file). A plugin extends Ansible itself and runs on the control node: connection plugins, inventory plugins, lookup and filter plugins, callback plugins. When you write <code>{{ x | upper }}</code> or <code>lookup('file', ...)</code> you are using plugins, not modules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0019","card":"q-ansible-003-playbooks-roles-modules","sec":"ansible-s004","q":"Why is it recommended to write <code>ansible.builtin.copy</code> instead of just <code>copy</code> in a task?","a":"<p>The fully qualified collection name (FQCN) names the collection that owns the module. Since modules moved into collections (Ansible 2.10+), a short name can resolve to a different collection's module with the same name. The FQCN removes that ambiguity and makes the playbook explicit and lint-clean.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0020","card":"q-ansible-003-playbooks-roles-modules","sec":"ansible-s004","q":"Why are Ansible modules described as idempotent, and which common modules are not?","a":"<p>Most modules take a desired state (<code>state: present</code>), check the current state, and act only on a difference, reporting <code>changed</code> or <code>ok</code>. <code>command</code>, <code>shell</code> and <code>raw</code> do not know the desired state, so they report <code>changed</code> every run unless you add <code>creates</code>, <code>removes</code> or <code>changed_when</code>. Prefer a purpose-built module over these.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0021","card":"q-ansible-003-playbooks-roles-modules","sec":"ansible-s004","q":"What is the purpose of an inventory in Ansible beyond listing hosts?","a":"<p>It also groups hosts and attaches variables to hosts and groups (connection user, ports, environment settings). Plays then target groups instead of individual machines, and the same playbook behaves differently per group through those variables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0022","card":"q-ansible-004-connection-and-transport","sec":"ansible-s005","q":"How does Ansible connect to remote hosts?","a":"<p>Via SSH by default. The control node authenticates (keys or passwords), sends modules, runs them with Python, and removes them. WinRM is used for Windows.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0023","card":"q-ansible-004-connection-and-transport","sec":"ansible-s005","q":"Default transport mechanism?","a":"<p>SSH. It is secure, agentless, and relies on the existing SSH setup on the hosts.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0024","card":"q-ansible-004-connection-and-transport","sec":"ansible-s005","q":"How do you control which user, port and key Ansible uses to reach a host?","a":"<p>Set connection variables on the host or group in the inventory (<code>ansible_user</code>, <code>ansible_port</code>, <code>ansible_ssh_private_key_file</code>), or set defaults in <code>ansible.cfg</code> (<code>remote_user</code>, <code>private_key_file</code>), or pass flags such as <code>-u</code> and <code>--private-key</code>. Inventory variables are more specific, so they win over the <code>ansible.cfg</code> defaults.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0025","card":"q-ansible-004-connection-and-transport","sec":"ansible-s005","q":"How would you run Ansible against hosts that are only reachable through a bastion (jump) host?","a":"<p>Tell SSH to hop through the bastion with <code>ansible_ssh_common_args</code> on the group, for example <code>-o ProxyJump=user@bastion</code> (older setups use <code>ProxyCommand</code>). The control node still runs everything, and the SSH client does the hop, so the private hosts need no direct route from the control node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0026","card":"q-ansible-004-connection-and-transport","sec":"ansible-s005","q":"What are SSH <code>ControlPersist</code> and <code>pipelining</code> for in Ansible?","a":"<p>Both reduce overhead per task. <code>ControlPersist</code> keeps one SSH connection open and reuses it for later tasks. <code>pipelining</code> sends the module over the existing connection instead of copying it to a temp file first, which cuts round trips. Pipelining is off by default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0027","card":"q-ansible-004-connection-and-transport","sec":"ansible-s005","q":"How do you run a task against the control node itself without SSH?","a":"<p>Use <code>connection: local</code> on the play, or set <code>ansible_connection=local</code> for the host, usually with <code>hosts: localhost</code>. Ansible then runs the modules directly with the local Python. This is typical for calling cloud APIs or building files on the control node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0028","card":"q-ansible-004-connection-and-transport","sec":"ansible-s005","q":"Which connection method does Ansible use for Windows hosts, and why not SSH with Python?","a":"<p>WinRM (PowerShell remoting) by default, with SSH also supported on newer setups. Windows has no Python for Ansible's modules, so Windows modules are PowerShell scripts and use the <code>win_*</code> module family.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0029","card":"q-ansible-005-ansible-vs-ansible-playbook-idempotency","sec":"ansible-s006","q":"Difference between <code>ansible</code> and <code>ansible-playbook</code>?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>ansible</code>: ad-hoc, single tasks (<code>ansible all -m ping</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>ansible-playbook</code>: runs full YAML playbooks for structured automation.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0030","card":"q-ansible-005-ansible-vs-ansible-playbook-idempotency","sec":"ansible-s006","q":"What is idempotency and why is it key?","a":"<p>Tasks only change a system if needed, for example skipping an install when the package is already present. That keeps results consistent, avoids redundant actions, and makes runs reliable.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0031","card":"q-ansible-005-ansible-vs-ansible-playbook-idempotency","sec":"ansible-s006","q":"When would you use an ad-hoc <code>ansible</code> command instead of a playbook, and when not?","a":"<p>Ad-hoc is good for one-off, quick actions: check connectivity, restart a service on a few hosts, check disk space, copy a file. It is not recorded in version control or repeatable. Anything you will do more than once, or that has several steps, belongs in a playbook.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0032","card":"q-ansible-005-ansible-vs-ansible-playbook-idempotency","sec":"ansible-s006","q":"Name the Ansible command-line tools besides <code>ansible</code> and <code>ansible-playbook</code>, and what each does.","a":"<p><code>ansible-doc</code> (module documentation), <code>ansible-galaxy</code> (install and create roles and collections), <code>ansible-vault</code> (encrypt secrets), <code>ansible-inventory</code> (inspect inventory), <code>ansible-config</code> (show effective configuration), <code>ansible-pull</code> (pull and apply a playbook from Git on the node), and <code>ansible-console</code> (interactive shell).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0033","card":"q-ansible-005-ansible-vs-ansible-playbook-idempotency","sec":"ansible-s006","q":"How can you verify that a playbook is idempotent?","a":"<p>Run it twice against the same hosts. The second run should report <code>changed=0</code> in the recap. Running with <code>--check --diff</code> also previews what would change. Tasks that keep reporting <code>changed</code> usually use <code>command</code> or <code>shell</code> without <code>creates</code> or <code>changed_when</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0034","card":"q-ansible-005-ansible-vs-ansible-playbook-idempotency","sec":"ansible-s006","q":"Why does idempotency matter in practice, not just in theory?","a":"<p>It makes a playbook safe to re-run after a partial failure, safe to run on a schedule to fix drift, and safe to use for both first-time setup and updates. Without it you must write \"only if not done yet\" logic yourself, and a retry might duplicate or corrupt things.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0035","card":"q-ansible-005-ansible-vs-ansible-playbook-idempotency","sec":"ansible-s006","q":"In the PLAY RECAP of <code>ansible-playbook</code>, what do <code>ok</code>, <code>changed</code>, <code>failed</code> and <code>unreachable</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>ok</code> is the number of tasks that ran with no change needed (changed tasks are counted in <code>ok</code> too). <code>changed</code> is tasks that modified something. <code>failed</code> is tasks that errored. <code>unreachable</code> is hosts Ansible could not connect to, such as SSH failures. <code>skipped</code> and <code>rescued</code> are counted separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0036","card":"q-ansible-006-prerequisites-and-installation","sec":"ansible-s007","q":"Prerequisites for installing Ansible?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Control node</strong>: Linux or macOS, Python 3.8+, SSH client.</li>\n  <li><strong>Managed nodes</strong>: SSH server, Python 2.6+/3.5+, network access. No agents.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0037","card":"q-ansible-006-prerequisites-and-installation","sec":"ansible-s007","q":"How to install Ansible on Linux?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo apt update\nsudo apt install ansible\n# or\npip install ansible\nansible --version</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0038","card":"q-ansible-006-prerequisites-and-installation","sec":"ansible-s007","q":"How to upgrade Ansible?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>sudo apt install --only-upgrade ansible\npip install --upgrade ansible</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0039","card":"q-ansible-006-prerequisites-and-installation","sec":"ansible-s007","q":"Why would you install Ansible with <code>pip</code> in a virtual environment instead of the OS package manager?","a":"<p>The distro package is often an older version and is tied to the OS release. With <code>pip</code> in a virtualenv you choose and pin the version per project, can keep several versions side by side, and upgrade without touching system Python. Pin it in a <code>requirements.txt</code> so every teammate and the CI use the same version.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0040","card":"q-ansible-006-prerequisites-and-installation","sec":"ansible-s007","q":"What does <code>ansible --version</code> tell you beyond the version number, and why is it useful when debugging?","a":"<p>It prints the config file in use, the module search paths, the Python version and path used by Ansible, and the executable location. That quickly shows whether the <code>ansible.cfg</code> you edited is the one being read, or whether a different Python or install is being used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0041","card":"q-ansible-006-prerequisites-and-installation","sec":"ansible-s007","q":"What is required on a managed node before Ansible can manage it?","a":"<p>Network access to it, an SSH server, a user that can log in (usually with key authentication) and that can escalate to root through <code>sudo</code> if needed, and Python for most modules. Nothing else needs installing, because Ansible is agentless.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0042","card":"q-ansible-006-prerequisites-and-installation","sec":"ansible-s007","q":"Why is SSH key authentication preferred over passwords for Ansible?","a":"<p>Runs can touch many hosts and be automated, so typing passwords does not scale and passwords in files are a risk. Keys allow non-interactive login, can be restricted per user, and can be loaded in <code>ssh-agent</code>. Passwords, when unavoidable, belong in Ansible Vault or a secrets manager.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0043","card":"q-ansible-007-the-ansible-cfg-file","sec":"ansible-s008","q":"Purpose of the <code>ansible.cfg</code> file?","a":"<p>It customizes Ansible: inventory path, SSH options, escalation settings. It avoids repeating CLI flags and keeps runs consistent.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[defaults]\ninventory = ./inventory\nremote_user = ansible\nprivate_key_file = ~/.ssh/id_rsa\nhost_key_checking = False\nretry_files_enabled = False\nlog_path = ./ansible.log\n\n[privilege_escalation]\nbecome = True\nbecome_method = sudo\nbecome_user = root\nbecome_ask_pass = False</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0044","card":"q-ansible-007-the-ansible-cfg-file","sec":"ansible-s008","q":"In which order does Ansible look for its configuration file, and which one wins?","a":"<p>The first found wins, and files are not merged: the <code>ANSIBLE_CONFIG</code> environment variable, then <code>./ansible.cfg</code> in the current directory, then <code>~/.ansible.cfg</code>, then <code>/etc/ansible/ansible.cfg</code>. A project-level file in the working directory is the usual choice so settings travel with the repository.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0045","card":"q-ansible-007-the-ansible-cfg-file","sec":"ansible-s008","q":"How do you find out which <code>ansible.cfg</code> is active and which settings differ from the defaults?","a":"<p><code>ansible --version</code> shows the config file path in use. <code>ansible-config dump --only-changed</code> lists settings that differ from defaults and where each came from. <code>ansible-config view</code> prints the active file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0046","card":"q-ansible-007-the-ansible-cfg-file","sec":"ansible-s008","q":"What is the precedence between <code>ansible.cfg</code>, environment variables and command-line flags?","a":"<p>Command-line flags override environment variables, which override <code>ansible.cfg</code>, which overrides built-in defaults. For example <code>ANSIBLE_FORKS=20</code> beats <code>forks</code> in the file, and <code>-f 50</code> beats both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0047","card":"q-ansible-007-the-ansible-cfg-file","sec":"ansible-s008","q":"Why might Ansible silently ignore an <code>ansible.cfg</code> in the current directory?","a":"<p>If the directory is world-writable, Ansible refuses to load its <code>ansible.cfg</code> for security, because another user could plant a malicious config. It prints a warning. Fix the directory permissions, or point to the file with <code>ANSIBLE_CONFIG</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0048","card":"q-ansible-007-the-ansible-cfg-file","sec":"ansible-s008","q":"In this <code>ansible.cfg</code>, which section holds <code>become</code> and why is it separate from <code>[defaults]</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>[privilege_escalation]</code> section. It groups the settings that govern how Ansible gains higher privileges after connecting (<code>become</code>, <code>become_method</code>, <code>become_user</code>, <code>become_ask_pass</code>), separate from connection and general settings in <code>[defaults]</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[defaults]\ninventory = ./inventory\nremote_user = ansible\n[privilege_escalation]\nbecome = True\nbecome_method = sudo</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0049","card":"q-ansible-008-ansible-cfg-settings-explained","sec":"ansible-s009","q":"In an <code>ansible.cfg</code>, what does <code>host_key_checking = False</code> change, and why is it risky in production?","a":"<p>It stops SSH from verifying the remote host key against <code>known_hosts</code>, so first connections do not prompt and changed keys are accepted. This removes protection against man-in-the-middle attacks. In production keep it enabled and pre-populate <code>known_hosts</code> (for example with <code>ssh-keyscan</code> or your provisioning step).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0050","card":"q-ansible-008-ansible-cfg-settings-explained","sec":"ansible-s009","q":"What does <code>retry_files_enabled = False</code> do in <code>ansible.cfg</code>?","a":"<p>It stops Ansible from writing a <code>.retry</code> file listing the hosts that failed in a playbook run. Those files were meant for <code>--limit @file.retry</code> reruns but clutter the project directory. They are disabled by default in current Ansible versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0051","card":"q-ansible-008-ansible-cfg-settings-explained","sec":"ansible-s009","q":"What does <code>log_path</code> in <code>ansible.cfg</code> do, and what should you watch for when enabling it?","a":"<p>It writes Ansible run output to a file, giving an audit trail of what ran and when. Make sure the user running Ansible can write to it, and note that task output may include sensitive values unless tasks use <code>no_log: true</code>. Rotate the file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0052","card":"q-ansible-008-ansible-cfg-settings-explained","sec":"ansible-s009","q":"Why enable <code>become</code> globally in <code>ansible.cfg</code>, and why might a task-level <code>become</code> be better?","a":"<p>A global setting saves repeating it, but every task then runs as root, which breaks the least privilege principle and can create files owned by root where a normal user was wanted. Setting <code>become: yes</code> only on the plays or tasks that need it limits privileged actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0053","card":"q-ansible-009-ad-hoc-commands","sec":"ansible-s010","q":"Run a single task without a playbook?","a":"<p>Use <code>ansible</code> with <code>-m</code> (module) and <code>-a</code> (args):</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible all -m ping\nansible webservers -m command -a &quot;uptime&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0054","card":"q-ansible-009-ad-hoc-commands","sec":"ansible-s010","q":"What is <code>ansible-doc</code>?","a":"<p>It shows module documentation, usage and parameters:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-doc apt</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0055","card":"q-ansible-009-ad-hoc-commands","sec":"ansible-s010","q":"In this ad-hoc command, what do <code>-m</code> and <code>-a</code> do, and what is the module if you leave out <code>-m</code>?","a":"<p><code>-m</code> picks the module and <code>-a</code> passes its arguments as one string. If <code>-m</code> is left out, Ansible uses the <code>command</code> module by default. <code>webservers</code> is the host pattern from the inventory.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible webservers -m command -a &quot;uptime&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0056","card":"q-ansible-009-ad-hoc-commands","sec":"ansible-s010","q":"How do you run an ad-hoc command with root privileges, for example installing a package on all web servers?","a":"<p>Add <code>-b</code> (<code>--become</code>), as in <code>ansible webservers -b -m apt -a \"name=nginx state=present\"</code>. Without it the task runs as the connecting user and fails on permission errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0057","card":"q-ansible-009-ad-hoc-commands","sec":"ansible-s010","q":"How do you find out the parameters of a module while working at the command line?","a":"<p>Use <code>ansible-doc &lt;module&gt;</code> for full docs, <code>ansible-doc -s &lt;module&gt;</code> for a short snippet of parameters, and <code>ansible-doc -l</code> to list all modules. It works offline, using the docs bundled with the installed version, so it matches what you actually run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0058","card":"q-ansible-009-ad-hoc-commands","sec":"ansible-s010","q":"Name two ad-hoc commands that are useful in an incident.","a":"<p><code>ansible all -m ping</code> to see which hosts are reachable. <code>ansible webservers -b -m service -a \"name=nginx state=restarted\"</code> to restart a service on one group. Others: <code>-m shell -a \"df -h\"</code> for disk space and <code>-m setup -a \"filter=ansible_memfree_mb\"</code> for facts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0059","card":"q-ansible-009-ad-hoc-commands","sec":"ansible-s010","q":"How do you control how many hosts an ad-hoc command works on at the same time?","a":"<p>Use <code>-f</code> (<code>--forks</code>), for example <code>ansible all -f 20 -m ping</code>. The default is 5. Combine it with <code>-l</code> to restrict the hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0060","card":"q-ansible-010-static-and-dynamic-inventories","sec":"ansible-s011","q":"Types of inventories?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Static</strong>: a manual host list (file).</li>\n  <li><strong>Dynamic</strong>: a script or plugin pulls hosts (for example from AWS).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Static suits small setups; dynamic scales.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0061","card":"q-ansible-010-static-and-dynamic-inventories","sec":"ansible-s011","q":"Dynamic vs static inventory?","a":"<p>Dynamic auto-fetches hosts from sources such as AWS via scripts or plugins. Static is a fixed, manual file. Dynamic scales, static is simple.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0062","card":"q-ansible-010-static-and-dynamic-inventories","sec":"ansible-s011","q":"When is a static inventory the wrong choice and a dynamic one the right one?","a":"<p>When the hosts change often: cloud instances in autoscaling groups come and go and have new IPs, so a hand-edited file is out of date at once. A dynamic inventory queries the provider's API on each run and returns the current hosts, grouped by tags, regions or other attributes. Static suits stable, small, on-premises setups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0063","card":"q-ansible-010-static-and-dynamic-inventories","sec":"ansible-s011","q":"What are the two ways Ansible can build an inventory dynamically, and which one should you use?","a":"<p>Inventory plugins (YAML config files such as <code>aws_ec2.yml</code>) and legacy inventory scripts that print JSON. Use plugins: they are the supported, maintained approach, they support caching and <code>keyed_groups</code>, and scripts are the old way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0064","card":"q-ansible-010-static-and-dynamic-inventories","sec":"ansible-s011","q":"How can you combine a static file and a dynamic inventory in one run?","a":"<p>Pass <code>-i</code> several times, or point <code>-i</code> at a directory containing a static file and plugin config files. Ansible merges all of them into one inventory. This is common when most hosts are in the cloud and a few appliances are fixed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0065","card":"q-ansible-010-static-and-dynamic-inventories","sec":"ansible-s011","q":"How do you see what an inventory resolves to without running a playbook?","a":"<p><code>ansible-inventory -i &lt;inventory&gt; --list</code> prints the full JSON including variables, and <code>--graph</code> shows the group tree. <code>ansible &lt;pattern&gt; --list-hosts</code> shows which hosts a pattern matches.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0066","card":"q-ansible-011-defining-and-grouping-hosts","sec":"ansible-s012","q":"Define a static inventory / group hosts?","a":"<p>INI:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[webservers]\nweb1.example.com\n[dbservers]\ndb1.example.com</code></pre></div>\n<p>YAML:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>all:\n  children:\n    webservers:\n      hosts:\n        web1.example.com:</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use with <code>-i hosts</code>, and target groups in playbooks (<code>hosts: webservers</code>).</p>\n<p>A fuller INI inventory with two hosts per group:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[webservers]\nweb1.example.com ansible_host=192.168.1.10\nweb2.example.com ansible_host=192.168.1.11\n[dbservers]\ndb1.example.com ansible_host=192.168.1.20\ndb2.example.com ansible_host=192.168.1.21</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0067","card":"q-ansible-011-defining-and-grouping-hosts","sec":"ansible-s012","q":"What groups does Ansible always create, even if you do not define them?","a":"<p><code>all</code> (every host) and <code>ungrouped</code> (hosts that belong to no other group). That is why <code>hosts: all</code> works without any group definition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0068","card":"q-ansible-011-defining-and-grouping-hosts","sec":"ansible-s012","q":"How do you make a group of groups in an INI inventory, for example a <code>prod</code> group containing <code>webservers</code> and <code>dbservers</code>?","a":"<p>Add a section with the <code>:children</code> suffix:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[prod:children]\nwebservers\ndbservers</code></pre></div>\n<p>Variables set on <code>prod</code> then apply to all hosts in both child groups, which is handy for environment-wide settings.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[webservers]\nweb1.example.com\n[dbservers]\ndb1.example.com</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0069","card":"q-ansible-011-defining-and-grouping-hosts","sec":"ansible-s012","q":"How do you express a range of similar hosts compactly in an Ansible inventory?","a":"<p>Use a range pattern such as <code>web[01:10].example.com</code>, which expands to ten hosts. Alphabetic ranges work too (<code>db-[a:c].example.com</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0070","card":"q-ansible-011-defining-and-grouping-hosts","sec":"ansible-s012","q":"In a playbook, how do you target hosts that are in the <code>webservers</code> group but not in <code>staging</code>?","a":"<p>Use a host pattern: <code>hosts: webservers:!staging</code>. Also <code>:&amp;</code> for intersection (<code>webservers:&amp;prod</code>) and <code>:</code> for union (<code>webservers:dbservers</code>). Quote patterns containing <code>!</code> on the shell.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0071","card":"q-ansible-011-defining-and-grouping-hosts","sec":"ansible-s012","q":"What is the difference between the INI and YAML inventory formats, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>They describe the same data. INI is shorter and quicker for simple lists. YAML handles nested groups and structured variables (lists, dictionaries) more cleanly, and has no INI quirks where a value is always parsed as a string. Pick YAML when variables are complex.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0072","card":"q-ansible-012-inventory-variables-and-ansible-host","sec":"ansible-s013","q":"Specify variables in an inventory?","a":"<p>INI:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>web1.example.com ansible_user=admin\n[webservers:vars]\nhttp_port=80</code></pre></div>\n<p>YAML:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>webservers:\n  hosts:\n    web1.example.com:\n      ansible_user: admin\n  vars:\n    http_port: 80</code></pre></div>\n<p>These are used in tasks and templates.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0073","card":"q-ansible-012-inventory-variables-and-ansible-host","sec":"ansible-s013","q":"Purpose of the <code>ansible_host</code> variable?","a":"<p>It sets the real IP or hostname for an alias. With the line below, targeting <code>web1</code> connects to <code>192.168.1.10</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>web1 ansible_host=192.168.1.10</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0074","card":"q-ansible-012-inventory-variables-and-ansible-host","sec":"ansible-s013","q":"Why use <code>ansible_host</code> instead of just putting the IP address as the inventory name?","a":"<p>It separates a stable, readable alias (<code>web1</code>) from the address Ansible connects to. The alias is what appears in output, <code>inventory_hostname</code> and <code>hostvars</code>, so you can change the IP in one place without renaming the host or touching <code>group_vars</code> and <code>host_vars</code> files named after it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0075","card":"q-ansible-012-inventory-variables-and-ansible-host","sec":"ansible-s013","q":"What is the precedence between a host variable and a group variable with the same name in an inventory?","a":"<p>The host variable wins over the group variable. More specific beats more general. A child group also overrides its parent group, and among groups at the same level, the later one alphabetically wins unless <code>ansible_group_priority</code> is set. <code>all</code> is overridden by every specific group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0076","card":"q-ansible-012-inventory-variables-and-ansible-host","sec":"ansible-s013","q":"In the inventory snippet, what does the <code>[webservers:vars]</code> section do?","a":"<p>It sets the variable <code>http_port</code> to 80 for every host in <code>webservers</code>. The <code>:vars</code> suffix marks a section of group variables instead of hosts. Note that INI values are read as strings unless typed in YAML.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[webservers:vars]\nhttp_port=80</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0077","card":"q-ansible-012-inventory-variables-and-ansible-host","sec":"ansible-s013","q":"Name the connection-related inventory variables you will meet most often.","a":"<p><code>ansible_host</code> (address), <code>ansible_port</code>, <code>ansible_user</code>, <code>ansible_connection</code> (<code>ssh</code>, <code>local</code>, <code>winrm</code>), <code>ansible_ssh_private_key_file</code>, <code>ansible_become</code>, and <code>ansible_python_interpreter</code> (which Python the target should use).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0078","card":"q-ansible-013-multiple-environments-and-the-l-flag","sec":"ansible-s014","q":"Inventory for multiple environments (dev, prod)?","a":"<p>Use a single file with environment groups, or separate files such as <code>dev_hosts</code> and <code>prod_hosts</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[dev_webservers]\ndev-web1\n[prod_webservers]\nprod-web1</code></pre></div>\n<p>Run: <code>ansible-playbook -i hosts -l dev</code>.</p>\n<p>The <code>-l</code> (or <code>--limit</code>) flag limits execution to the hosts matching the pattern, here <code>dev</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0079","card":"q-ansible-013-multiple-environments-and-the-l-flag","sec":"ansible-s014","q":"What is the safest way to keep dev and prod separated in Ansible: one inventory with both, or separate inventories?","a":"<p>Separate inventories, one per environment (for example <code>inventories/dev</code> and <code>inventories/prod</code>, each with its own <code>group_vars</code>). Then you select the environment with <code>-i</code>, and running against the wrong one requires a conscious choice. A single mixed inventory relies on <code>-l</code> every time, and forgetting it touches production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0080","card":"q-ansible-013-multiple-environments-and-the-l-flag","sec":"ansible-s014","q":"What does <code>--limit</code> (<code>-l</code>) do, and how does it relate to the <code>hosts:</code> line of the play?","a":"<p>It restricts a run to the hosts matching a pattern, but only within the hosts the play already targets. The result is the intersection: <code>-l</code> cannot add hosts that <code>hosts:</code> did not select. It is useful for canary runs on one host or rerunning on a failed host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0081","card":"q-ansible-013-multiple-environments-and-the-l-flag","sec":"ansible-s014","q":"How do you do a canary run of a playbook on a single host before rolling out to the group?","a":"<p>Run with <code>-l &lt;hostname&gt;</code> first (and <code>--check</code> if desired), verify, then run without the limit. For a built-in rolling approach, use <code>serial: 1</code> in the play so hosts update one at a time and the play stops if one fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0082","card":"q-ansible-013-multiple-environments-and-the-l-flag","sec":"ansible-s014","q":"How do you list which hosts a playbook and limit would hit, without running anything?","a":"<p><code>ansible-playbook site.yml -l web1 --list-hosts</code> shows the hosts per play. It is a quick check before touching production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0083","card":"q-ansible-014-testing-connectivity-i-flag-and-cloud-in","sec":"ansible-s015","q":"Test connectivity to inventory hosts?","a":"<p><code>ansible all -m ping</code> checks SSH and Python on all hosts. Add <code>-i</code> for a custom inventory.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0084","card":"q-ansible-014-testing-connectivity-i-flag-and-cloud-in","sec":"ansible-s015","q":"What is the <code>-i</code> flag?","a":"<p>It specifies the inventory file or script and overrides the default <code>/etc/ansible/hosts</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible -i my_hosts all -m ping</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0085","card":"q-ansible-014-testing-connectivity-i-flag-and-cloud-in","sec":"ansible-s015","q":"Use a cloud provider for dynamic inventory (AWS)?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>plugin: aws_ec2\nregions:\n  - us-east-1</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible -i aws_ec2.yml all -m ping</code></pre></div>\n<p>It fetches hosts dynamically.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0086","card":"q-ansible-014-testing-connectivity-i-flag-and-cloud-in","sec":"ansible-s015","q":"A host shows <code>UNREACHABLE</code> in <code>ansible all -m ping</code>. What do you check?","a":"<p>UNREACHABLE means the connection failed, not the task. Check: the IP or <code>ansible_host</code>, network or security group rules for port 22, the SSH user and key (<code>ansible_user</code>, key file permissions), host key problems, and that SSH works manually with the same user. Re-run with <code>-vvv</code> to see the SSH command and error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0087","card":"q-ansible-014-testing-connectivity-i-flag-and-cloud-in","sec":"ansible-s015","q":"What is the difference between <code>UNREACHABLE</code> and <code>FAILED</code> in Ansible output?","a":"<p><code>UNREACHABLE</code> means Ansible could not connect to the host (network, SSH, authentication). <code>FAILED</code> means the connection worked but the task itself returned an error (missing Python, wrong parameters, permission denied). They need different fixes, and <code>ignore_errors</code> does not cover unreachable hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0088","card":"q-ansible-014-testing-connectivity-i-flag-and-cloud-in","sec":"ansible-s015","q":"In this dynamic inventory config, what is the role of <code>plugin: aws_ec2</code>, and what must be installed for it to work?","a":"<p><code>plugin</code> selects the inventory plugin that queries the EC2 API. It comes from the <code>amazon.aws</code> collection, and needs <code>boto3</code> and <code>botocore</code> on the control node plus AWS credentials (environment variables, profile or instance role) with permission to describe instances. The config file name must end in <code>aws_ec2.yml</code> for the plugin to accept it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>plugin: aws_ec2\nregions:\n  - us-east-1</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0089","card":"q-ansible-014-testing-connectivity-i-flag-and-cloud-in","sec":"ansible-s015","q":"What does <code>-i</code> do, and what is used if you omit it?","a":"<p>It sets the inventory source (file, directory or plugin config). Without it Ansible uses <code>inventory</code> from <code>ansible.cfg</code>, or <code>/etc/ansible/hosts</code> by default. Setting <code>inventory</code> in the project's <code>ansible.cfg</code> removes the need to pass <code>-i</code> each time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0090","card":"q-ansible-015-yaml-and-basic-playbook-structure","sec":"ansible-s016","q":"What is YAML, and why does Ansible use it?","a":"<p>A human-readable format that relies on indentation. It makes playbooks simple and clear for defining automation.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0091","card":"q-ansible-015-yaml-and-basic-playbook-structure","sec":"ansible-s016","q":"Structure a basic Playbook?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>---\n- name: Install Nginx\n  hosts: webservers\n  tasks:\n    - name: Install\n      apt:\n        name: nginx\n        state: present\n    - name: Start\n      service:\n        name: nginx\n        state: started</code></pre></div>\n<p>It targets hosts and lists tasks.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0092","card":"q-ansible-015-yaml-and-basic-playbook-structure","sec":"ansible-s016","q":"Key Playbook components?","a":"<p><code>hosts</code> (targets), <code>tasks</code> (actions), <code>vars</code> (variables), <code>name</code> (labels), <code>become</code> (sudo), <code>handlers</code> (triggered tasks).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0093","card":"q-ansible-015-yaml-and-basic-playbook-structure","sec":"ansible-s016","q":"Why should you quote <code>'0644'</code> in the <code>mode</code> of a file or copy task?","a":"<p>YAML would read an unquoted <code>0644</code> as a number, possibly as octal or decimal, and the module could set the wrong permissions. Quoting it passes the string, which Ansible interprets as an octal mode. Quote it, or use symbolic modes such as <code>u=rw,g=r,o=r</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0094","card":"q-ansible-015-yaml-and-basic-playbook-structure","sec":"ansible-s016","q":"When must a YAML value starting with <code>{{ ... }}</code> be quoted?","a":"<p>When the value starts with a Jinja2 expression: <code>name: \"{{ item }}\"</code>. Unquoted, YAML treats the opening <code>{</code> as the start of a mapping and parsing fails. If the expression is in the middle of a string, quotes are not required, but quoting is safer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0095","card":"q-ansible-015-yaml-and-basic-playbook-structure","sec":"ansible-s016","q":"What are common YAML mistakes that break a playbook?","a":"<p>Tabs instead of spaces, inconsistent indentation, a missing space after <code>-</code> or <code>:</code>, unquoted values with a colon followed by a space, and unquoted <code>yes</code>/<code>no</code>/<code>on</code>/<code>off</code> that YAML reads as booleans (for example a country code <code>no</code>). Run <code>ansible-playbook --syntax-check</code> and <code>yamllint</code> to catch them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0096","card":"q-ansible-015-yaml-and-basic-playbook-structure","sec":"ansible-s016","q":"How are tasks in a play executed, and what happens when a task fails on one host?","a":"<p>Tasks run top to bottom, each on all targeted hosts (in parallel up to <code>forks</code>) before the next task starts. When a task fails on a host, that host is removed from the rest of the play while the others continue. The recap shows it as failed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0097","card":"q-ansible-015-yaml-and-basic-playbook-structure","sec":"ansible-s016","q":"How do you check a playbook for syntax errors and style problems before running it?","a":"<p><code>ansible-playbook site.yml --syntax-check</code> verifies it parses. <code>ansible-lint</code> flags bad practice (for example missing FQCN, use of <code>shell</code> where a module exists). <code>--check</code> simulates the run against real hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0098","card":"q-ansible-015-yaml-and-basic-playbook-structure","sec":"ansible-s016","q":"What does the <code>---</code> at the top of a YAML playbook mean, and is it required?","a":"<p>It marks the start of a YAML document. It is optional for Ansible but conventional, and linters expect it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0099","card":"q-ansible-016-verbose-output-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s017","q":"Run a Playbook with verbose output?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook playbook.yml -v</code></pre></div>\n<p>Add <code>-vv</code>, <code>-vvv</code>, <code>-vvvv</code> for more detail.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0100","card":"q-ansible-016-verbose-output-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s017","q":"Include variables in a Playbook?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>Inline:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>vars:\n  package_name: nginx</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n  <li>File:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>vars_files:\n  - vars/main.yml</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n  <li>CLI:</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook -e &quot;package_name=nginx&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0101","card":"q-ansible-016-verbose-output-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s017","q":"<code>vars</code> vs <code>vars_files</code>?","a":"<p><code>vars</code> holds inline variables in the playbook (local). <code>vars_files</code> loads an external YAML file (modular).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0102","card":"q-ansible-016-verbose-output-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s017","q":"What extra information do <code>-v</code>, <code>-vv</code>, <code>-vvv</code> and <code>-vvvv</code> show in <code>ansible-playbook</code>?","a":"<p><code>-v</code> shows task results (return values). <code>-vv</code> adds the input and the playbook file locations. <code>-vvv</code> adds connection details such as the SSH commands used, which is the level to use for connection problems. <code>-vvvv</code> adds more connection plugin and script detail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0103","card":"q-ansible-016-verbose-output-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s017","q":"Which wins when the same variable is set in a play's <code>vars</code> and with <code>-e</code> on the command line?","a":"<p>The <code>-e</code> extra vars. They have the highest precedence of all other variable types and cannot be overridden by anything else. That makes them good for one-off overrides but dangerous as a default mechanism.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0104","card":"q-ansible-016-verbose-output-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s017","q":"How do you pass many extra variables to a playbook without a long command line?","a":"<p>Put them in a YAML or JSON file and pass it with <code>-e @vars.yml</code>. You can also pass JSON inline, for example <code>-e '{\"app_version\": \"2.0\"}'</code>, which keeps types (numbers, lists) instead of everything being a string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0105","card":"q-ansible-016-verbose-output-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s017","q":"What is the difference between <code>vars</code>, <code>vars_files</code> and <code>vars_prompt</code> in a play?","a":"<p><code>vars</code> defines values inline. <code>vars_files</code> loads variables from external YAML files, good for keeping secrets (with Vault) or per-environment values out of the playbook. <code>vars_prompt</code> asks the user for a value when the playbook starts, for example a password or a confirmation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0106","card":"q-ansible-016-verbose-output-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s017","q":"In Ansible, why does a value passed with <code>-e \"count=5\"</code> behave as a string in conditions, and how do you avoid that?","a":"<p>The <code>key=value</code> form of <code>-e</code> makes every value a string, so <code>when: count &gt; 3</code> can compare text. Use the JSON or YAML form (<code>-e '{\"count\": 5}'</code> or <code>-e @file.yml</code>) to keep the integer, or cast with <code>| int</code> in the condition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0107","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"Use conditionals (<code>when</code>)?","a":"<p>The task runs only if the condition is true.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install on Ubuntu\n  apt:\n    name: nginx\n    state: present\n  when: ansible_os_family == &quot;Debian&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0108","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"Purpose of <code>become</code>?","a":"<p>It escalates privileges (sudo).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install Nginx\n  apt:\n    name: nginx\n    state: present\n  become: yes</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0109","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"Loop over a list?","a":"<p>The task runs once per item.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install packages\n  apt:\n    name: &quot;{{ item }}&quot;\n    state: present\n  loop:\n    - nginx\n    - git</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0110","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"What is wrong with <code>when: \"{{ ansible_os_family == 'Debian' }}\"</code>?","a":"<p><code>when</code> already takes a Jinja2 expression, so wrapping it in <code>{{ }}</code> is unnecessary and triggers a warning or odd behaviour. Write <code>when: ansible_os_family == \"Debian\"</code>. Braces are for variables used inside strings, not for the <code>when</code> condition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0111","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"How do you combine several conditions in one Ansible <code>when</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>and</code>, <code>or</code> and parentheses in one expression, or give <code>when</code> a list, which means all must be true: <code>when: [cond1, cond2]</code>. Use <code>is defined</code> to guard against missing variables, and <code>| bool</code> for variables that may hold the string <code>\"yes\"</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0112","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"In Ansible, how does <code>when</code> behave on a task that also has <code>loop</code>? Here it is written to skip <code>git</code>.","a":"<p>Adding a <code>when</code> to a looped task evaluates the condition separately for each item, so some items can run and others be skipped. This lets you write <code>when: item != \"git\"</code> to filter inside the loop.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install packages\n  apt:\n    name: &quot;{{ item }}&quot;\n    state: present\n  loop:\n    - nginx\n    - git\n  when: item != &quot;git&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0113","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"Why is passing a list directly to <code>name</code> often better than looping over <code>apt</code>?","a":"<p><code>apt: name: [nginx, git]</code> makes one package transaction for all packages, while <code>loop</code> runs the module once per item, which is slower and creates several dependency resolutions. Use a loop when each item needs different parameters or conditions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0114","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"What is the difference between <code>loop</code> and the older <code>with_items</code>?","a":"<p><code>loop</code> was introduced in Ansible 2.5 as the standard way to iterate, and <code>with_*</code> keywords (such as <code>with_items</code>) are the older lookup-based form. They still work, but new playbooks should use <code>loop</code> with filters and <code>loop_control</code> (for example <code>label</code> to shorten output, or <code>loop_var</code> to rename <code>item</code> in nested loops).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0115","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"At what levels can you set <code>become</code>, and which is preferable?","a":"<p>On the play, on a block, on a task, in <code>ansible.cfg</code> or on the command line with <code>-b</code>. Prefer the narrowest level that works: put <code>become: yes</code> on the tasks that need root so the rest of the play runs unprivileged. Use <code>become_user</code> to switch to a user other than root.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0116","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"Why do tasks fail with \"Missing sudo password\" and what are the fixes?","a":"<p><code>become</code> is on, but sudo wants a password and Ansible has none. Either pass <code>--ask-become-pass</code> (<code>-K</code>), provide <code>ansible_become_password</code> from Vault, or configure passwordless sudo for the automation user in sudoers (the usual way for unattended runs).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0117","card":"q-ansible-017-conditionals-become-and-loops","sec":"ansible-s018","q":"In Ansible, why use a <code>block</code> to group tasks apart from error handling?","a":"<p>A <code>block</code> lets you set <code>when</code>, <code>become</code>, <code>tags</code> or other keywords once for all tasks inside it instead of repeating them on every task. A <code>rescue</code> section is optional.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0118","card":"q-ansible-018-handlers","sec":"ansible-s019","q":"What is the <code>handlers</code> section?","a":"<p>Handlers are tasks triggered by <code>notify</code>; they run only when the notifying task reports a change.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Copy config\n  copy:\n    src: nginx.conf\n    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf\n  notify: Restart Nginx\nhandlers:\n  - name: Restart Nginx\n    service:\n      name: nginx\n      state: restarted</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0119","card":"q-ansible-018-handlers","sec":"ansible-s019","q":"When does a handler run, and how many times does it run if several tasks notify it?","a":"<p>Handlers run at the end of the play (after all tasks in that section), and each runs once, even when many tasks notify it. They run only if at least one notifying task reported <code>changed</code>. This avoids restarting a service several times.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0120","card":"q-ansible-018-handlers","sec":"ansible-s019","q":"In which order do handlers run if tasks notify them in a different order than they are defined?","a":"<p>In the order they are defined in the <code>handlers</code> section, not the order they were notified. If one handler depends on another (for example a daemon reload before a restart), define them in that order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0121","card":"q-ansible-018-handlers","sec":"ansible-s019","q":"How can you run pending handlers in the middle of a play rather than at the end?","a":"<p>Add a task <code>meta: flush_handlers</code> at that point. This is useful when a later task needs the service restarted first, for example restarting a service before a health check.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0122","card":"q-ansible-018-handlers","sec":"ansible-s019","q":"What happens to notified handlers if a later task in the play fails?","a":"<p>By default, handlers do not run for a host that failed, even if earlier tasks changed things, so a service may be left with changed config but not restarted. Use <code>--force-handlers</code> or <code>force_handlers: yes</code> in the play to run notified handlers anyway. Because tasks are idempotent, the next run also fixes it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0123","card":"q-ansible-018-handlers","sec":"ansible-s019","q":"How do you make one notify trigger several handlers?","a":"<p>Use <code>listen</code>: give each handler the same <code>listen: \"restart web stack\"</code> topic and have the task use <code>notify: \"restart web stack\"</code>. A task can also notify a list of handler names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0124","card":"q-ansible-018-handlers","sec":"ansible-s019","q":"Why use a handler to restart a service instead of a normal task after the config copy?","a":"<p>A normal restart task would run on every play run and cause needless downtime, breaking idempotency. A handler restarts only when the config task actually changed something.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0125","card":"q-ansible-019-debugging-register-and-errors","sec":"ansible-s020","q":"Debug Playbook execution?","a":"<p>Use <code>-v</code>, add a debug task, or use <code>--step</code> and <code>--check</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- debug:\n    msg: &quot;Value: {{ my_var }}&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0126","card":"q-ansible-019-debugging-register-and-errors","sec":"ansible-s020","q":"What is <code>register</code>?","a":"<p>It captures task output into a variable.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Check package\n  command: dpkg -l nginx\n  register: result\n- debug:\n    msg: &quot;Installed&quot;\n  when: result.rc == 0</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0127","card":"q-ansible-019-debugging-register-and-errors","sec":"ansible-s020","q":"Handle errors with <code>ignore_errors</code>?","a":"<p>The play continues on failure.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Remove file\n  file:\n    path: /tmp/test.txt\n    state: absent\n  ignore_errors: yes</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0128","card":"q-ansible-019-debugging-register-and-errors","sec":"ansible-s020","q":"What fields does a registered variable usually contain for a <code>command</code> task?","a":"<p><code>rc</code> (return code), <code>stdout</code>, <code>stdout_lines</code>, <code>stderr</code>, <code>stderr_lines</code>, <code>cmd</code>, <code>changed</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>start</code>, <code>end</code> and <code>delta</code>. Print the whole thing with <code>debug: var=result</code> to see what a specific module returns, since fields vary by module.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0129","card":"q-ansible-019-debugging-register-and-errors","sec":"ansible-s020","q":"In this task, how do <code>failed_when</code> and <code>changed_when</code> differ?","a":"<p><code>failed_when</code> decides whether the task counts as failed, for example <code>failed_when: result.rc not in [0, 1]</code>. <code>changed_when</code> decides whether it reports <code>changed</code>, for example <code>changed_when: false</code> for a read-only command so the run stays idempotent in the recap and handlers are not triggered.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Check package\n  command: dpkg -l nginx\n  register: result\n  failed_when: result.rc not in [0, 1]\n  changed_when: false</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0130","card":"q-ansible-019-debugging-register-and-errors","sec":"ansible-s020","q":"When would you use <code>block</code>, <code>rescue</code> and <code>always</code> instead of <code>ignore_errors</code>?","a":"<p>When you want proper error handling: tasks in <code>block</code> run, if any fails the <code>rescue</code> tasks run (rollback, notify), and <code>always</code> tasks run either way (cleanup). <code>ignore_errors</code> just carries on and hides the failure, with no recovery path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0131","card":"q-ansible-019-debugging-register-and-errors","sec":"ansible-s020","q":"What is <code>ignore_errors: yes</code> not able to ignore?","a":"<p>Unreachable hosts and errors in parsing or undefined variables outside task execution are not covered; it only ignores a task returning a failure. For unreachable hosts use <code>ignore_unreachable: yes</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0132","card":"q-ansible-019-debugging-register-and-errors","sec":"ansible-s020","q":"In the sample task, <code>ignore_errors</code> is used with <code>state: absent</code>. Is it needed?","a":"<p>No. <code>state: absent</code> is idempotent: if the file is already gone, the task reports <code>ok</code>, not failure. <code>ignore_errors</code> here would only hide real problems such as permission errors.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Remove file\n  file:\n    path: /tmp/test.txt\n    state: absent\n  ignore_errors: yes</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0133","card":"q-ansible-019-debugging-register-and-errors","sec":"ansible-s020","q":"What do <code>--check</code>, <code>--diff</code>, <code>--step</code> and <code>--start-at-task</code> do when debugging a playbook?","a":"<p><code>--check</code> is a dry run that predicts changes without making them (some modules, such as <code>command</code>, do not support it). <code>--diff</code> shows the before and after of files. <code>--step</code> asks for confirmation before each task. <code>--start-at-task</code> begins at a named task, useful after fixing an error halfway.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0134","card":"q-ansible-019-debugging-register-and-errors","sec":"ansible-s020","q":"What is the difference between <code>debug: msg=</code> and <code>debug: var=</code>?","a":"<p><code>msg</code> prints a string, in which you use <code>{{ }}</code> to interpolate variables. <code>var</code> prints a variable by name, without braces, and shows its full structure (dicts and lists). Use <code>var</code> to inspect registered results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0135","card":"q-ansible-020-include-vs-import","sec":"ansible-s021","q":"Difference: <code>include</code> vs <code>import</code>?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>include</code> (for example <code>include_tasks: setup.yml</code>): runtime, dynamic.</li>\n  <li><code>import</code> (for example <code>import_tasks: setup.yml</code>): parse-time, static.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th><code>include_tasks</code></th><th><code>import_tasks</code></th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Processing time</td><td>At runtime (dynamic)</td><td>At parse-time (static)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Flexibility</td><td>Can use runtime variables and conditions</td><td>Variables must be known at parse-time</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Performance</td><td>Slightly slower (runtime evaluation)</td><td>Faster (pre-loaded during parsing)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Use case</td><td>Conditional or dynamic task inclusion</td><td>Static, predictable task inclusion</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Debugging</td><td>Harder (resolved at runtime)</td><td>Easier (resolved before execution)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0136","card":"q-ansible-020-include-vs-import","sec":"ansible-s021","q":"Why can you not choose the file name from a runtime variable with <code>import_tasks</code>, while <code>include_tasks</code> can?","a":"<p><code>import_tasks</code> is static: Ansible reads and inserts the tasks when it parses the playbook, before any facts or registered values exist. <code>include_tasks</code> is dynamic: it is processed when execution reaches it, so variables from facts, <code>register</code> or <code>set_fact</code> are available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0137","card":"q-ansible-020-include-vs-import","sec":"ansible-s021","q":"How does a <code>when</code> on an <code>include_tasks</code> differ from a <code>when</code> on an <code>import_tasks</code>?","a":"<p>On <code>include_tasks</code>, the condition is evaluated once and decides whether the whole file is included. On <code>import_tasks</code>, the condition is copied to every task in the file and evaluated per task. Evaluating per task matters if an earlier task changes what the condition depends on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0138","card":"q-ansible-020-include-vs-import","sec":"ansible-s021","q":"How do tags behave differently with <code>include_tasks</code> and <code>import_tasks</code>?","a":"<p>Tags on <code>import_tasks</code> are applied to all imported tasks, so <code>--tags</code> can select them. Tags on <code>include_tasks</code> apply only to the include statement itself, and the tasks inside run only if the include is selected. To tag the included tasks you must use <code>apply: { tags: ... }</code> and tag the include too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0139","card":"q-ansible-020-include-vs-import","sec":"ansible-s021","q":"What shows up in <code>--list-tasks</code> and with <code>--start-at-task</code> for include versus import?","a":"<p><code>--list-tasks</code> and <code>--list-tags</code> show tasks from <code>import_tasks</code> but not from <code>include_tasks</code>, because the latter are unknown until runtime. <code>--start-at-task</code> cannot start inside an included file for the same reason.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0140","card":"q-ansible-020-include-vs-import","sec":"ansible-s021","q":"What happened to the plain <code>include</code> keyword in Ansible?","a":"<p>It was deprecated in Ansible 2.4 in favour of <code>include_tasks</code>, <code>import_tasks</code>, <code>include_role</code> and <code>import_playbook</code>, and was removed in ansible-core 2.16. Replace it with the explicit keyword that matches the behaviour you need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0141","card":"q-ansible-020-include-vs-import","sec":"ansible-s021","q":"How do you structure reuse beyond single task files in Ansible?","a":"<p>Use roles with <code>include_role</code> (dynamic) or <code>import_role</code> (static), or <code>import_playbook</code> to compose playbooks. The same static versus dynamic rules apply as for task files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0142","card":"q-ansible-021-include-tasks-in-detail","sec":"ansible-s022","q":"In this play, when is the file name resolved, and what happens if <code>dev_setup.yml</code> does not exist?","a":"<p>The name is resolved at runtime, when this task is reached. If the file does not exist, only that task fails then, after earlier tasks have already run, whereas a missing file with <code>import_tasks</code> fails before anything runs.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- hosts: localhost\n  vars:\n    env: dev\n  tasks:\n    - name: Include tasks based on environment\n      include_tasks: &quot;{{ env }}_setup.yml&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0143","card":"q-ansible-021-include-tasks-in-detail","sec":"ansible-s022","q":"What is the debugging downside of <code>include_tasks</code>, and how do you mitigate it?","a":"<p>The included tasks are not visible to <code>--list-tasks</code>, and errors appear only mid-run. Use <code>-v</code>, give the include a clear <code>name</code>, keep included files small, and use <code>import_tasks</code> when you do not need dynamic behaviour.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0144","card":"q-ansible-021-include-tasks-in-detail","sec":"ansible-s022","q":"How do you pass variables to an included task file?","a":"<p>Add <code>vars:</code> to the include task (variables are available inside the file), for example <code>include_tasks: setup.yml</code> with <code>vars: { app_port: 8080 }</code>. The variables are scoped to the included tasks. <code>include_vars</code> is a different module that loads variable files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0145","card":"q-ansible-021-include-tasks-in-detail","sec":"ansible-s022","q":"How would you run the same task file once per item in a list?","a":"<p>Use <code>include_tasks</code> with <code>loop</code>. Each iteration includes the file, with the current element as <code>item</code> (rename it with <code>loop_control: loop_var</code>). This is a common pattern for creating several similar resources.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0146","card":"q-ansible-022-import-tasks-in-detail","sec":"ansible-s023","q":"Why is <code>import_tasks</code> considered more predictable than <code>include_tasks</code>?","a":"<p>All tasks, tags and conditions are known after parsing, so <code>--list-tasks</code>, <code>--list-tags</code>, <code>--check</code> and <code>--syntax-check</code> see the complete play, and errors in the imported file show up before the run begins. Nothing depends on runtime values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0147","card":"q-ansible-022-import-tasks-in-detail","sec":"ansible-s023","q":"What fails with this task if <code>env</code> is only set by <code>set_fact</code> earlier in the play, and why?","a":"<p>The import is processed during parsing, before <code>set_fact</code> runs, so <code>env</code> is undefined and Ansible reports an error. Use <code>include_tasks</code> for names built from runtime values, or set the variable in play <code>vars</code> or <code>-e</code> (known at parse time).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Import tasks\n  import_tasks: &quot;{{ env }}_setup.yml&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0148","card":"q-ansible-022-import-tasks-in-detail","sec":"ansible-s023","q":"What is the practical advantage of <code>import_tasks</code> when you apply <code>become</code> or <code>tags</code> to it?","a":"<p>The setting is inherited by every task in the imported file, so a single line (<code>tags: setup</code>, <code>become: yes</code>, <code>when:</code>) covers the whole file, with consistent behaviour in <code>--tags</code> runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0149","card":"q-ansible-023-conditional-include","sec":"ansible-s024","q":"In this task, what does the <code>when</code> control?","a":"<p>It decides, per host, whether <code>debian_tasks.yml</code> is included at all. The file is skipped on hosts where <code>ansible_os_family</code> is not <code>Debian</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Include for Debian\n  ansible.builtin.include_tasks: { file: debian_tasks.yml }\n  when: ansible_os_family == &quot;Debian&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0150","card":"q-ansible-023-conditional-include","sec":"ansible-s024","q":"How would you support Debian and RedHat hosts with separate task files without writing two <code>when</code> lines?","a":"<p>Build the file name from a fact: <code>include_tasks: \"{{ ansible_os_family }}.yml\"</code>, with files named <code>Debian.yml</code> and <code>RedHat.yml</code>. Alternatively use <code>with_first_found</code> or <code>first_found</code> lookups to fall back to a default file if no specific one exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0151","card":"q-ansible-024-tags","sec":"ansible-s025","q":"Use tags in a Playbook?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install\n  apt:\n    name: nginx\n    state: present\n  tags:\n    - install</code></pre></div>\n<p>Run:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook --tags &quot;install&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0152","card":"q-ansible-024-tags","sec":"ansible-s025","q":"How do you run everything in a playbook except the tasks tagged <code>slow</code>?","a":"<p>Use <code>--skip-tags slow</code>. Combine with <code>--tags</code> to select and exclude at once. List what is available with <code>--list-tags</code> first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0153","card":"q-ansible-024-tags","sec":"ansible-s025","q":"What are the special tags <code>always</code> and <code>never</code> for?","a":"<p>A task tagged <code>always</code> runs even when <code>--tags</code> selects other tags (unless skipped with <code>--skip-tags always</code>), which suits setup tasks like loading variables. A task tagged <code>never</code> is skipped unless one of its other tags is requested explicitly, which suits risky or rarely used tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0154","card":"q-ansible-024-tags","sec":"ansible-s025","q":"Where can tags be placed, and what is inherited?","a":"<p>On tasks, blocks, plays, roles and <code>import_*</code> statements. A tag on a play, block or role applies to everything inside it. Tags on <code>include_*</code> statements apply only to the include itself unless you use <code>apply</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0155","card":"q-ansible-024-tags","sec":"ansible-s025","q":"What is a good use of tags in a large playbook, and what is a trap?","a":"<p>Tags let you run a slice, such as <code>--tags config</code> after changing only a template, which saves time. The trap is that a tagged run skips untagged tasks, including setup that later tasks depend on (for example a fact or registered variable). Tag prerequisites <code>always</code>, or do not rely on tags for correctness.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0156","card":"q-ansible-025-common-modules-and-command-vs-shell","sec":"ansible-s026","q":"Commonly used modules?","a":"<p><code>command</code>, <code>shell</code>, <code>file</code>, <code>copy</code>, <code>yum</code>/<code>apt</code>, <code>service</code>, <code>template</code>, <code>user</code>, <code>setup</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0157","card":"q-ansible-025-common-modules-and-command-vs-shell","sec":"ansible-s026","q":"Use the <code>command</code> module? Limitations?","a":"<p>It has no shell features (pipes, redirects); use <code>shell</code> for those.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Run command\n  command: uptime</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0158","card":"q-ansible-025-common-modules-and-command-vs-shell","sec":"ansible-s026","q":"<code>shell</code> vs <code>command</code>?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><code>command</code>: direct, no shell (for example <code>uptime</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>shell</code>: runs through a shell, supports pipes (for example <code>ls &gt; file</code>).</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0159","card":"q-ansible-025-common-modules-and-command-vs-shell","sec":"ansible-s026","q":"A <code>shell</code> task contains <code>cat /etc/passwd | grep root</code>. Would <code>command</code> work, and what is the preferred option?","a":"<p><code>command</code> would not work, because it does not use a shell, so <code>|</code> is passed as an argument. <code>shell</code> works, but the preferred option is to use a module (for example <code>getent</code>, <code>lineinfile</code>, <code>slurp</code>) or pass the output through Ansible filters, because modules are idempotent and give structured results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0160","card":"q-ansible-025-common-modules-and-command-vs-shell","sec":"ansible-s026","q":"Why is <code>shell</code> riskier than <code>command</code>?","a":"<p><code>shell</code> passes the string through <code>/bin/sh</code>, so special characters and variables in it are interpreted. If the string includes values from variables, that risks shell injection and quoting bugs. <code>command</code> runs the program directly with arguments and is safer. Use the <code>quote</code> filter if you must put variables in <code>shell</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0161","card":"q-ansible-025-common-modules-and-command-vs-shell","sec":"ansible-s026","q":"How do you make a <code>command</code> task idempotent?","a":"<p>Use <code>creates</code> or <code>removes</code> (skip if a file exists or is missing), or <code>changed_when</code> and <code>when</code> conditions based on a prior check. Example: <code>command: /opt/app/install.sh</code> with <code>args: creates: /opt/app/.installed</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0162","card":"q-ansible-025-common-modules-and-command-vs-shell","sec":"ansible-s026","q":"When would you use the <code>raw</code> module instead of <code>command</code>?","a":"<p>When the target has no Python, since <code>raw</code> sends the command directly through SSH with no module wrapper. It is mostly used to install Python on a new host or to manage network devices. It cannot be idempotent and returns less structured output.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0163","card":"q-ansible-025-common-modules-and-command-vs-shell","sec":"ansible-s026","q":"Why prefer a dedicated module over <code>command</code> for tasks like installing packages or creating users?","a":"<p>A module knows the desired state and checks it, so it is idempotent, supports <code>--check</code> mode and diffs, handles errors with clear messages, and works across operating systems. <code>command</code> is opaque: it always reports changed and cannot tell what the outcome means.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0164","card":"q-ansible-025-common-modules-and-command-vs-shell","sec":"ansible-s026","q":"What is the <code>script</code> module and how does it differ from <code>command</code>?","a":"<p><code>script</code> copies a local script from the control node to the target and runs it there, so you do not need the script to exist on the target beforehand. <code>command</code> can only run something already on the target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0165","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"Use the <code>file</code> module?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- file:\n    path: /opt/app\n    state: directory\n    mode: &#x27;0755&#x27;\n- file:\n    path: /tmp/test.txt\n    state: absent</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0166","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"Install a package with <code>yum</code>/<code>apt</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- yum:\n    name: httpd\n    state: present\n- apt:\n    name: nginx\n    state: present</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0167","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"What is the <code>copy</code> module for?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- copy:\n    src: config.conf\n    dest: /etc/app/config.conf\n    mode: &#x27;0644&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0168","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"Manage services with <code>service</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- service:\n    name: nginx\n    state: started\n    enabled: yes</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0169","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"What values can <code>state</code> take in the <code>file</code> module and what does each do?","a":"<p><code>file</code> (default: check it exists), <code>directory</code> (create it, including parents), <code>touch</code> (create an empty file or update the timestamp), <code>absent</code> (remove file or directory), <code>link</code> (symlink) and <code>hard</code> (hard link). Setting <code>recurse: yes</code> applies ownership and mode to directory contents.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0170","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"Why does an <code>apt</code> task often need <code>update_cache: yes</code>, and how do you avoid doing it on every run?","a":"<p>A fresh host has a stale package index, so installs can fail with \"package not found\". <code>update_cache: yes</code> runs <code>apt-get update</code>, but doing it on every task wastes time. Add <code>cache_valid_time: 3600</code> so it refreshes only if the cache is older than an hour.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0171","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"What is the difference between <code>state: present</code> and <code>state: latest</code> for a package?","a":"<p><code>present</code> installs it if absent and leaves any installed version alone, which is idempotent and keeps versions stable. <code>latest</code> upgrades to the newest available version on each run, which can cause unplanned upgrades. In production pin a version or use <code>present</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0172","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"How do you write a playbook that installs a package on both Debian and RedHat hosts with one task?","a":"<p>Use the generic <code>ansible.builtin.package</code> module, which delegates to apt, dnf or yum depending on the host. Package names often differ between families (<code>apache2</code> versus <code>httpd</code>), so you may need a variable per OS family.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0173","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"In the <code>service</code> module, what is the difference between <code>state: started</code> and <code>state: restarted</code>, and what does <code>enabled: yes</code> do?","a":"<p><code>started</code> makes sure the service is running and does nothing if it is (idempotent). <code>restarted</code> always restarts it, so it reports <code>changed</code> every run, and is best placed in a handler. <code>enabled: yes</code> configures start at boot, which is independent of whether it is running now.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0174","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"When would you use the <code>systemd</code> module rather than <code>service</code>?","a":"<p><code>service</code> is a generic wrapper across init systems. <code>systemd</code> exposes systemd features such as <code>daemon_reload: yes</code> (after changing a unit file), <code>masked</code>, and unit scopes. Use <code>systemd</code> on modern Linux when you edit unit files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0175","card":"q-ansible-026-file-packages-copy-and-service","sec":"ansible-s027","q":"What are useful <code>copy</code> options besides <code>src</code>, <code>dest</code> and <code>mode</code>?","a":"<p><code>content</code> writes a string directly into the file with no source file, <code>backup: yes</code> keeps a timestamped copy of the old file, <code>validate</code> checks the file with a command before putting it in place, and <code>remote_src: yes</code> copies from one path on the target to another instead of from the control node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0176","card":"q-ansible-027-template-vs-copy","sec":"ansible-s028","q":"How is <code>template</code> different from <code>copy</code>?","a":"<p><code>template</code> renders a Jinja2 file with variables, loops and conditionals; <code>copy</code> transfers a static file as-is.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- template:\n    src: httpd.conf.j2\n    dest: /etc/httpd/httpd.conf</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th><code>copy</code></th><th><code>template</code></th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>File content</td><td>Static, copied as-is</td><td>Dynamic, processed as Jinja2</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Use case</td><td>Scripts, binaries, static config</td><td>Configs that vary by host or environment</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Source file</td><td>Any file</td><td>Jinja2 template (<code>.j2</code> by convention)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Flexibility</td><td>No dynamic content</td><td>Variable substitution, loops, conditionals</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p>Variables can be used in the <code>src</code> and <code>dest</code> paths of both modules, but only <code>template</code> processes variables inside the file content.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0177","card":"q-ansible-027-template-vs-copy","sec":"ansible-s028","q":"A config file differs per environment only by a hostname and a port. Which module do you use and why?","a":"<p><code>template</code>. The file becomes a Jinja2 template with <code>{{ var }}</code> placeholders, so one source file produces the correct output per host or environment from variables. With <code>copy</code> you would keep a separate static file per environment, which drifts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0178","card":"q-ansible-027-template-vs-copy","sec":"ansible-s028","q":"Where does Ansible look for the <code>src</code> of <code>copy</code> and <code>template</code> when the task is inside a role?","a":"<p><code>copy</code> looks in the role's <code>files/</code> directory and <code>template</code> looks in <code>templates/</code>, so you can write just the file name. Outside roles, paths are relative to the playbook directory (and a <code>files</code> or <code>templates</code> subdirectory next to it).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0179","card":"q-ansible-027-template-vs-copy","sec":"ansible-s028","q":"Where is a Jinja2 template rendered: on the control node or on the target?","a":"<p>On the control node. The finished text is then sent to the target. That means the template can use variables and facts Ansible holds, but cannot call commands or read files on the target at render time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0180","card":"q-ansible-027-template-vs-copy","sec":"ansible-s028","q":"What is <code>{{ ansible_managed }}</code> used for in a template?","a":"<p>It is a variable holding a comment text (set in <code>ansible.cfg</code>) that you place at the top of a rendered file to mark it as managed by Ansible, so people do not hand-edit it and lose their change on the next run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0181","card":"q-ansible-027-template-vs-copy","sec":"ansible-s028","q":"Why use <code>validate</code> on a <code>template</code> task for a config like nginx or sudoers?","a":"<p>A broken config can take a service down or lock out sudo. <code>validate</code> runs a command (for example <code>nginx -t -c %s</code>) on a temp copy and only installs the file if the check passes, so a bad template fails the task instead of breaking production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0182","card":"q-ansible-027-template-vs-copy","sec":"ansible-s028","q":"What control structures can a Jinja2 template use that <code>copy</code> cannot?","a":"<p>Loops (<code>{% for host in groups['web'] %}</code>), conditionals (<code>{% if %}</code>), filters (<code>| default('x')</code>) and access to facts and <code>hostvars</code>. A typical use is building an upstream list or <code>/etc/hosts</code> from inventory groups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0183","card":"q-ansible-028-copy-and-template-examples","sec":"ansible-s029","q":"In this <code>copy</code> task, what do <code>owner</code>, <code>group</code> and <code>mode</code> do, and are they applied if the content has not changed?","a":"<p>They set the ownership and permissions of the destination. Ansible compares checksums for the content and also checks these attributes, so if only the mode differs, it fixes the mode and reports <code>changed</code>. If everything matches, the task reports <code>ok</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Copy config.txt to remote host\n  ansible.builtin.copy:\n    src: ./files/config.txt\n    dest: /etc/app/config.txt\n    owner: appuser\n    group: appgroup\n    mode: &#x27;0644&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0184","card":"q-ansible-028-copy-and-template-examples","sec":"ansible-s029","q":"In Ansible, why must the user and group named in <code>owner:</code> and <code>group:</code> of a <code>copy</code> task exist before the task runs?","a":"<p><code>copy</code> sets ownership by name, and fails if the user or group does not exist on the target. Create them earlier in the play with the <code>user</code> and <code>group</code> modules, which is a case where task order matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0185","card":"q-ansible-028-copy-and-template-examples","sec":"ansible-s029","q":"In this <code>template</code> task, where do the values for the placeholders in <code>config.j2</code> come from?","a":"<p>From the variables visible to the host at runtime: play <code>vars</code> as here, but also inventory, <code>group_vars</code>, <code>host_vars</code>, facts, registered variables and extra vars, following variable precedence. The template itself holds no values, only placeholders.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Generate config.txt from template\n  hosts: dev-web1\n  vars:\n    server_name: &quot;webserver_dev&quot;\n    app_port: 8080\n  tasks:\n    - name: Generate config.txt from template\n      ansible.builtin.template:\n        src: ./templates/config.j2\n        dest: /etc/app/config.txt</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0186","card":"q-ansible-028-copy-and-template-examples","sec":"ansible-s029","q":"What happens on a second run of a <code>template</code> task if the variables have not changed?","a":"<p>Ansible renders the template again, compares the result with the file on the host, finds it identical and reports <code>ok</code>, so no handler is triggered. A change in any variable used in the template changes the output and so reports <code>changed</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0187","card":"q-ansible-029-lineinfile-and-user","sec":"ansible-s030","q":"What is <code>lineinfile</code> for?","a":"<p>It edits a single line, useful for config tweaks.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- lineinfile:\n    path: /etc/ssh/sshd_config\n    regexp: &#x27;^PermitRootLogin&#x27;\n    line: &#x27;PermitRootLogin no&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0188","card":"q-ansible-029-lineinfile-and-user","sec":"ansible-s030","q":"Manage users with <code>user</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- user:\n    name: appuser\n    password: &quot;{{ &#x27;pass&#x27; | password_hash(&#x27;sha512&#x27;) }}&quot;\n    state: present</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0189","card":"q-ansible-029-lineinfile-and-user","sec":"ansible-s030","q":"What does <code>lineinfile</code> do when the <code>regexp</code> matches no line, and when it matches several?","a":"<p>If nothing matches, the <code>line</code> is appended to the end of the file (or placed by <code>insertafter</code> or <code>insertbefore</code>). If several lines match, only the last match is replaced. It is idempotent: if the line is already correct, nothing changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0190","card":"q-ansible-029-lineinfile-and-user","sec":"ansible-s030","q":"When should you use <code>blockinfile</code>, <code>replace</code> or <code>template</code> instead of <code>lineinfile</code>?","a":"<p><code>blockinfile</code> manages a multi-line block with marker comments. <code>replace</code> changes all matches of a regex in a file. <code>template</code> is better when you own the whole file, which is the most predictable choice. <code>lineinfile</code> is for a single line in a file that something else owns.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0191","card":"q-ansible-029-lineinfile-and-user","sec":"ansible-s030","q":"How do you edit <code>sshd_config</code> with <code>lineinfile</code> safely?","a":"<p>Add <code>validate: '/usr/sbin/sshd -t -f %s'</code> so the new file is checked before replacing the old one, and notify a handler to reload <code>sshd</code>. <code>backup: yes</code> keeps the original. A bad sshd config can lock you out of the server.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0192","card":"q-ansible-029-lineinfile-and-user","sec":"ansible-s030","q":"Why does the <code>user</code> module task with <code>password_hash('sha512')</code> report <code>changed</code> on every run?","a":"<p><code>password_hash</code> creates a random salt each time, so the hash differs on every run and the module sees a different value. Provide a fixed salt (<code>password_hash('sha512', 'mysalt')</code>) or set <code>update_password: on_create</code> so the password is only set at creation.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- user:\n    name: appuser\n    password: &quot;{{ &#x27;pass&#x27; | password_hash(&#x27;sha512&#x27;) }}&quot;\n    state: present</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0193","card":"q-ansible-029-lineinfile-and-user","sec":"ansible-s030","q":"Why should a plaintext password in a <code>user</code> task be treated carefully?","a":"<p>It sits in the playbook and in logs. Store it in Ansible Vault, pass it in with a variable, and add <code>no_log: true</code> to the task so the value is not printed. The <code>user</code> module expects an already hashed password, not plaintext.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0194","card":"q-ansible-029-lineinfile-and-user","sec":"ansible-s030","q":"How do you add a user to a supplementary group without removing other group memberships?","a":"<p>Use <code>groups: docker</code> together with <code>append: yes</code>. Without <code>append</code>, the <code>groups</code> list replaces all supplementary groups the user currently has.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0195","card":"q-ansible-030-fetch","sec":"ansible-s031","q":"Fetch files with <code>fetch</code>?","a":"<p><code>fetch</code> pulls files from the remote host to the control node, the opposite of <code>copy</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- fetch:\n    src: /var/log/app.log\n    dest: /local/logs/{{ inventory_hostname }}.log\n    flat: yes</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>src</code> is the file on the remote host.</li>\n  <li><code>dest</code> is the path on the control node; <code>{{ inventory_hostname }}</code> keeps files from different hosts from overwriting each other (<code>/local/logs/dev-web1.log</code>, <code>/local/logs/prod-web1.log</code>).</li>\n  <li><code>flat: yes</code> saves directly to <code>dest</code>. Without it, the remote path is mirrored (for example <code>/local/logs/dev-web1/var/log/app.log</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Typical uses are collecting logs for debugging or auditing, backing up configuration files, and centralizing files from many hosts.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Fetch logs from remote hosts\n  hosts: all\n  tasks:\n    - name: Fetch app.log from remote host\n      ansible.builtin.fetch:\n        src: /var/log/app.log\n        dest: /local/logs/{{ inventory_hostname }}.log\n        flat: yes</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0196","card":"q-ansible-030-fetch","sec":"ansible-s031","q":"What is the difference between <code>fetch</code> and <code>copy</code>, and which direction does each move files?","a":"<p><code>copy</code> sends a file from the control node to the managed node. <code>fetch</code> pulls a file from the managed node to the control node. <code>fetch</code> works on single files only, so for whole directories use <code>synchronize</code> with <code>mode: pull</code> or archive first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0197","card":"q-ansible-030-fetch","sec":"ansible-s031","q":"In this <code>fetch</code> task, what happens to the destination path when <code>flat</code> is left out?","a":"<p>Without <code>flat: yes</code>, <code>dest</code> is treated as a base directory and Ansible builds <code>dest/&lt;hostname&gt;/&lt;full remote path&gt;</code>, so you get nested directories per host. With <code>flat: yes</code> the file is written exactly at <code>dest</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- fetch:\n    src: /var/log/app.log\n    dest: /local/logs/{{ inventory_hostname }}.log\n    flat: yes</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0198","card":"q-ansible-030-fetch","sec":"ansible-s031","q":"Why include <code>{{ inventory_hostname }}</code> in the <code>dest</code> when fetching from many hosts with <code>flat: yes</code>?","a":"<p>With <code>flat: yes</code>, every host writes to the same <code>dest</code>, so each fetch would overwrite the previous host's file. Including the host name in the path gives each its own file. A <code>dest</code> ending with <code>/</code> with <code>flat</code> would instead keep the base name, which can also collide.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0199","card":"q-ansible-030-fetch","sec":"ansible-s031","q":"When would you use <code>fetch</code> in real operations?","a":"<p>Collecting logs or config for troubleshooting, pulling created certificates or reports back to the control node, backing up configuration before a change, and gathering data from many hosts for audit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0200","card":"q-ansible-030-fetch","sec":"ansible-s031","q":"Why is <code>slurp</code> sometimes a better choice than <code>fetch</code>?","a":"<p><code>slurp</code> reads a remote file into a variable (base64 encoded) so you can use its content in later tasks without writing a local file. <code>fetch</code> saves a copy on disk, which is better for archives and large files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0201","card":"q-ansible-031-setup-and-custom-modules","sec":"ansible-s032","q":"What is the <code>setup</code> module?","a":"<p>It gathers facts. It runs automatically unless <code>gather_facts: no</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- setup:</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0202","card":"q-ansible-031-setup-and-custom-modules","sec":"ansible-s032","q":"Write a custom module?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from ansible.module_utils.basic import AnsibleModule\ndef main():\n    module = AnsibleModule(argument_spec=dict(name=dict(type=&#x27;str&#x27;, required=True)))\n    module.exit_json(changed=True, msg=f&quot;Hello, {module.params[&#x27;name&#x27;]}&quot;)\nif __name__ == &#x27;__main__&#x27;:\n    main()</code></pre></div>\n<p>Use it in a playbook:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- my_custom_module:\n    name: &quot;world&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0203","card":"q-ansible-031-setup-and-custom-modules","sec":"ansible-s032","q":"How do you inspect the facts of a host from the command line, and filter them?","a":"<p><code>ansible web1 -m setup</code> shows all facts. Use <code>-a \"filter=ansible_distribution*\"</code> to match names, for example for OS details or memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0204","card":"q-ansible-031-setup-and-custom-modules","sec":"ansible-s032","q":"What is the difference between <code>setup</code> and <code>set_fact</code>?","a":"<p><code>setup</code> collects facts about the host (hardware, OS, network). <code>set_fact</code> creates your own variable during the play, from expressions or registered results, and it stays for the host through the run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0205","card":"q-ansible-031-setup-and-custom-modules","sec":"ansible-s032","q":"What does <code>module.exit_json</code> do in a custom Ansible module, and what is the equivalent for failure?","a":"<p><code>exit_json</code> returns the result to Ansible as JSON and ends the module, with keys like <code>changed</code> and any data to register. <code>fail_json(msg=...)</code> reports failure with a message and stops the task. Everything printed this way is returned to the playbook.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from ansible.module_utils.basic import AnsibleModule\ndef main():\n    module = AnsibleModule(argument_spec=dict(name=dict(type=&#x27;str&#x27;, required=True)))\n    module.exit_json(changed=True, msg=f&quot;Hello, {module.params[&#x27;name&#x27;]}&quot;)\nif __name__ == &#x27;__main__&#x27;:\n    main()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0206","card":"q-ansible-031-setup-and-custom-modules","sec":"ansible-s032","q":"In an Ansible custom module built on <code>AnsibleModule</code>, what is <code>argument_spec</code> for?","a":"<p>It declares the accepted parameters with their types, defaults and whether they are <code>required</code>. <code>AnsibleModule</code> validates the task's arguments against it before your code runs, so wrong types or missing parameters fail with a clear message.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0207","card":"q-ansible-031-setup-and-custom-modules","sec":"ansible-s032","q":"What is wrong with a custom module that always returns <code>changed=True</code>?","a":"<p>It breaks idempotency: every run reports a change, handlers fire each time and the recap never settles. A module should compare current state with the desired state and return <code>changed=True</code> only when it modifies something. It should also support check mode via <code>supports_check_mode=True</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0208","card":"q-ansible-031-setup-and-custom-modules","sec":"ansible-s032","q":"Where do you put a custom module so Ansible finds it, and can it be written in a language other than Python?","a":"<p>In a <code>library/</code> directory next to the playbook (or inside a role's <code>library/</code>), or a path in <code>ANSIBLE_LIBRARY</code> or <code>library</code> in <code>ansible.cfg</code>. Modules can be written in any language that reads its arguments file and prints JSON, but Python with <code>AnsibleModule</code> is the standard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0209","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"What are Ansible Facts?","a":"<p>Host data (OS, IP) collected automatically by the <code>setup</code> module at the start of a play.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0210","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"Access facts in a Playbook?","a":"<p>Use <code>ansible_facts['key']</code> or dot notation.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- debug:\n    msg: &quot;Host: {{ ansible_hostname }}&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0211","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"Facts vs variables?","a":"<p>Facts are dynamic and come from the hosts. Variables are static and user-defined.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0212","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"In Ansible, how are facts collected, and when does that happen during a playbook run?","a":"<p>The <code>setup</code> module runs automatically as an implicit first task of each play (called \"Gathering Facts\"). It connects to every host in the play and returns data such as OS family, distribution, IP addresses, memory and CPU. The results become variables for the rest of the play. You can switch it off with <code>gather_facts: false</code> on the play.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0213","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"In Ansible, a play sets <code>gather_facts: false</code> and a later task uses <code>when: ansible_os_family == \"Debian\"</code>. What happens and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The task fails with an undefined variable error, because no fact was collected. Fix it by turning fact gathering back on, by running the <code>ansible.builtin.setup</code> module explicitly (optionally with <code>gather_subset</code>) before the task, or by using a variable you define yourself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0214","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"In Ansible, what is the difference between <code>ansible_facts['distribution']</code> and <code>ansible_distribution</code>?","a":"<p>Both give the same value. <code>ansible_facts['distribution']</code> is the namespaced form. <code>ansible_distribution</code> is the older form where each fact is also injected as a top-level variable with an <code>ansible_</code> prefix. The namespaced form is preferred because the injected form can be turned off (<code>inject_facts_as_vars = False</code>) and can clash with other variable names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0215","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"In Ansible, what is the difference between the fact <code>ansible_hostname</code> and the magic variable <code>inventory_hostname</code>?","a":"<p><code>inventory_hostname</code> is the name of the host as written in the inventory. It is always available and needs no fact gathering. <code>ansible_hostname</code> is the hostname the machine reports about itself and exists only after facts are gathered. They can differ, for example when the inventory uses an alias or an IP address.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- debug:\n    msg: &quot;Host: {{ ansible_hostname }}&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0216","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"How would you give Ansible your own host-specific facts, such as the role of a server, without editing playbooks?","a":"<p>Put a file ending in <code>.fact</code> in <code>/etc/ansible/facts.d/</code> on the managed node. It can be INI or JSON, or an executable that prints JSON. The <code>setup</code> module reads it and exposes the values under <code>ansible_local</code>. This lets teams or other tools tag machines locally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0217","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"In Ansible, how does a task on one host read a fact of a different host?","a":"<p>Through the <code>hostvars</code> magic variable, for example <code>hostvars['db1']['ansible_facts']['default_ipv4']['address']</code>. The facts of that host must have been gathered earlier in the same run (or come from the fact cache), otherwise the lookup is undefined.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0218","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"How do you decide between a fact and a variable you define yourself in Ansible?","a":"<p>Use a fact when the value is a property of the machine that you do not want to maintain (OS, IPs, memory), because it is discovered and always current. Use a variable for a decision or a setting you control (port, version, environment name). Do not hardcode something in a variable that a fact can tell you reliably.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0219","card":"q-ansible-032-facts-and-variables","sec":"ansible-s033","q":"In Ansible, what is the cost of gathering facts, and how do you reduce it without losing all facts?","a":"<p>Fact gathering adds a round trip and some work on every host at the start of every play, which adds up on large fleets. Reduce it with <code>gather_subset</code> (for example <code>min</code>, or exclude <code>hardware</code>), or enable fact caching so facts are reused across runs. If a play needs no facts at all, set <code>gather_facts: false</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0220","card":"q-ansible-033-defining-variables-and-precedence","sec":"ansible-s034","q":"Define variables at different levels?","a":"<ul>\n  <li>Inventory: <code>web1 ansible_user=admin</code></li>\n  <li>Playbook: <code>vars:</code> with <code>app: nginx</code></li>\n  <li>Role: <code>defaults/main.yml</code></li>\n  <li>CLI: <code>-e \"app=nginx\"</code></li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0221","card":"q-ansible-033-defining-variables-and-precedence","sec":"ansible-s034","q":"Variable precedence?","a":"<p>Low to high: role defaults, inventory, playbook vars, CLI <code>-e</code>. The higher one wins.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0222","card":"q-ansible-033-defining-variables-and-precedence","sec":"ansible-s034","q":"Override a variable during execution?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook playbook.yml -e &quot;app_version=2.0&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0223","card":"q-ansible-033-defining-variables-and-precedence","sec":"ansible-s034","q":"In Ansible, where would you put a value that differs per environment, such as a port, and why there?","a":"<p>In <code>group_vars</code> (or <code>host_vars</code>) of the inventory for that environment. The playbook and role stay identical, and each environment's inventory supplies its own value. Putting it in the playbook would force one playbook per environment, and putting it only on the command line makes runs hard to reproduce.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0224","card":"q-ansible-033-defining-variables-and-precedence","sec":"ansible-s034","q":"In Ansible, a role has <code>port: 80</code> in <code>defaults/main.yml</code> and also in <code>vars/main.yml</code>. Which one is used and why?","a":"<p>The one in <code>vars/main.yml</code>. Role defaults have the lowest precedence of all variable locations and are meant to be overridden. Role <code>vars</code> have a high precedence and are meant for internal constants that callers should not override. Put overridable settings in <code>defaults</code>, fixed values in <code>vars</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0225","card":"q-ansible-033-defining-variables-and-precedence","sec":"ansible-s034","q":"Rank these Ansible variable locations from lowest to highest precedence: role defaults, inventory <code>group_vars</code>, play <code>vars</code>, <code>set_fact</code>, <code>-e</code> on the command line.","a":"<p>Role defaults, inventory <code>group_vars</code>, play <code>vars</code>, <code>set_fact</code>, then <code>-e</code>. Extra vars passed with <code>-e</code> always win. The full precedence list has about 22 levels, so keep the number of places where a variable is defined small.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0226","card":"q-ansible-033-defining-variables-and-precedence","sec":"ansible-s034","q":"How do you find out which value an Ansible variable has for a specific host?","a":"<p>Run <code>ansible -i inventory host1 -m debug -a \"var=app_version\"</code> for a quick look, or add a <code>debug</code> task with <code>var:</code> in the playbook. For inventory-level values, <code>ansible-inventory --host host1</code> shows the merged host and group variables. Note that variables set at runtime (<code>set_fact</code>, registered) only show up inside a run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0227","card":"q-ansible-033-defining-variables-and-precedence","sec":"ansible-s034","q":"Why is overriding with <code>-e</code> on the command line convenient but risky in Ansible?","a":"<p>It beats every other location, so it is the quick way to override a value for one run, for example a one-off version. The risk is that the override is invisible in the repository and in the playbook, so the same run cannot be reproduced later unless the exact command is recorded. Use it for exceptions, not for normal configuration.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook playbook.yml -e &quot;app_version=2.0&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0228","card":"q-ansible-033-defining-variables-and-precedence","sec":"ansible-s034","q":"Why prefix role variables with the role name in Ansible, for example <code>nginx_port</code> instead of <code>port</code>?","a":"<p>Variables from all roles share one namespace for a play. A generic name like <code>port</code> can silently collide with a variable from another role or from the inventory and change behaviour. A prefix avoids the collision and shows which role a variable belongs to.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0229","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"Jinja2 filters with variables?","a":"<p>Examples: <code>{{ var | upper }}</code>, <code>{{ list | join(',') }}</code>, <code>{{ var | default('n/a') }}</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0230","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"Use <code>set_fact</code> for custom facts?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- set_fact:\n    custom_ver: &quot;1.5&quot;\n- debug:\n    msg: &quot;{{ custom_ver }}&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0231","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"What is <code>group_vars</code>?","a":"<p>A directory for group variables, one file per group:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># group_vars/webservers.yml\nhttp_port: 80</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0232","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"In Ansible, what does the <code>default</code> filter do, and why can <code>{{ name | default('n/a') }}</code> still print an empty string?","a":"<p><code>default</code> replaces a variable only when it is undefined. If <code>name</code> is defined but empty, the empty string is kept. To also replace empty or false values, pass <code>true</code> as the second argument: <code>{{ name | default('n/a', true) }}</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0233","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"In Ansible, what does <code>default(omit)</code> do on a module parameter?","a":"<p>It removes the parameter from the task when the variable is undefined, so the module uses its own default. For example <code>mode: \"{{ file_mode | default(omit) }}\"</code> leaves the mode untouched when <code>file_mode</code> is not set. Without <code>omit</code>, an undefined variable would raise an error or send an empty value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0234","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"In Ansible, how does the <code>join</code> filter work, and when is it handy?","a":"<p><code>join</code> turns a list into a string with the given separator, so <code>['a','b','c'] | join(',')</code> gives <code>a,b,c</code>. It is handy for building comma-separated values in config files, command arguments or messages.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>msg: &quot;{{ list | join(&#x27;,&#x27;) }}&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0235","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"In Ansible, which filter makes a variable required so that a missing value fails the play early?","a":"<p><code>mandatory</code>, for example <code>{{ db_host | mandatory }}</code>. Without it, an undefined value fails only when it is used, or in some contexts is silently replaced by an empty string. <code>mandatory</code> gives a clear error at the point of use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0236","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"In Ansible, what is the lifetime of a variable created with <code>set_fact</code>?","a":"<p>It is stored per host and lasts for the rest of the run, including later plays. It is not saved on disk unless fact caching is enabled and the fact is marked <code>cacheable</code>. It has a high precedence, higher than play <code>vars</code> and inventory variables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0237","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"In Ansible, when would you use <code>set_fact</code> and when <code>register</code> to create a new value?","a":"<p>Use <code>register</code> to capture the result of a task (return code, stdout, changed). Use <code>set_fact</code> to compute a new value from existing variables, often from a registered result, for example to extract one field or combine two strings, so later tasks can use a simple name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0238","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"In Ansible, where can <code>group_vars</code> live, and how do the locations interact?","a":"<p>Ansible loads a <code>group_vars</code> directory next to the inventory and also next to the playbook. A group can be a file (<code>webservers.yml</code>) or a directory of files. <code>group_vars/all</code> applies to every host. The inventory location is loaded first, the playbook location after it, so the playbook one wins on a conflict.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># group_vars/webservers.yml\nhttp_port: 80</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0239","card":"q-ansible-034-jinja2-filters-set-fact-and-group-vars","sec":"ansible-s035","q":"Why use <code>group_vars</code> and <code>host_vars</code> files instead of defining variables inline in the inventory?","a":"<p>Inline variables make the inventory long, hard to review and unable to hold structured data like lists and dictionaries cleanly. Separate files keep the host list readable, hold full YAML, and allow secrets to be split into an encrypted file per group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0240","card":"q-ansible-035-encrypting-variables-with-vault","sec":"ansible-s036","q":"What is Ansible Vault?","a":"<p>It encrypts secrets (passwords, API keys) in files so they can be stored safely, even in version control.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0241","card":"q-ansible-035-encrypting-variables-with-vault","sec":"ansible-s036","q":"Encrypt variables with Vault?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-vault create secrets.yml</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>db_pass: secret</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook --ask-vault-pass</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>ansible-vault create</code> prompts for a vault password and opens an editor. The saved file is AES256-encrypted on disk (it starts with <code>$ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.1;AES256</code>).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0242","card":"q-ansible-035-encrypting-variables-with-vault","sec":"ansible-s036","q":"What problem does Ansible Vault solve, and what does it not solve?","a":"<p>It lets you keep secrets (passwords, API keys, certificates) encrypted at rest in files that can sit in version control. It does not hide a secret at runtime. A decrypted value can still show up in task output, logs, <code>-v</code> output or on the managed node, so tasks that handle secrets need <code>no_log: true</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0243","card":"q-ansible-035-encrypting-variables-with-vault","sec":"ansible-s036","q":"What is the difference between <code>ansible-vault create</code> and <code>ansible-vault encrypt</code>?","a":"<p><code>create</code> makes a new file, asks for a vault password and opens an editor, so the file is never stored in plaintext. <code>encrypt</code> takes an existing plaintext file and encrypts it in place. Use <code>encrypt</code> only after making sure the plaintext is not committed anywhere.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0244","card":"q-ansible-035-encrypting-variables-with-vault","sec":"ansible-s036","q":"What does an Ansible Vault encrypted file look like, and which cipher does it use?","a":"<p>The file is text. The first line is a header such as <code>$ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.1;AES256</code>, followed by hex-encoded ciphertext. It uses AES-256 with an HMAC for integrity. Format 1.2 adds the vault ID label to the header when one is used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0245","card":"q-ansible-035-encrypting-variables-with-vault","sec":"ansible-s036","q":"In Ansible, how do you encrypt only one value inside an otherwise plain YAML file?","a":"<p>Use <code>ansible-vault encrypt_string 'value' --name 'db_pass'</code>. It prints a <code>!vault |</code> block that you paste into the variable file. This keeps the file diffable and readable, with only the secret encrypted. The trade-off is that the key names are visible and the block is harder to grep and edit than a fully encrypted file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0246","card":"q-ansible-035-encrypting-variables-with-vault","sec":"ansible-s036","q":"Why do many teams keep a plain variable file that points to an encrypted one in Ansible, such as <code>db_pass: \"{{ vault_db_pass }}\"</code>?","a":"<p>The plain file shows which variables exist and where they are used, so reviewers and <code>grep</code> still work. Only the <code>vault_</code>-prefixed values sit in the encrypted file. It also makes the encrypted file the only thing that needs the vault password.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0247","card":"q-ansible-035-encrypting-variables-with-vault","sec":"ansible-s036","q":"When would you use Ansible Vault and when an external secrets manager such as HashiCorp Vault or AWS Secrets Manager?","a":"<p>Vault is simple, needs no extra service and fits small teams and bootstrap secrets. An external manager gives central access control, audit logs, rotation and short-lived credentials, and Ansible can read from it with lookup plugins. Large or regulated environments usually prefer the manager, and often still use Ansible Vault for the one secret needed to reach it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0248","card":"q-ansible-035-encrypting-variables-with-vault","sec":"ansible-s036","q":"Why is it not enough to delete a plaintext secret from a Git repository after encrypting it with Ansible Vault?","a":"<p>The old commit still contains the plaintext in history. Treat the secret as exposed: rotate it, then commit the encrypted version. Re-keying the vault changes only the vault password, not the secret that was already leaked.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0249","card":"q-ansible-036-using-a-vault-variable-in-a-playbook","sec":"ansible-s037","q":"In an Ansible play, how does the play get a variable from a vault-encrypted file?","a":"<p><code>vars_files</code> loads the file as variables for the play. If the file is vault-encrypted, Ansible decrypts it in memory when it has a vault password, supplied for example with <code>--ask-vault-pass</code>, <code>--vault-password-file</code> or <code>--vault-id</code>. The file stays encrypted on disk.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>  vars_files:\n    - secrets.yml</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0250","card":"q-ansible-036-using-a-vault-variable-in-a-playbook","sec":"ansible-s037","q":"What error appears when an Ansible play loads an encrypted file with <code>vars_files</code> and no vault password is given?","a":"<p>The run stops with an error saying it attempted to decrypt a file but no vault secrets were found (and the file cannot be decoded). Fix it by passing <code>--ask-vault-pass</code>, <code>--vault-password-file</code> or <code>--vault-id</code>, or by setting <code>vault_password_file</code> in <code>ansible.cfg</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0251","card":"q-ansible-036-using-a-vault-variable-in-a-playbook","sec":"ansible-s037","q":"What is the danger in this Ansible task, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>It prints the decrypted secret in the console and in any log or CI output. Never print vault variables. If a task must use a secret, for example to pass it to a module, add <code>no_log: true</code> to that task so Ansible hides its arguments and results.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>    - name: Print the database password\n      ansible.builtin.debug:\n        msg: &quot;The database password is {{ db_pass }}&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0252","card":"q-ansible-036-using-a-vault-variable-in-a-playbook","sec":"ansible-s037","q":"In Ansible, why does a <code>mysql_user</code> task that takes <code>password: \"{{ db_pass }}\"</code> need <code>no_log: true</code> even though the variable comes from a vault file?","a":"<p>Vault protects the file at rest only. Once the variable is decrypted and passed to the module, Ansible would show the module arguments in verbose output, in failure messages and in callback logs. <code>no_log: true</code> suppresses that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0253","card":"q-ansible-036-using-a-vault-variable-in-a-playbook","sec":"ansible-s037","q":"In Ansible, why is <code>priv: '*.*:ALL'</code> for an application database user a bad practice?","a":"<p>It grants all privileges on all databases and tables, which is far more than an application needs. Grant only the privileges needed on the one database, for example <code>appdb.*:SELECT,INSERT,UPDATE,DELETE</code>. This limits the damage if the credential leaks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0254","card":"q-ansible-036-using-a-vault-variable-in-a-playbook","sec":"ansible-s037","q":"What do you need so that an Ansible <code>mysql_user</code> task works on the managed node?","a":"<p>The <code>mysql_user</code> module lives in the <code>community.mysql</code> collection, so the collection must be installed on the control node. The target host (or the host the module runs on) needs a Python MySQL driver such as PyMySQL, and the task needs working login credentials for MySQL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0255","card":"q-ansible-037-vault-commands-and-passwords","sec":"ansible-s038","q":"Edit a vault-encrypted file?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-vault edit secrets.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0256","card":"q-ansible-037-vault-commands-and-passwords","sec":"ansible-s038","q":"Use a vault variable in a playbook?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>vars_files:\n  - secrets.yml\ntasks:\n  - debug:\n      msg: &quot;{{ my_secret }}&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Run with <code>--ask-vault-pass</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0257","card":"q-ansible-037-vault-commands-and-passwords","sec":"ansible-s038","q":"Single-file vs multi-file encryption?","a":"<p>Single: one file, one password. Multi: multiple files with separate passwords or IDs.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0258","card":"q-ansible-037-vault-commands-and-passwords","sec":"ansible-s038","q":"Specify the vault password?","a":"<p>Use <code>--ask-vault-pass</code> or <code>--vault-password-file pass.txt</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0259","card":"q-ansible-037-vault-commands-and-passwords","sec":"ansible-s038","q":"Which <code>ansible-vault</code> subcommand do you use to read, change, decrypt or re-key an encrypted file?","a":"<p><code>view</code> shows the content without saving it. <code>edit</code> decrypts into an editor and re-encrypts on save. <code>decrypt</code> writes plaintext back to disk, so avoid it. <code>rekey</code> changes the vault password. <code>encrypt</code> and <code>create</code> make encrypted files, and <code>encrypt_string</code> encrypts a single value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0260","card":"q-ansible-037-vault-commands-and-passwords","sec":"ansible-s038","q":"In Ansible, how do you give the vault password to a playbook run in CI where nobody can type?","a":"<p>Use <code>--vault-password-file</code>, pointing to a file that the CI system creates from a stored secret just for the run, or set <code>ANSIBLE_VAULT_PASSWORD_FILE</code>. The file can also be an executable script that prints the password, for example by reading it from a secrets manager. Avoid committing the password file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0261","card":"q-ansible-037-vault-commands-and-passwords","sec":"ansible-s038","q":"What are the security rules for a vault password file in Ansible?","a":"<p>Keep it out of the repository (add it to <code>.gitignore</code>), restrict permissions (<code>chmod 600</code>), and keep it on the machine that runs Ansible only. If Ansible sees the file is executable, it runs it and reads the password from stdout, which allows fetching it from a secrets manager instead of storing plaintext.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook playbook.yml --vault-password-file pass.txt</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0262","card":"q-ansible-037-vault-commands-and-passwords","sec":"ansible-s038","q":"What is the difference between <code>--ask-vault-pass</code> and <code>--vault-password-file</code> in Ansible, and when do you use each?","a":"<p><code>--ask-vault-pass</code> prompts for the password, so it fits interactive runs and nothing is stored. <code>--vault-password-file</code> reads it from a file or script, so it fits automation. The prompt cannot run unattended, and the file must be protected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0263","card":"q-ansible-037-vault-commands-and-passwords","sec":"ansible-s038","q":"What is the difference between using a single vault password and multiple vault passwords in Ansible?","a":"<p>With a single password every encrypted file is opened by the same secret, which is simple but means anyone who has it can read all secrets. With multiple passwords, usually through vault IDs, each environment or team uses its own, so the developers' password cannot decrypt production secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0264","card":"q-ansible-037-vault-commands-and-passwords","sec":"ansible-s038","q":"In Ansible, how would you change the vault password of existing encrypted files after someone leaves the team?","a":"<p>Run <code>ansible-vault rekey</code> on every encrypted file with the old and new password, and distribute the new one. Rekeying does not change the underlying secrets, so also rotate the secrets themselves if the person could have read them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0265","card":"q-ansible-038-multiple-vault-passwords-with-vault-ids","sec":"ansible-s039","q":"Manage multiple vault passwords?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-vault encrypt secrets.yml --vault-id dev@prompt\nansible-playbook --vault-id dev@dev_pass.txt</code></pre></div>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>--vault-id dev@prompt</code>: <code>dev</code> is a user-defined label and <code>prompt</code> means the password is typed in.</li>\n  <li><code>--vault-id dev@dev_pass.txt</code>: Ansible reads the password for the <code>dev</code> label from <code>dev_pass.txt</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Different environments can use different IDs (<code>dev@prompt</code>, <code>prod@prod_pass.txt</code>), and several <code>--vault-id</code> flags can be passed in one run. A password file holds the plaintext password, so restrict it (<code>chmod 600 dev_pass.txt</code>) or use a secrets manager.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0266","card":"q-ansible-038-multiple-vault-passwords-with-vault-ids","sec":"ansible-s039","q":"In Ansible, what do the label and the location mean in <code>--vault-id dev@prompt</code>?","a":"<p>The part before <code>@</code> is a label you choose (<code>dev</code>) that names the vault. The part after is where the password comes from: <code>prompt</code> asks you to type it, or a file path or script reads it. The label is stored in the encrypted file header, so Ansible knows which password to try first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0267","card":"q-ansible-038-multiple-vault-passwords-with-vault-ids","sec":"ansible-s039","q":"Why use vault IDs in Ansible instead of one shared vault password?","a":"<p>To separate secrets by environment or team. Dev and prod files get different passwords, so a developer who has the dev password cannot read production secrets. A single run can load several IDs, for example <code>--vault-id dev@dev_pass.txt --vault-id prod@prompt</code>, and Ansible picks the right one per file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0268","card":"q-ansible-038-multiple-vault-passwords-with-vault-ids","sec":"ansible-s039","q":"How do you encrypt a production file with its own password using vault IDs in Ansible?","a":"<p>Use the same command with the production label, for example <code>ansible-vault encrypt prod_secrets.yml --vault-id prod@prompt</code>. When you encrypt with <code>--vault-id label@location</code>, the label is written into the file header (format 1.2). If several IDs are supplied, choose the one to encrypt with <code>--encrypt-vault-id label</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-vault encrypt secrets.yml --vault-id dev@prompt</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0269","card":"q-ansible-038-multiple-vault-passwords-with-vault-ids","sec":"ansible-s039","q":"What happens in Ansible if a vault-encrypted file has a label that does not match any <code>--vault-id</code> you passed?","a":"<p>Ansible tries the passwords it was given against the file anyway, since the label is only a hint for which to try first. If one of them decrypts it, the run continues. If none does, the run fails with a decryption error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0270","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"What are Roles? Why useful?","a":"<p>Modular bundles of tasks and variables (for example web setup). They are reusable, organized, and reduce playbook clutter.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0271","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"Create a role?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-galaxy init my_role</code></pre></div>\n<p>It creates <code>tasks/</code>, <code>vars/</code> and the other standard directories.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0272","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"Role directory structure?","a":"<p><code>tasks/</code>, <code>defaults/</code>, <code>vars/</code>, <code>handlers/</code>, <code>templates/</code>, <code>files/</code>, <code>meta/</code>, <code>tests/</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0273","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"Call a role in a playbook?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- hosts: webservers\n  roles:\n    - my_role</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0274","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"What problem do Ansible roles solve compared with one large playbook?","a":"<p>A role groups tasks, handlers, variables, templates and files for one purpose, such as installing nginx, in a standard layout. This makes the logic reusable across playbooks and projects, easier to test, and easier to share, while the playbook stays short and only says which roles apply to which hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0275","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"What does <code>ansible-galaxy init my_role</code> create, and which files does Ansible load automatically?","a":"<p>It creates the standard role skeleton: <code>tasks/</code>, <code>handlers/</code>, <code>defaults/</code>, <code>vars/</code>, <code>files/</code>, <code>templates/</code>, <code>meta/</code> and <code>tests/</code>. Ansible automatically loads <code>main.yml</code> from <code>tasks</code>, <code>handlers</code>, <code>defaults</code>, <code>vars</code> and <code>meta</code> when the role is used. Other task files are used only if you include them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0276","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"In an Ansible role, what is the difference between the <code>files/</code> and <code>templates/</code> directories?","a":"<p><code>files/</code> holds static files that the <code>copy</code> module sends as they are. <code>templates/</code> holds Jinja2 <code>.j2</code> files that the <code>template</code> module renders with variables first. For both, a role task can use a bare file name and Ansible searches the matching directory of the role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0277","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"In Ansible, how do you apply a role to hosts?","a":"<p>List it under <code>roles:</code> in a play. The <code>hosts:</code> line picks the machines. Roles listed under <code>roles:</code> run before the play's own <code>tasks:</code> (after <code>pre_tasks</code>), and their handlers are available to the whole play.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- hosts: webservers\n  roles:\n    - my_role</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0278","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"In what order does an Ansible play run <code>pre_tasks</code>, <code>roles</code>, <code>tasks</code>, <code>post_tasks</code> and handlers?","a":"<p><code>pre_tasks</code> run first, then notified handlers are flushed. Next the <code>roles</code> and then the <code>tasks</code> run, and handlers notified there are flushed. Last <code>post_tasks</code> run, followed by a final handler flush. A role listed under <code>roles:</code> therefore always runs before the play's <code>tasks:</code> regardless of where you write the keys.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0279","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"When should a group of tasks in an Ansible playbook become a role and when is a plain task file enough?","a":"<p>Make it a role when it has its own variables, templates, files or handlers, is used by more than one playbook, or is shared with others. A task file is enough for a few related steps that belong to one playbook and have no supporting files.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0280","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"What does the <code>tests/</code> directory of an Ansible role contain?","a":"<p>A small sample inventory and playbook to try the role, created by <code>ansible-galaxy init</code>. Ansible does not run it automatically. Real role testing is usually done with a tool such as Molecule.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0281","card":"q-ansible-039-what-roles-are-and-how-to-create-one","sec":"ansible-s040","q":"How do you test an Ansible role?","a":"<p>Use Molecule: it creates a test instance (usually a container), runs the role (<code>converge</code>), runs it again to check idempotence (the second run must report no changes), and then runs verification checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0282","card":"q-ansible-040-tasks-vs-defaults","sec":"ansible-s041","q":"Tasks vs defaults in a role?","a":"<p><code>tasks/</code> holds the actions (install, configure). <code>defaults/</code> holds fallback variables with the lowest precedence, so any other source overrides them.</p>\n<p><code>roles/my_role/tasks/main.yml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install nginx\n  ansible.builtin.package:\n    name: nginx\n    state: present\n\n- name: Ensure nginx is running\n  ansible.builtin.service:\n    name: nginx\n    state: started\n    enabled: yes</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>roles/my_role/defaults/main.yml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>port: 80\nwebserver_user: www-data</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0283","card":"q-ansible-040-tasks-vs-defaults","sec":"ansible-s041","q":"In an Ansible role, where do the installation steps go and where do the settings they use go?","a":"<p>Steps go in <code>tasks/main.yml</code> and the settings (port, user, version) go in <code>defaults/main.yml</code>. Tasks then refer to <code>{{ port }}</code>. This keeps the logic fixed and the settings overridable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0284","card":"q-ansible-040-tasks-vs-defaults","sec":"ansible-s041","q":"Why use <code>ansible.builtin.package</code> instead of <code>apt</code> or <code>yum</code> in a reusable Ansible role task?","a":"<p><code>package</code> is a generic front end that picks the package manager of the host. The trade-off is that it supports only basic options. When you need <code>apt</code> or <code>yum</code> features such as <code>update_cache</code>, use the specific module.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install nginx\n  ansible.builtin.package:\n    name: nginx\n    state: present</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0285","card":"q-ansible-040-tasks-vs-defaults","sec":"ansible-s041","q":"In Ansible, why does the nginx role start the service with <code>enabled: yes</code> as well as <code>state: started</code>?","a":"<p><code>state: started</code> makes sure it is running now, and <code>enabled: yes</code> makes it start at boot. Without <code>enabled</code>, the service would be down after a reboot even though the playbook reports success.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Ensure nginx is running\n  ansible.builtin.service:\n    name: nginx\n    state: started\n    enabled: yes</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0286","card":"q-ansible-041-passing-variables-to-a-role","sec":"ansible-s042","q":"Pass variables to a role?","a":"<p>Variables passed in the playbook override the role defaults, so the same role serves different environments (for example port 8080 for dev and 443 for prod).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- hosts: webservers\n  roles:\n    - role: my_role\n      vars:\n        port: 8080</code></pre></div>\n<p>If <code>port</code> is not passed, the role falls back to the default <code>port: 80</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0287","card":"q-ansible-041-passing-variables-to-a-role","sec":"ansible-s042","q":"In Ansible, what does the <code>vars</code> key do when a role is listed like this, and what is the precedence of that value?","a":"<p>It sets <code>port</code> for that role (as a role parameter). That value overrides the role's <code>defaults</code> and also inventory variables, so it is hard to override later except with <code>-e</code>. Pass values this way for fixed intent in the playbook. For per-environment settings, <code>group_vars</code> is more flexible.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- hosts: webservers\n  roles:\n    - role: my_role\n      vars:\n        port: 8080</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0288","card":"q-ansible-041-passing-variables-to-a-role","sec":"ansible-s042","q":"In Ansible, why can overriding role variables inline in the <code>roles:</code> list be a trap?","a":"<p>Role parameters have a high precedence, so inventory <code>group_vars</code> cannot override them any more. A user who sets the value in the inventory will be surprised that it has no effect. It also hardcodes environment values in the playbook.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0289","card":"q-ansible-041-passing-variables-to-a-role","sec":"ansible-s042","q":"What happens in Ansible if a role uses <code>{{ port }}</code> and neither the role defaults nor the caller define <code>port</code>?","a":"<p>The template or task fails with an undefined variable error. Defining every variable the role needs in <code>defaults/main.yml</code> prevents this and also documents the role's interface.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0290","card":"q-ansible-042-reusing-roles-across-playbooks","sec":"ansible-s043","q":"Reuse roles across playbooks?","a":"<p>Store them in <code>roles/</code> and call them with <code>roles:</code> or <code>include_role</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible_project/\n├── playbooks/\n│   └── deploy.yml\n├── roles/\n│   └── my_role/\n│       ├── tasks/main.yml\n│       ├── defaults/main.yml\n│       └── templates/nginx.conf.j2\n└── inventory</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>roles:</code> applies the role at play level, before the play's tasks. <code>include_role</code> applies it as a task, giving control over when it runs (inside a loop or conditionally).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Apply my_role dynamically\n  hosts: webservers\n  tasks:\n    - name: Include my_role with custom port\n      ansible.builtin.include_role:\n        name: my_role\n      vars:\n        port: 8080</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0291","card":"q-ansible-042-reusing-roles-across-playbooks","sec":"ansible-s043","q":"What is the difference between <code>roles:</code> and <code>include_role</code> in Ansible?","a":"<p><code>roles:</code> is static and applied at play level, before the play's tasks. <code>include_role</code> is a task that is processed at runtime, so it can run conditionally, in a loop, or at a precise point between other tasks. <code>import_role</code> is the static version of the task form: parsed when the playbook loads.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0292","card":"q-ansible-042-reusing-roles-across-playbooks","sec":"ansible-s043","q":"In Ansible, how do you run a role only on hosts that satisfy a condition, in the middle of a task list?","a":"<p>Add <code>when:</code> to the <code>include_role</code> task. The condition is evaluated at runtime on each host, and the role is skipped if it is false. With the <code>roles:</code> list you would need <code>when</code> on the role entry, which applies to every task in the role.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>    - name: Include my_role with custom port\n      ansible.builtin.include_role:\n        name: my_role\n      vars:\n        port: 8080</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0293","card":"q-ansible-042-reusing-roles-across-playbooks","sec":"ansible-s043","q":"Where do you keep shared roles for several Ansible playbooks so they are found automatically?","a":"<p>In a <code>roles/</code> directory next to the playbook, which Ansible searches by default. Other places can be added with <code>roles_path</code> in <code>ansible.cfg</code>, or by installing roles from Galaxy or a Git repository into a shared directory, which is better for roles used by many projects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0294","card":"q-ansible-042-reusing-roles-across-playbooks","sec":"ansible-s043","q":"How would you share an Ansible role between several repositories without copying it?","a":"<p>Publish it as its own Git repository (or a collection) and list it in <code>requirements.yml</code> with a version tag. Each project installs it with <code>ansible-galaxy install -r requirements.yml</code>. This keeps a single source of truth and lets each project pin the version it tested.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0295","card":"q-ansible-043-complete-role-example","sec":"ansible-s044","q":"In Ansible, what happens to <code>notify: Restart nginx</code> when the <code>template</code> task reports no change?","a":"<p>Nothing. A handler is queued only when a task reports <code>changed</code>. If the rendered file is identical to the file on the host, the task is <code>ok</code>, and nginx is not restarted.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Configure nginx\n  ansible.builtin.template:\n    src: nginx.conf.j2\n    dest: /etc/nginx/nginx.conf\n    owner: root\n    group: root\n    mode: &#x27;0644&#x27;\n  notify: Restart nginx</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0296","card":"q-ansible-043-complete-role-example","sec":"ansible-s044","q":"In an Ansible role, where does a handler like <code>Restart nginx</code> have to be defined for a role task to notify it?","a":"<p>In the role's <code>handlers/main.yml</code>. Ansible loads it with the role, and handler names are matched by name across the play, so a task in the same role can notify it. Because names are global, give handlers distinctive names or use <code>listen</code> topics to avoid collisions between roles.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0297","card":"q-ansible-043-complete-role-example","sec":"ansible-s044","q":"Why does the role's template use <code>listen {{ port }};</code> and how should the value be supplied?","a":"<p>The port is a variable so one template works for every environment. Supply the default in the role's <code>defaults/main.yml</code> and override it per environment through <code>group_vars</code> or role parameters. A hardcoded port would require editing the template per environment.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Text</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>server {\n    listen {{ port }};\n    server_name example.com;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0298","card":"q-ansible-043-complete-role-example","sec":"ansible-s044","q":"In an Ansible role, in what order should install, configure and start tasks run, and why?","a":"<p>Install the package, then render the configuration, then ensure the service is started and enabled. Installing first creates the directories the config file needs. Configuring before starting avoids starting the service with the default config. If config changes later, the handler restarts it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0299","card":"q-ansible-044-reusing-a-role-per-environment","sec":"ansible-s045","q":"Two Ansible plays call the same role with <code>port: 8080</code> for dev and <code>port: 443</code> for prod. What is a cleaner design?","a":"<p>Keep one play that applies the role to <code>webservers</code>, and set <code>port</code> per environment in <code>group_vars</code> of each inventory (or per group). The playbook then has no environment-specific values, and a new environment needs only a new inventory.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- hosts: dev_webservers\n  roles:\n    - role: my_role\n      vars:\n        port: 8080</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0300","card":"q-ansible-044-reusing-a-role-per-environment","sec":"ansible-s045","q":"What are the benefits of using one Ansible role for several environments?","a":"<p>One tested implementation, consistent behaviour across dev and prod, no duplicated tasks, and a single place to fix a bug. Only the variables differ between environments.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0301","card":"q-ansible-044-reusing-a-role-per-environment","sec":"ansible-s045","q":"In Ansible, how do you make sure a change to a shared role is safe for production?","a":"<p>Test it on a dev or staging inventory first, use <code>--check --diff</code> to preview, pin the role version in <code>requirements.yml</code> for production, and roll out in stages with <code>--limit</code> or <code>serial</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0302","card":"q-ansible-045-meta-galaxy-and-role-debugging","sec":"ansible-s046","q":"Purpose of the <code>meta</code> directory?","a":"<p><code>meta/main.yml</code> defines role dependencies and info:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dependencies:\n  - role: common</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0303","card":"q-ansible-045-meta-galaxy-and-role-debugging","sec":"ansible-s046","q":"Install roles from Galaxy?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-galaxy install geerlingguy.nginx</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- roles:\n    - geerlingguy.nginx</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0304","card":"q-ansible-045-meta-galaxy-and-role-debugging","sec":"ansible-s046","q":"Debug role issues?","a":"<p>Use <code>-v</code>, <code>--check</code> and tags, and add a debug task:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- debug:\n    var: my_var</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0305","card":"q-ansible-045-meta-galaxy-and-role-debugging","sec":"ansible-s046","q":"In an Ansible role, what does <code>meta/main.yml</code> with <code>dependencies</code> do?","a":"<p>It runs the listed roles before this role, every time this role is used. It is useful for shared setup like a <code>common</code> role. Dependency roles run only once per play unless they have different parameters or <code>allow_duplicates: true</code>. The same file holds Galaxy metadata such as author, license and supported platforms.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dependencies:\n  - role: common</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0306","card":"q-ansible-045-meta-galaxy-and-role-debugging","sec":"ansible-s046","q":"How do you install roles from Ansible Galaxy reproducibly for a project?","a":"<p>List them in a <code>requirements.yml</code> with exact versions, then run <code>ansible-galaxy install -r requirements.yml</code>. Pinning avoids a surprise change when a community role is updated. Review third-party roles before using them, since they run with your privileges.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0307","card":"q-ansible-045-meta-galaxy-and-role-debugging","sec":"ansible-s046","q":"In Ansible, what is the difference between installing a role with <code>ansible-galaxy install</code> and a collection with <code>ansible-galaxy collection install</code>?","a":"<p>A role is a single reusable unit of tasks. A collection is a distribution package that can hold modules, plugins, roles and playbooks under a namespace, for example <code>community.docker</code>. Modules that used to ship with Ansible are now delivered in collections.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0308","card":"q-ansible-045-meta-galaxy-and-role-debugging","sec":"ansible-s046","q":"A role behaves wrongly. What steps do you take in Ansible to find the cause?","a":"<p>Run with <code>-v</code> or more to see module results. Use <code>--check --diff</code> to see what would change, <code>--list-tasks</code> and <code>--list-tags</code> to see what runs, and <code>--tags</code> or <code>--start-at-task</code> to narrow it. Add <code>debug</code> tasks with <code>var:</code> to print variables. Remember variable precedence: a role variable may be overridden by a higher location.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0309","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"What is Ansible Tower/AWX?","a":"<p>A GUI, API and workflow layer over Ansible. Core Ansible is CLI only; Tower (commercial) and AWX (open source) add scheduling and RBAC. AWX can be installed with Docker or Kubernetes, then used to create projects, inventories and job templates (for example schedule a playbook daily).</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0310","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"Configure Ansible with SSH keys?","a":"<p>Generate a key (<code>ssh-keygen</code>), copy it to hosts (<code>ssh-copy-id</code>), and set it in the inventory or <code>ansible.cfg</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0311","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"Purpose of the <code>--check</code> flag?","a":"<p>A dry run: it simulates the playbook without making changes.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0312","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"Limit a playbook to specific hosts?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook playbook.yml --limit webservers</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0313","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"Why would a team use AWX or Ansible Tower instead of running <code>ansible-playbook</code> from laptops?","a":"<p>It adds what the command line lacks: role-based access control, a central credential store so users can run jobs without seeing keys, scheduling, job history and logs, a REST API, workflows that chain job templates, and notifications. This gives auditability and safe self-service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0314","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"What is the relationship between AWX, Ansible Tower and Red Hat Ansible Automation Platform?","a":"<p>AWX is the open source upstream project. Tower was the commercial product built from it, and in Ansible Automation Platform 2.x it is called automation controller. They offer the same kind of web UI and API on top of Ansible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0315","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"What are job templates, inventories and projects in AWX?","a":"<p>A project points to a source control repository that holds playbooks. An inventory is the set of hosts. A job template ties together a playbook, an inventory, credentials and options. Running the template creates a job, and templates can be scheduled or chained into workflows.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0316","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"How do you set up SSH key authentication for Ansible and keep the private key safe?","a":"<p>Generate a key pair with <code>ssh-keygen</code>, install the public key on the hosts with <code>ssh-copy-id</code> (or a bootstrap playbook), and point Ansible to the private key with <code>ansible_ssh_private_key_file</code> or <code>private_key_file</code> in <code>ansible.cfg</code>. Protect the key with a passphrase and <code>ssh-agent</code>, and give it file mode 600. Use a dedicated key per environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0317","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"What are the limits of <code>--check</code> mode in Ansible?","a":"<p>A dry run only predicts changes. Modules that do not support check mode are skipped, so later tasks that depend on their result may behave differently. <code>command</code> and <code>shell</code> tasks are skipped unless the task sets <code>check_mode: false</code>. Combine it with <code>--diff</code> to see file changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0318","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"In Ansible, how do you make one task always run even during <code>--check</code>?","a":"<p>Set <code>check_mode: false</code> on the task (older playbooks use <code>check_mode: no</code>). This is useful for read-only tasks whose result later tasks need, for example a <code>command</code> that registers a version number.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0319","card":"q-ansible-046-tower-awx-ssh-keys-check-mode-limit","sec":"ansible-s047","q":"What are execution environments in Ansible Automation Platform 2.x, and how does <code>ansible-navigator</code> use them?","a":"<p>An execution environment is a container image holding ansible-core, collections and Python dependencies. <code>ansible-navigator</code> runs playbooks inside one, so a run behaves the same on a laptop and in AWX or Automation Platform.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0320","card":"q-ansible-047-delegate-to","sec":"ansible-s048","q":"What is <code>delegate_to</code>?","a":"<p>It runs a task on another host.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- command: script.sh\n  delegate_to: localhost</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0321","card":"q-ansible-047-delegate-to","sec":"ansible-s048","q":"What does <code>delegate_to</code> do in Ansible, and what is the usual reason to use it?","a":"<p>It runs the task on a different host than the one being processed in the play, while variables still come from the original host. Typical uses: calling an API or load balancer from the control node, removing a node from a pool before updating it, or taking a snapshot on a hypervisor.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- command: script.sh\n  delegate_to: localhost</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0322","card":"q-ansible-047-delegate-to","sec":"ansible-s048","q":"In Ansible, which host's variables and facts are used when a task has <code>delegate_to</code>?","a":"<p>Those of the original inventory host. For example, <code>inventory_hostname</code> still names the host being processed, not the delegate. To store gathered facts under the delegated host use <code>delegate_facts: true</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0323","card":"q-ansible-047-delegate-to","sec":"ansible-s048","q":"How do you make a delegated Ansible task run once instead of once per host in the play?","a":"<p>Combine it with <code>run_once: true</code>. For example, a task that registers a deploy in an external system can use <code>delegate_to: localhost</code> with <code>run_once: true</code>, so it runs a single time for the whole play.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0324","card":"q-ansible-047-delegate-to","sec":"ansible-s048","q":"What is the difference between <code>delegate_to: localhost</code> and <code>connection: local</code> in Ansible?","a":"<p><code>delegate_to: localhost</code> runs this task on the control node but the host is still the inventory host for variables. <code>connection: local</code> changes how Ansible connects to the host being processed, so the task runs on the control node as if that host were local. Use <code>delegate_to</code> for single tasks and <code>connection: local</code> for a whole play or inventory host such as an API-only target.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0325","card":"q-ansible-048-parallel-execution-and-forks","sec":"ansible-s049","q":"How does parallel execution work?","a":"<p>Forks (default 5) run tasks across hosts concurrently, while tasks on each single host run sequentially. With 10 hosts and the default, Ansible does 5 hosts, then the next 5.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0326","card":"q-ansible-048-parallel-execution-and-forks","sec":"ansible-s049","q":"What are <code>forks</code>?","a":"<p>The number of parallel processes. Higher forks (for example 20) speed up large runs but strain the control node (CPU, memory, SSH connections). Set it with <code>-f</code> or in <code>ansible.cfg</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0327","card":"q-ansible-048-parallel-execution-and-forks","sec":"ansible-s049","q":"How does Ansible decide how many hosts it works on at the same time?","a":"<p>By <code>forks</code>, default 5. Ansible starts up to that many worker processes, each handling one host for the current task. With 10 hosts and the default, it runs a task on 5 hosts and then on the other 5. Tasks on one host still run one after another.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0328","card":"q-ansible-048-parallel-execution-and-forks","sec":"ansible-s049","q":"In Ansible, what limits how high you can set <code>forks</code>?","a":"<p>The control node. Each fork is a process using memory and CPU and usually its own SSH connection, so very high values can exhaust RAM, CPU or file descriptors, or overload target hosts and bastions. Raise it gradually and watch the load on the control node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0329","card":"q-ansible-048-parallel-execution-and-forks","sec":"ansible-s049","q":"What is the difference between <code>forks</code> and <code>serial</code> in Ansible?","a":"<p><code>forks</code> is how many hosts run in parallel for a task, a performance setting of the control node. <code>serial</code> splits a play into batches of hosts and finishes the whole play on one batch before the next batch, which is for rolling updates and limiting blast radius. Forks work inside each serial batch.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0330","card":"q-ansible-048-parallel-execution-and-forks","sec":"ansible-s049","q":"Does setting <code>forks</code> higher than the number of hosts make Ansible faster?","a":"<p>No. Ansible never uses more workers than there are hosts for a task, so extra forks give no gain. Set it to roughly the number of hosts you want to handle at once, within the limits of the control node.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0331","card":"q-ansible-048-parallel-execution-and-forks","sec":"ansible-s049","q":"Where can you set the number of forks in Ansible, and which one wins?","a":"<p>In <code>ansible.cfg</code> under <code>[defaults]</code> as <code>forks = 20</code>, with the <code>ANSIBLE_FORKS</code> environment variable, or with <code>-f</code> on the command line. The command-line flag overrides the environment variable, which overrides the config file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0332","card":"q-ansible-049-forks-example","sec":"ansible-s050","q":"In Ansible, 10 hosts run one task that sleeps 2 seconds. Roughly how long does the task take with 5 forks and with 10 forks, and why?","a":"<p>About 4 seconds with 5 forks and about 2 seconds with 10, plus connection overhead. With 5 forks the hosts run in two batches of 5, one after the other, each taking 2 seconds. With 10 forks all hosts run at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0333","card":"q-ansible-050-optimizing-ansible-for-scale","sec":"ansible-s051","q":"Optimize Ansible for scale?","a":"<p>More forks, pipelining, dynamic inventory, fact caching, and Tower/AWX.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0334","card":"q-ansible-050-optimizing-ansible-for-scale","sec":"ansible-s051","q":"Your Ansible run against 500 hosts is slow. Which settings or techniques do you try, in what order?","a":"<p>First measure with the <code>profile_tasks</code> callback to find slow tasks. Then raise <code>forks</code>, enable SSH pipelining and <code>ControlPersist</code>, avoid gathering facts when unused (<code>gather_facts: false</code> or fact caching), use the <code>free</code> strategy where order does not matter, and prefer modules that handle many items in one call, such as passing a list to <code>package</code>. For very large fleets, consider pull mode or AWX with multiple execution nodes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0335","card":"q-ansible-050-optimizing-ansible-for-scale","sec":"ansible-s051","q":"How do you measure where time goes in an Ansible playbook?","a":"<p>Enable callbacks such as <code>profile_tasks</code> and <code>timer</code> (<code>callbacks_enabled = profile_tasks, timer</code> in <code>ansible.cfg</code> in recent versions; older versions used <code>callback_whitelist</code>). They print the duration of every task and the total, so you can optimise the slowest ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0336","card":"q-ansible-050-optimizing-ansible-for-scale","sec":"ansible-s051","q":"Why is running a task once per item with <code>loop</code> slower than passing the whole list to the module where possible in Ansible?","a":"<p>Each loop iteration is a separate module execution, with its own connection work and result handling per host. Modules like <code>package</code> and <code>apt</code> accept a list in <code>name</code> and handle all items in one call, which is faster and lets the package manager resolve dependencies together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0337","card":"q-ansible-051-pipelining","sec":"ansible-s052","q":"In Ansible, what does SSH pipelining change in how a module is executed on the target?","a":"<p>Without pipelining, Ansible copies the module file to a temporary directory on the target with an extra SSH operation, runs it, then removes it. With pipelining, it sends the module over the existing SSH connection to the interpreter's standard input, which removes the copy and cleanup steps. That is fewer SSH operations per task and a faster run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0338","card":"q-ansible-051-pipelining","sec":"ansible-s052","q":"What is the default value of <code>pipelining</code> in Ansible, and where do you enable it?","a":"<p>It is off by default. Enable it in the <code>[ssh_connection]</code> section of <code>ansible.cfg</code>, or with <code>ANSIBLE_PIPELINING=True</code>. It is not on by default because of the <code>requiretty</code> issue on some older systems and with some privilege escalation setups.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[ssh_connection]\npipelining = True</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0339","card":"q-ansible-051-pipelining","sec":"ansible-s052","q":"Why does <code>requiretty</code> in sudoers break Ansible pipelining, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>With pipelining and <code>become</code>, Ansible runs <code>sudo</code> without a terminal. If <code>Defaults requiretty</code> is set, <code>sudo</code> refuses to run without a TTY and the task fails. Remove that setting (or exempt the Ansible user) in <code>/etc/sudoers</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0340","card":"q-ansible-051-pipelining","sec":"ansible-s052","q":"How would you verify that pipelining makes a difference for a playbook?","a":"<p>Run the same playbook with <code>pipelining = False</code> and then <code>True</code> and compare task times, for example with the <code>profile_tasks</code> callback. The gain grows with the number of tasks per host and with SSH latency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0341","card":"q-ansible-052-dynamic-inventory-with-aws-ec2","sec":"ansible-s053","q":"In Ansible, an inventory file named <code>aws_inventory.yml</code> contains <code>plugin: aws_ec2</code>, but the plugin rejects it. What is likely wrong?","a":"<p>The <code>aws_ec2</code> inventory plugin accepts only files whose name ends in <code>aws_ec2.yml</code> or <code>aws_ec2.yaml</code>. Rename the file, for example to <code>prod.aws_ec2.yml</code>, otherwise Ansible warns that the file did not meet the plugin's requirements and ignores it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>plugin: aws_ec2\nregions:\n  - us-east-1</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0342","card":"q-ansible-052-dynamic-inventory-with-aws-ec2","sec":"ansible-s053","q":"What does <code>filters: instance-state-name: running</code> do in the Ansible <code>aws_ec2</code> inventory plugin, and why use it?","a":"<p>It passes an EC2 <code>DescribeInstances</code> filter, so only running instances enter the inventory. Without it, stopped and terminated instances are included and show up as unreachable hosts.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>filters:\n  instance-state-name: running</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0343","card":"q-ansible-052-dynamic-inventory-with-aws-ec2","sec":"ansible-s053","q":"How does the Ansible <code>aws_ec2</code> inventory plugin get AWS credentials, and what is the safest option on an EC2-based control node?","a":"<p>It uses the standard boto3 credential chain: environment variables, shared credentials or a named profile, or an instance role. On EC2 an instance profile with a least-privilege policy (for example <code>ec2:DescribeInstances</code>) is safest, because there are no long-term keys to store.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0344","card":"q-ansible-052-dynamic-inventory-with-aws-ec2","sec":"ansible-s053","q":"How do you make the <code>aws_ec2</code> plugin connect with private IPs or a specific attribute instead of the public DNS name in Ansible?","a":"<p>Use the <code>hostnames</code> option to choose what becomes the host name, for example <code>private-ip-address</code>, or set <code>compose</code> with <code>ansible_host: private_ip_address</code>. This is required when instances have no public address and the control node reaches them over a VPC network or VPN.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0345","card":"q-ansible-052-dynamic-inventory-with-aws-ec2","sec":"ansible-s053","q":"In the Ansible <code>aws_ec2</code> plugin, what does <code>keyed_groups</code> with <code>key: tags.Role</code> and <code>separator: ''</code> produce?","a":"<p>One group per value of the <code>Role</code> tag. With an empty separator and no prefix, the group is named just by the tag value, such as <code>web</code>, instead of <code>Role_web</code>. You can then write <code>hosts: web</code> in a play.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>keyed_groups:\n  - key: tags.Role\n    separator: &#x27;&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0346","card":"q-ansible-053-fact-caching","sec":"ansible-s054","q":"What problem does fact caching solve in Ansible, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>Gathering facts costs time on every play. A cache stores facts so later runs can reuse them. The trade-off is staleness: cached facts may no longer reflect the host (new IP, new package) until they expire or are refreshed, so choose the timeout with that in mind.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0347","card":"q-ansible-053-fact-caching","sec":"ansible-s054","q":"Why might Ansible still gather facts on every run although fact caching is configured?","a":"<p>The default <code>gathering = implicit</code> gathers facts at the start of every play and refreshes the cache anyway. To reuse the cached facts, set <code>gathering = smart</code>, which gathers only for hosts with no valid cache entry.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[defaults]\nfact_caching = redis\nfact_caching_timeout = 86400  # Cache for 24 hours</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0348","card":"q-ansible-053-fact-caching","sec":"ansible-s054","q":"Which fact cache backends does Ansible support, and how do you choose?","a":"<p>Common ones are <code>jsonfile</code> (files on the control node, simple and no extra service), <code>redis</code> and <code>memcached</code> (shared between several control nodes or AWX workers), and <code>yaml</code>. The default is <code>memory</code>, which lasts only for one run. Use <code>jsonfile</code> for a single control node, and Redis when several control nodes should share facts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0349","card":"q-ansible-053-fact-caching","sec":"ansible-s054","q":"What additional setting does the Redis fact cache in Ansible need to find the server?","a":"<p><code>fact_caching_connection</code>, a required setting for the Redis cache, in the form <code>host:port:db</code>, for example <code>localhost:6379:0</code>. The control node also needs the Python <code>redis</code> client installed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0350","card":"q-ansible-053-fact-caching","sec":"ansible-s054","q":"How would you force fresh facts for one run when fact caching is on in Ansible?","a":"<p>Run the playbook with <code>--flush-cache</code> to clear the cache, or run the <code>setup</code> module explicitly in a play so fresh values are gathered and stored. Reduce <code>fact_caching_timeout</code> if facts must be recent.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0351","card":"q-ansible-054-strategies","sec":"ansible-s055","q":"What is <code>strategy</code>?","a":"<p>The task execution mode:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><code>linear</code> (default): waits for all hosts to finish a task before the next one.</li>\n  <li><code>free</code>: each host proceeds independently, so faster hosts do not wait.</li>\n  <li><code>debug</code>: interactive, for troubleshooting.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Test linear strategy\n  hosts: webservers\n  strategy: linear\n  tasks:\n    - name: Task 1 - Simulate variable delay\n      ansible.builtin.command: &quot;sleep {{ sleep_time }}&quot;\n      vars:\n        sleep_time: &quot;{{ 2 if inventory_hostname in [&#x27;host1&#x27;, &#x27;host2&#x27;] else 5 }}&quot;\n    - name: Task 2 - Output\n      ansible.builtin.debug:\n        msg: &quot;Task 2 on {{ inventory_hostname }}&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p>Setting <code>strategy: free</code> or <code>strategy: debug</code> on a play works the same way. With <code>linear</code>, everyone waits for the 5-second hosts; with <code>free</code>, <code>host1</code> and <code>host2</code> move to Task 2 after 2 seconds.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0352","card":"q-ansible-054-strategies","sec":"ansible-s055","q":"In Ansible, when would you choose the <code>free</code> strategy over <code>linear</code>, and what is the risk?","a":"<p>Use <code>free</code> when hosts are independent and some are much slower than others, so fast hosts finish early instead of waiting. The risk is that tasks no longer run in lockstep, so a task cannot assume other hosts have finished an earlier task, and output from hosts is interleaved and harder to read.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0353","card":"q-ansible-054-strategies","sec":"ansible-s055","q":"With the <code>linear</code> strategy, 5 hosts run a task that takes 2 seconds on two hosts and 5 seconds on three. When can the second task start on the fast hosts?","a":"<p>After about 5 seconds. <code>linear</code> waits until every host in the current batch has finished a task before any host starts the next one. With <code>free</code>, the two fast hosts would start their second task after about 2 seconds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0354","card":"q-ansible-054-strategies","sec":"ansible-s055","q":"What does the <code>debug</code> strategy do in Ansible?","a":"<p>When a task fails, it drops into an interactive debugger on that task where you can print and change variables, update task arguments and re-run the task, then continue. It is for troubleshooting a playbook, not for production runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0355","card":"q-ansible-054-strategies","sec":"ansible-s055","q":"What is the <code>host_pinned</code> strategy in Ansible?","a":"<p>It is like <code>free</code> but keeps each worker on one host until that host has completed the whole play, which is useful when one host must finish all tasks without being interleaved with others. It still respects <code>forks</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0356","card":"q-ansible-054-strategies","sec":"ansible-s055","q":"How do you set the default strategy for all plays in Ansible?","a":"<p>Set <code>strategy = free</code> (or another one) under <code>[defaults]</code> in <code>ansible.cfg</code>, or use <code>ANSIBLE_STRATEGY</code>. A play can override it with its own <code>strategy:</code> key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0357","card":"q-ansible-054-strategies","sec":"ansible-s055","q":"How does the Ansible strategy relate to <code>serial</code>?","a":"<p><code>serial</code> chooses which hosts are in the current batch, and the strategy decides how tasks proceed across the hosts in that batch. They can be combined, for example a rolling update in batches of 5 where each batch uses <code>linear</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0358","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"Use Ansible with Docker?","a":"<p>It needs the Docker SDK.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- docker_container:\n    name: nginx\n    image: nginx:latest\n    state: started</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0359","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"Integrate Ansible with CI/CD (Jenkins)?","a":"<p>Use the Jenkins plugin and credentials.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansiblePlaybook playbook: &#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;, inventory: &#x27;hosts&#x27;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0360","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"What are Collections?","a":"<p>Bundles of modules and roles, versus standalone roles.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-galaxy collection install</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0361","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"Troubleshoot a failed task?","a":"<p>Use <code>-v</code> and <code>--check</code>, add debug tasks, and test connectivity with <code>ping</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0362","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"What do you need so Ansible's Docker modules work on a host?","a":"<p>The Docker collection (<code>community.docker</code>) installed on the control node, and the Docker SDK for Python (the <code>docker</code> package) installed for the Python interpreter that Ansible uses on the target host. The Docker engine itself must also run there.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0363","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"In Ansible, why write <code>community.docker.docker_container</code> instead of <code>docker_container</code>?","a":"<p>Since Ansible 2.10 most non-core modules live in collections, and the fully qualified name states which collection provides it, so it resolves to the intended module. The short name still works through redirects in many cases, but FQCN is the recommended form.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- docker_container:\n    name: nginx\n    image: nginx:latest\n    state: started</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0364","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"Why is <code>image: nginx:latest</code> a bad practice in an Ansible Docker task?","a":"<p><code>latest</code> is a moving tag, so two runs can deploy different versions, and the task cannot be reproduced or rolled back easily. Pin an explicit version tag or digest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0365","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"How do you run an Ansible playbook from a Jenkins pipeline, and how do you keep secrets safe?","a":"<p>Use the Jenkins Ansible plugin step shown, or call <code>ansible-playbook</code> in a shell step on an agent that has Ansible installed. Keep SSH keys and the vault password in Jenkins credentials (bound only for the stage), and never echo them. The plugin's <code>credentialsId</code> and <code>vaultCredentialsId</code> options pass them to the run.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Groovy</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansiblePlaybook playbook: &#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;, inventory: &#x27;hosts&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0366","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"What is a collection in Ansible, and how does it differ from a role?","a":"<p>A collection is a distribution format for Ansible content, with a namespace and a name, such as <code>community.docker</code>. It can bundle modules, plugins, roles and playbooks and is versioned and installed with <code>ansible-galaxy collection install</code>. A role is a single unit of tasks, and roles can be shipped inside collections.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0367","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"How do you install the collections a project needs in a repeatable way?","a":"<p>List them in <code>requirements.yml</code> under <code>collections:</code> with versions and run <code>ansible-galaxy collection install -r requirements.yml</code>. Install them in the CI job so every run uses the same versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0368","card":"q-ansible-055-docker-jenkins-and-collections","sec":"ansible-s056","q":"A task fails in a playbook. How do you troubleshoot it in Ansible?","a":"<p>Read the error and rerun the task with <code>-vvv</code> to see the connection and module arguments. Check connectivity with <code>ansible host -m ping</code>, add <code>debug</code> tasks to print variables, use <code>--check --diff</code> to preview, and run with <code>--step</code> or <code>--start-at-task</code> to continue from the failing task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0369","card":"q-ansible-056-deploy-nginx-on-multiple-hosts","sec":"ansible-s057","q":"In this Ansible play, when does the handler <code>Restart Nginx</code> run?","a":"<p>Never. No task has <code>notify: Restart Nginx</code>, so the handler is not queued. A handler runs only when a task that changed something notifies it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>  tasks:\n    - apt:\n        name: nginx\n        state: present\n    - service:\n        name: nginx\n        state: started\n  handlers:\n    - name: Restart Nginx\n      service:\n        name: nginx\n        state: restarted</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0370","card":"q-ansible-056-deploy-nginx-on-multiple-hosts","sec":"ansible-s057","q":"What would you improve in this Ansible play so it is production ready?","a":"<p>Add <code>enabled: yes</code> so nginx starts at boot. Use <code>ansible.builtin.package</code> or a condition if hosts are not all Debian-based, since <code>apt</code> fails on RedHat. Use fully qualified module names and task names. Add a config <code>template</code> task that notifies a handler, so nginx restarts only when its configuration changes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>    - apt:\n        name: nginx\n        state: present\n    - service:\n        name: nginx\n        state: started</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0371","card":"q-ansible-057-update-packages-and-reboot","sec":"ansible-s058","q":"In this Ansible play, why does the reboot task use <code>when: update.changed</code>?","a":"<p><code>register</code> captures the result of the update task, and <code>update.changed</code> is true only if packages were actually updated. The reboot then happens only when needed, not on every run.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>    - package:\n        name: &#x27;*&#x27;\n        state: latest\n      register: update\n    - reboot:\n      when: update.changed</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0372","card":"q-ansible-057-update-packages-and-reboot","sec":"ansible-s058","q":"What does the Ansible <code>reboot</code> module do that <code>command: reboot</code> does not?","a":"<p>It reboots the host, waits for it to go down, then waits until Ansible can connect again and a test command works, with configurable timeouts such as <code>reboot_timeout</code>. A plain <code>command: reboot</code> drops the connection and fails the task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0373","card":"q-ansible-057-update-packages-and-reboot","sec":"ansible-s058","q":"How would you run a patch-and-reboot playbook in Ansible without taking down all servers at once?","a":"<p>Use <code>serial</code> to process hosts in small batches, for example <code>serial: 1</code> or a percentage, and use <code>max_fail_percentage</code> to stop if hosts fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0374","card":"q-ansible-057-update-packages-and-reboot","sec":"ansible-s058","q":"Why is upgrading all packages with <code>state: latest</code> on <code>'*'</code> risky in production, and what is the alternative?","a":"<p>It can pull in major version changes and restart services unexpectedly, and runs differ over time. Safer options are to update a defined list of packages, use security-only updates, patch staging first, or use pinned versions in image builds instead of patching live hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0375","card":"q-ansible-057-update-packages-and-reboot","sec":"ansible-s058","q":"How can an Ansible playbook reboot only the Debian hosts that actually need it?","a":"<p>Stat the <code>/var/run/reboot-required</code> file with the <code>stat</code> module, register the result, and reboot <code>when: result.stat.exists</code>. This is more precise than rebooting whenever any package changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0376","card":"q-ansible-058-manage-windows-hosts","sec":"ansible-s059","q":"In Ansible, what do you need on the control node and the Windows host to manage Windows with WinRM?","a":"<p>The control node needs the <code>pywinrm</code> Python library. The Windows host needs WinRM enabled and listening (commonly port 5986 for HTTPS or 5985 for HTTP), and an account with administrator rights. Ansible modules for Windows come from the <code>ansible.windows</code> and <code>community.windows</code> collections.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Ini</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>[windows]\nwinserver ansible_connection=winrm ansible_user=Admin ansible_password=&lt;password&gt;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0377","card":"q-ansible-058-manage-windows-hosts","sec":"ansible-s059","q":"Why is it a problem to write <code>ansible_password</code> in plain text in an Ansible inventory, and what is the alternative?","a":"<p>Anyone with repository access can read it. Put the password in a vault-encrypted <code>group_vars</code> file and reference the variable, or use a credential store such as AWX credentials, and keep the inventory free of secrets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0378","card":"q-ansible-058-manage-windows-hosts","sec":"ansible-s059","q":"In Ansible, why do you use <code>win_package</code> or <code>ansible.windows.win_*</code> modules instead of <code>package</code> or <code>command</code> for Windows?","a":"<p>Standard Linux modules such as <code>apt</code>, <code>copy</code> and <code>service</code> rely on Linux tools and Python on the target. Windows hosts run PowerShell, so there is a parallel set of <code>win_*</code> modules (<code>win_copy</code>, <code>win_service</code>, <code>win_package</code>, <code>win_feature</code>) written for Windows and idempotent in the same way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0379","card":"q-ansible-058-manage-windows-hosts","sec":"ansible-s059","q":"What is the security trade-off of the WinRM transport and authentication choices in Ansible?","a":"<p>Use HTTPS (port 5986) with a valid certificate, and Kerberos or NTLM over HTTPS rather than Basic authentication, which sends credentials in a weakly protected form. Setting <code>ansible_winrm_server_cert_validation: ignore</code> is acceptable only for testing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0380","card":"q-ansible-058-manage-windows-hosts","sec":"ansible-s059","q":"How can you use SSH instead of WinRM to manage Windows with Ansible?","a":"<p>Newer Ansible releases can use the OpenSSH server on Windows Server 2019 and later, with <code>ansible_connection=ssh</code> and <code>ansible_shell_type</code> set to <code>powershell</code> or <code>cmd</code>. Support was marked experimental in some releases, so check the docs for your version. WinRM remains the most common method.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0381","card":"q-ansible-059-handle-a-playbook-failure-midway","sec":"ansible-s060","q":"A long Ansible playbook fails at task 15 of 30. What do you do?","a":"<p>Read the error (<code>-v</code> or <code>-vvv</code>), fix the cause, and rerun. If tasks are idempotent you can simply rerun the whole playbook. To save time, <code>--start-at-task</code> resumes from the named task, which must match the task name exactly.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook playbook.yml --start-at-task &quot;task_name&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0382","card":"q-ansible-059-handle-a-playbook-failure-midway","sec":"ansible-s060","q":"What is the risk of <code>--start-at-task</code> in Ansible?","a":"<p>Skipped earlier tasks do not run, so variables they set with <code>set_fact</code> or <code>register</code> are missing, and handlers they would have notified are not queued. Later tasks that rely on that can fail or behave differently. Rerunning the entire idempotent playbook is often safer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0383","card":"q-ansible-059-handle-a-playbook-failure-midway","sec":"ansible-s060","q":"How can you design an Ansible playbook so that a failure midway is easy to recover from?","a":"<p>Make every task idempotent, so a rerun is safe. Use <code>block</code> with <code>rescue</code> and <code>always</code> for cleanup or rollback on critical sections, tag the stages so you can run part of it with <code>--tags</code>, and run in batches with <code>serial</code> so a failure affects few hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0384","card":"q-ansible-059-handle-a-playbook-failure-midway","sec":"ansible-s060","q":"How do you rerun an Ansible playbook only on the hosts that failed?","a":"<p>Use <code>--limit</code> with the failed hosts. When retry files are enabled, Ansible writes the failed host names to a <code>.retry</code> file and you can pass <code>--limit @playbook.retry</code>. Retry files are off by default in recent versions, so otherwise list the hosts by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0385","card":"q-ansible-060-back-up-configs-from-servers","sec":"ansible-s061","q":"Can the Ansible <code>fetch</code> module copy a whole directory?","a":"<p>No. It fetches single files. To pull a directory, use <code>synchronize</code> with <code>mode: pull</code>, or archive it on the host with the <code>archive</code> module and fetch the archive.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0386","card":"q-ansible-060-back-up-configs-from-servers","sec":"ansible-s061","q":"How would you keep dated backups of configs from many servers in Ansible?","a":"<p>Include a date in the destination, for example the <code>ansible_date_time</code> fact or a timestamp from <code>lookup('pipe', 'date +%F')</code>, and store backups by host. For on-host backups of a file being changed, use the <code>backup: yes</code> option of <code>copy</code>, <code>template</code> or <code>lineinfile</code>, which keeps a timestamped copy before the change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0387","card":"q-ansible-060-back-up-configs-from-servers","sec":"ansible-s061","q":"When fetching files from many hosts in Ansible, what security consideration applies to the backup directory?","a":"<p>Config files often contain secrets, so the control node's backup directory must have restricted permissions and should not be committed to Git or shared. Consider encrypting backups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0388","card":"q-ansible-061-create-users-with-roles","sec":"ansible-s062","q":"In an Ansible role that creates users, why should <code>admin_user</code> come from <code>defaults</code> or inventory rather than being hardcoded?","a":"<p>Defining it in <code>defaults/main.yml</code> makes the role reusable, and each inventory or group can override the value. Hardcoding the name makes the role usable only for one user.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- user:\n    name: &quot;{{ admin_user }}&quot;\n    groups: sudo</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0389","card":"q-ansible-061-create-users-with-roles","sec":"ansible-s062","q":"How would you create several users with the same Ansible role task?","a":"<p>Define a list of users in a variable, such as <code>admin_users</code>, and loop over it with <code>loop: \"{{ admin_users }}\"</code> using <code>item</code> in <code>name</code>, or use a list of dictionaries when users need different groups or keys. This avoids copying the task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0390","card":"q-ansible-061-create-users-with-roles","sec":"ansible-s062","q":"The <code>groups: sudo</code> value in the Ansible <code>user</code> task only works on some Linux distributions. Why, and how do you handle it?","a":"<p>Debian-based systems use the <code>sudo</code> group for administrators, while RedHat-based systems use <code>wheel</code>. Set the group name from a variable chosen by <code>ansible_os_family</code>, for example in <code>vars/Debian.yml</code> and <code>vars/RedHat.yml</code> loaded with <code>include_vars</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0391","card":"q-ansible-061-create-users-with-roles","sec":"ansible-s062","q":"How do you add SSH public keys for users created by an Ansible role?","a":"<p>Use the <code>ansible.posix.authorized_key</code> module with the user name and key, usually in a loop. It adds the key idempotently to the user's <code>authorized_keys</code> without overwriting other keys unless <code>exclusive: true</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0392","card":"q-ansible-061-create-users-with-roles","sec":"ansible-s062","q":"Why should an Ansible play that changes user accounts target a specific group instead of <code>all</code>?","a":"<p>The role changes accounts and privileges, which is sensitive. Targeting a specific group limits where the change happens. The hosts line should always name the smallest set that needs the change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0393","card":"q-ansible-062-deploy-an-app-with-dependencies","sec":"ansible-s063","q":"In Ansible, how does <code>hostvars[groups['db_servers'][0]].ansible_host</code> work?","a":"<p><code>groups['db_servers']</code> is the list of hosts in that inventory group, <code>[0]</code> takes the first, and <code>hostvars[...]</code> gives the variables of that host. <code>.ansible_host</code> reads the address from the inventory. This lets the app play find the database address without hardcoding it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0394","card":"q-ansible-062-deploy-an-app-with-dependencies","sec":"ansible-s063","q":"What is the weakness of using only the first database host in Ansible, and what is a better approach for multiple databases?","a":"<p>If the first host is down or you have a cluster, the app points at one host. Use a floating address, load balancer or DNS name for the database, and set it as a variable, instead of picking a host from the list.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0395","card":"q-ansible-062-deploy-an-app-with-dependencies","sec":"ansible-s063","q":"What is needed for <code>hostvars</code> to contain a fact of the database host in Ansible?","a":"<p>Facts of that host must have been gathered earlier in the same run, or be in the fact cache. Here <code>ansible_host</code> comes from the inventory, so no facts are needed, but for a value like <code>ansible_default_ipv4</code> the database play must run first with fact gathering enabled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0396","card":"q-ansible-062-deploy-an-app-with-dependencies","sec":"ansible-s063","q":"How would you make the app play in Ansible wait until the database is actually ready?","a":"<p>Add a task such as <code>wait_for</code> on the database port (<code>host</code>, <code>port</code>, <code>timeout</code>), or use <code>community.mysql</code> modules to check a login, before deploying the application. Finishing the database play does not mean the service accepts connections yet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0397","card":"q-ansible-063-os-specific-tasks","sec":"ansible-s064","q":"What does this Ansible play do on a Debian host, and why does it not fail?","a":"<p>Both tasks are reported as <code>skipped</code>. On Debian the fact <code>ansible_distribution</code> is <code>Debian</code>, which matches neither condition, so nginx is not installed and the run still ends green. A silent skip is the pitfall. Add a fallback task or a final <code>fail</code> task for unsupported systems, or use <code>ansible_os_family</code> (<code>Debian</code>, <code>RedHat</code>) to cover whole families.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- hosts: all\n  tasks:\n    - apt:\n        name: nginx\n        state: present\n      when: ansible_distribution == &quot;Ubuntu&quot;\n    - yum:\n        name: nginx\n        state: present\n      when: ansible_distribution == &quot;CentOS&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0398","card":"q-ansible-063-os-specific-tasks","sec":"ansible-s064","q":"In Ansible, how do <code>ansible_distribution</code> and <code>ansible_os_family</code> differ, and which one should a cross-distro task test?","a":"<p><code>ansible_distribution</code> is the specific name (<code>Ubuntu</code>, <code>Debian</code>, <code>CentOS</code>, <code>RedHat</code>, <code>Amazon</code>). <code>ansible_os_family</code> groups them (<code>Debian</code> covers Ubuntu and Debian, <code>RedHat</code> covers CentOS, RHEL, Rocky, Amazon Linux). Test the family when the behaviour is shared, such as the package manager, and the distribution only when versions or names really differ.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0399","card":"q-ansible-063-os-specific-tasks","sec":"ansible-s064","q":"In Ansible, what are three ways to run OS-specific tasks other than a <code>when</code> on every task?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Use the generic <code>ansible.builtin.package</code> module when only the package name is the same on every OS.</li>\n  <li>Load per-OS variables with <code>include_vars</code> or <code>vars_files</code> using <code>{{ ansible_os_family }}.yml</code>, so the task stays one line and the variables hold the differences (for example <code>httpd</code> versus <code>apache2</code>).</li>\n  <li>Put hosts into groups per OS (inventory or the <code>group_by</code> module) and target those groups in separate plays or <code>group_vars</code>.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0400","card":"q-ansible-064-multi-tier-architecture","sec":"ansible-s065","q":"In Ansible, why does a multi-tier deployment use one play per tier instead of one play for all hosts?","a":"<p>Each play has its own <code>hosts:</code> line, so each tier gets its own roles, privilege settings, variables, <code>serial</code> and failure handling. One play with all hosts would need <code>when</code> conditions on every task. Plays also run in file order, which lets you control the order of tiers.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- hosts: load_balancers\n  roles:\n    - haproxy\n- hosts: app_servers\n  roles:\n    - tomcat\n- hosts: db_servers\n  roles:\n    - mysql</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0401","card":"q-ansible-064-multi-tier-architecture","sec":"ansible-s065","q":"In what order should you configure the tiers of a load balancer, application and database stack with Ansible, and why?","a":"<p>Bottom up: database first, then the application, then the load balancer. The app needs a reachable database when it starts, and the load balancer should only send traffic once its backends are ready. Plays run in file order, so list them in that order. The order for a teardown is the reverse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0402","card":"q-ansible-064-multi-tier-architecture","sec":"ansible-s065","q":"How do you roll out a new version across an Ansible multi-tier setup without dropping traffic?","a":"<p>Use <code>serial</code> on the app play so only a few servers change at a time. Before each batch, take the servers out of the load balancer with a task using <code>delegate_to</code> on the balancer host, upgrade, check health (for example with <code>uri</code> and <code>until</code>/<code>retries</code>), then add them back. Put the removal in <code>pre_tasks</code> and the re-add in <code>post_tasks</code>. Add <code>max_fail_percentage</code> to stop the rollout if too many servers fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0403","card":"q-ansible-064-multi-tier-architecture","sec":"ansible-s065","q":"In Ansible, one host belongs to both <code>app_servers</code> and <code>db_servers</code> in the inventory. What happens when two plays target those groups?","a":"<p>The host runs both plays, one after the other, in file order. Groups are just labels, so a host can sit in many groups. Variables from every group it belongs to are merged, and conflicts follow the normal precedence rules, so watch for the same variable name in two groups.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0404","card":"q-ansible-065-roll-back-a-failed-deployment","sec":"ansible-s066","q":"In this Ansible play, when does the <code>rescue</code> section run and what does it do?","a":"<p><code>rescue</code> runs only when a task in the <code>block</code> fails on that host. It restores the backup that the first task made, so the host returns to the old version. If nothing in the block fails, <code>rescue</code> is skipped. <code>remote_src: yes</code> means the source path is on the managed host, not on the control node.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- hosts: app_servers\n  tasks:\n    - block:\n        - copy:\n            src: /opt/app\n            dest: /opt/app_backup\n            remote_src: yes\n        - copy:\n            src: new_app.tar.gz\n            dest: /opt/app.tar.gz\n          register: deploy\n      rescue:\n        - copy:\n            src: /opt/app_backup\n            dest: /opt/app\n            remote_src: yes</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0405","card":"q-ansible-065-roll-back-a-failed-deployment","sec":"ansible-s066","q":"What are the weaknesses of this Ansible <code>block</code>/<code>rescue</code> rollback, whose deploy step is only a plain <code>copy</code> of an archive?","a":"<p><code>copy</code> only uploads the archive. It does not unpack it, so nothing new is deployed, and a successful copy proves nothing about the application. Use <code>unarchive</code>, then add a health check inside the block (for example <code>uri</code> against the app, with <code>retries</code> and <code>until</code>). Only a failing check triggers <code>rescue</code>, which makes the rollback useful. Also restart the service after the restore.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- block:\n    - copy:\n        src: new_app.tar.gz\n        dest: /opt/app.tar.gz\n  rescue:\n    - copy:\n        src: /opt/app_backup\n        dest: /opt/app\n        remote_src: yes</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0406","card":"q-ansible-065-roll-back-a-failed-deployment","sec":"ansible-s066","q":"In Ansible, a task in <code>rescue</code> runs successfully after a failure in <code>block</code>. Is the host still marked as failed, and how do you make the pipeline fail anyway?","a":"<p>The host is not marked failed. The recap shows <code>rescued=1</code>, and the play continues. If a rollback must still fail the CI job, end the <code>rescue</code> with an <code>ansible.builtin.fail</code> task. Use <code>always</code> for steps that must run in both cases, such as cleanup or notification.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0407","card":"q-ansible-065-roll-back-a-failed-deployment","sec":"ansible-s066","q":"What deployment layout makes a rollback simpler than copying a backup directory in Ansible?","a":"<p>Keep each release in its own directory (<code>releases/&lt;version&gt;</code>) and point a <code>current</code> symlink at the active one, using the <code>file</code> module with <code>state: link</code>. Deploying switches the link, and a rollback switches it back, which is atomic and instant. Keep the last few releases and delete older ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0408","card":"q-ansible-066-two-tasks-modify-the-same-file-delegatio","sec":"ansible-s067","q":"Two tasks modify the same file?","a":"<p>The last task wins unless you use <code>lineinfile</code> or <code>blockinfile</code> to append. Design for idempotency.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0409","card":"q-ansible-066-two-tasks-modify-the-same-file-delegatio","sec":"ansible-s067","q":"Run a task once, on a delegated host?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- command: echo &quot;once&quot;\n  delegate_to: localhost\n  run_once: true</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0410","card":"q-ansible-066-two-tasks-modify-the-same-file-delegatio","sec":"ansible-s067","q":"In Ansible, two roles each use <code>template</code> to write the same config file. What goes wrong and how do you design around it?","a":"<p>The last task to run overwrites the other, so the file flips on every run, which breaks idempotency (<code>changed</code> every time). Give each file a single owner. If several parties contribute settings, let each add a fragment (a <code>conf.d</code> style drop-in directory, or the <code>assemble</code> module), or merge their variables into one template. For a few lines in a shared file, use <code>lineinfile</code> or <code>blockinfile</code> with a unique marker.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0411","card":"q-ansible-066-two-tasks-modify-the-same-file-delegatio","sec":"ansible-s067","q":"In Ansible, a play runs with <code>serial: 2</code> over 6 hosts and contains a <code>run_once: true</code> task. How many times does that task run?","a":"<p>Three times, once per batch. <code>run_once</code> runs the task on the first host of each batch, not once for the whole play. Without <code>serial</code>, it runs once in total.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0412","card":"q-ansible-066-two-tasks-modify-the-same-file-delegatio","sec":"ansible-s067","q":"In Ansible, a <code>run_once</code> task uses <code>register</code>. Do the other hosts see the result?","a":"<p>Yes. The result of a <code>run_once</code> task is applied to all hosts in the play (in the batch), so later tasks can read the registered variable on every host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0413","card":"q-ansible-067-network-devices","sec":"ansible-s068","q":"Can Ansible manage network devices?","a":"<p>Yes, with modules like <code>ios_config</code>. It uses <code>network_cli</code> over SSH or NETCONF.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- cisco.ios.ios_config:\n    lines:\n      - hostname NEW_ROUTER</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0414","card":"q-ansible-067-network-devices","sec":"ansible-s068","q":"In Ansible, why can network device modules such as <code>ios_config</code> run against a switch that has no Python?","a":"<p>Network modules run on the control node, not on the device. They connect through a connection plugin (<code>ansible.netcommon.network_cli</code> over SSH, <code>netconf</code>, or <code>httpapi</code>) and send CLI or API commands. The device only needs to accept that connection, so no agent or Python is needed there.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- cisco.ios.ios_config:\n    lines:\n      - hostname NEW_ROUTER</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0415","card":"q-ansible-067-network-devices","sec":"ansible-s068","q":"Which inventory variables does Ansible need for a Cisco IOS device, and why?","a":"<p><code>ansible_connection: ansible.netcommon.network_cli</code> selects the connection plugin, <code>ansible_network_os: cisco.ios.ios</code> tells it which platform dialect and prompt handling to use, and <code>ansible_user</code>/<code>ansible_password</code> (or an SSH key) give the login. Add <code>ansible_become: yes</code> with <code>ansible_become_method: enable</code> to enter privileged mode.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0416","card":"q-ansible-067-network-devices","sec":"ansible-s068","q":"In Ansible, how do <code>ios_command</code> and <code>ios_config</code> differ, and which one is idempotent?","a":"<p><code>ios_command</code> sends commands, usually <code>show</code> commands, and returns their output. It reports <code>changed</code> only according to your rules, so treat it as read-only. <code>ios_config</code> pushes configuration lines. It compares them with the running configuration and sends only the missing ones, so it is idempotent. Use <code>save_when</code> to write the running config to startup when something changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0417","card":"q-ansible-067-network-devices","sec":"ansible-s068","q":"Why use a vendor collection such as <code>cisco.ios</code> instead of sending raw CLI with <code>command</code> in Ansible?","a":"<p>The collection modules understand the device's state, so they are idempotent, support check mode and diff, and handle prompts and parent-child configuration blocks. Raw CLI would run every time and always report changed. Collections are also versioned separately from the core, so you can update network support on its own (Ansible 2.10 and later).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0418","card":"q-ansible-068-limitations-and-secrets","sec":"ansible-s069","q":"Limitations of Ansible?","a":"<p>It is slower (SSH), has no real-time operation, is less expressive than Ruby/DSL tools, and has weaker network support.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0419","card":"q-ansible-068-limitations-and-secrets","sec":"ansible-s069","q":"Secrets beyond Vault?","a":"<p>Use HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or lookups:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- debug:\n    msg: &quot;{{ lookup(&#x27;hashi_vault&#x27;, &#x27;secret/my-key&#x27;) }}&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0420","card":"q-ansible-068-limitations-and-secrets","sec":"ansible-s069","q":"In Ansible, why can configuration drift appear between runs, and how do you reduce it?","a":"<p>Ansible is push based, so it only changes hosts when someone runs it. A manual edit on a server stays until the next run. Reduce drift by scheduling regular runs (cron, a CI schedule, or AWX schedules), using <code>ansible-pull</code> on the hosts, running with <code>--check --diff</code> to detect drift, and limiting manual access.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0421","card":"q-ansible-068-limitations-and-secrets","sec":"ansible-s069","q":"In Ansible, how does a <code>lookup</code> such as <code>lookup('hashi_vault', ...)</code> differ from reading a secret from a vault-encrypted variable file?","a":"<p>A lookup fetches the value at run time from an external system, on the control node, so the secret is not stored in the repository, and rotation needs no re-encryption. A vault file stores an encrypted secret in Git. The lookup needs network access, credentials for the secrets system, and the plugin's dependencies on the control node (for HashiCorp Vault, the <code>community.hashi_vault</code> collection and the <code>hvac</code> library).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0422","card":"q-ansible-068-limitations-and-secrets","sec":"ansible-s069","q":"Why does Ansible suit short, ordered jobs but not fit tasks needing real-time reaction?","a":"<p>It runs tasks in sequence over SSH, so each run has connection and module-transfer overhead and starts only when triggered. It cannot watch a host and react in seconds. Use a monitoring and event system for that (for example event-driven automation that triggers playbooks), or an agent-based tool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0423","card":"q-ansible-069-changed-vs-ok-races-unreachable-hosts","sec":"ansible-s070","q":"Difference between <code>changed</code> and <code>ok</code>?","a":"<p><code>changed</code> means the system was modified. <code>ok</code> means no change was needed.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0424","card":"q-ansible-069-changed-vs-ok-races-unreachable-hosts","sec":"ansible-s070","q":"Handle race conditions?","a":"<p>Execution is sequential per host; use <code>serial</code> or <code>run_once</code> for shared resources.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0425","card":"q-ansible-069-changed-vs-ok-races-unreachable-hosts","sec":"ansible-s070","q":"What happens with an unreachable host in the inventory?","a":"<p>It is marked <code>UNREACHABLE</code> and the playbook continues, unless <code>any_errors_fatal</code> is set.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0426","card":"q-ansible-069-changed-vs-ok-races-unreachable-hosts","sec":"ansible-s070","q":"In Ansible, what is the difference between <code>any_errors_fatal</code> and <code>max_fail_percentage</code>?","a":"<p><code>any_errors_fatal: true</code> stops the whole play for all hosts as soon as one host fails. <code>max_fail_percentage</code> lets the play continue until the share of failed hosts in the current batch exceeds the limit, then aborts. Use the first for all-or-nothing changes, the second for rolling updates together with <code>serial</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0427","card":"q-ansible-069-changed-vs-ok-races-unreachable-hosts","sec":"ansible-s070","q":"In Ansible, how can you let a play carry on although some hosts cannot be reached?","a":"<p>Unreachable hosts are dropped from the rest of the play by default, and the others continue. To keep going within a task, use <code>ignore_unreachable: true</code> (Ansible 2.7+) on the task or block. To treat any unreachable host as a stop condition, use <code>any_errors_fatal</code>. Always check the recap for the unreachable count.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0428","card":"q-ansible-069-changed-vs-ok-races-unreachable-hosts","sec":"ansible-s070","q":"Two people run the same Ansible playbook against the same hosts at the same time. What can go wrong and how do you prevent it?","a":"<p>Both runs may edit the same files or restart services at the same time, giving partial or conflicting states. Prevent it with a locking approach: one controller (AWX or a CI job with a <code>concurrency</code> group so only one run goes at a time), or a lock file on the target taken with <code>flock</code> or a task that fails if the lock exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0429","card":"q-ansible-070-scale-and-ci-cd-failures","sec":"ansible-s071","q":"Scale Ansible for thousands of hosts?","a":"<p>More forks, dynamic inventory, Tower, fact caching, and optimized playbooks.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0430","card":"q-ansible-070-scale-and-ci-cd-failures","sec":"ansible-s071","q":"A playbook fails in CI/CD but works locally?","a":"<p>Check version mismatch, credentials, inventory, and network differences. Standardize the environment and test with <code>-v</code>.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0431","card":"q-ansible-070-scale-and-ci-cd-failures","sec":"ansible-s071","q":"A playbook passes on your laptop and fails in a CI pipeline in Ansible. What are the most common causes?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Different <code>ansible-core</code> or collection versions. Pin them in <code>requirements.txt</code> and <code>requirements.yml</code>.</li>\n  <li>SSH host key checking with an empty <code>known_hosts</code> on the runner.</li>\n  <li>Missing SSH key, vault password or environment variable, because CI is non-interactive.</li>\n  <li>No network route from the runner to the hosts.</li>\n  <li>Prompts that cannot be answered (sudo password, host key confirmation). Run with <code>-vvv</code> to see which one applies.</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0432","card":"q-ansible-070-scale-and-ci-cd-failures","sec":"ansible-s071","q":"In CI, Ansible fails with \"Host key verification failed\". Is disabling host key checking the right fix?","a":"<p>No. Disabling it removes protection against man-in-the-middle attacks. Add the hosts' keys to <code>known_hosts</code> on the runner, for example with <code>ssh-keyscan</code> from a trusted network or a stored fingerprint, or bake them into the runner image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0433","card":"q-ansible-070-scale-and-ci-cd-failures","sec":"ansible-s071","q":"In Ansible, how do <code>async</code> and <code>poll</code> help with a long-running task?","a":"<p><code>async: &lt;seconds&gt;</code> starts the task in the background with that time limit. <code>poll: &lt;seconds&gt;</code> makes Ansible check at that interval; with <code>poll: 0</code> it moves on immediately, and you check later with the <code>async_status</code> module. This avoids SSH timeouts on slow tasks and lets one task run on many hosts in parallel.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0434","card":"q-ansible-070-scale-and-ci-cd-failures","sec":"ansible-s071","q":"How does AWX job slicing help scale Ansible to thousands of hosts?","a":"<p>A job template with a slice count splits the inventory into several smaller jobs that run in parallel on different execution capacity. Each job handles part of the hosts, so total time drops without raising <code>forks</code> on a single controller.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0435","card":"q-ansible-071-ad-hoc-pings-copy-and-uptime","sec":"ansible-s072","q":"Ad-hoc command to ping all hosts?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible all -m ping</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0436","card":"q-ansible-071-ad-hoc-pings-copy-and-uptime","sec":"ansible-s072","q":"Copy <code>config.conf</code> to <code>/etc/app/</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible all -m copy -a &quot;src=config.conf dest=/etc/app/config.conf owner=root mode=0644&quot; --become</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0437","card":"q-ansible-071-ad-hoc-pings-copy-and-uptime","sec":"ansible-s072","q":"Check uptime of <code>dbservers</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible dbservers -m command -a &quot;uptime&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0438","card":"q-ansible-071-ad-hoc-pings-copy-and-uptime","sec":"ansible-s072","q":"Update the package cache on Ubuntu?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible all -m apt -a &quot;update_cache=yes&quot; --become -l ubuntu_hosts</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0439","card":"q-ansible-071-ad-hoc-pings-copy-and-uptime","sec":"ansible-s072","q":"In Ansible, what is the difference between <code>ansible all -m ping</code> and <code>ansible all -m command -a \"uptime\"</code> as a check of a host?","a":"<p><code>ping</code> tests the whole path: SSH login and a working Python interpreter on the host. It does not send ICMP. <code>command -a \"uptime\"</code> also tests the login and runs an actual program, and it returns data (load, uptime). Use <code>ping</code> for connectivity and <code>command</code> for a quick status.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0440","card":"q-ansible-071-ad-hoc-pings-copy-and-uptime","sec":"ansible-s072","q":"In Ansible, why does the ad-hoc <code>copy</code> into <code>/etc/app/</code> need <code>--become</code>?","a":"<p>The SSH user is normally unprivileged, and <code>/etc</code> is writable only by root. <code>--become</code> (or <code>-b</code>) escalates with <code>sudo</code> for that module run. Without it the task fails with a permission error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0441","card":"q-ansible-071-ad-hoc-pings-copy-and-uptime","sec":"ansible-s072","q":"In Ansible, how do you target hosts of <code>webservers</code> that are also in <code>production</code> but not in <code>staging</code> in an ad-hoc command?","a":"<p>Use a host pattern: <code>ansible 'webservers:&amp;production:!staging' -m ping</code>. <code>:</code> means union, <code>:&amp;</code> intersection, and <code>:!</code> exclusion. Quote the pattern so the shell does not interpret <code>!</code> and <code>&amp;</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0442","card":"q-ansible-071-ad-hoc-pings-copy-and-uptime","sec":"ansible-s072","q":"In Ansible, running the same ad-hoc <code>copy</code> twice with the same file prints a different result the second time. What is it and why?","a":"<p>The first run shows <code>CHANGED</code>, because the file was written. The second run shows <code>SUCCESS</code> with <code>\"changed\": false</code>, because the module compares checksum, owner and mode and finds nothing to do. This is idempotency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0443","card":"q-ansible-072-install-httpd-and-manage-nginx","sec":"ansible-s073","q":"Playbook to install <code>httpd</code> on <code>webservers</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install httpd\n  hosts: webservers\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install httpd\n    ansible.builtin.yum: { name: httpd, state: present }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook install_httpd.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0444","card":"q-ansible-072-install-httpd-and-manage-nginx","sec":"ansible-s073","q":"Playbook to start and enable <code>nginx</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Manage nginx\n  hosts: all\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install nginx\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: nginx, state: present }\n  - name: Start nginx\n    ansible.builtin.service: { name: nginx, state: started, enabled: yes }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook nginx_service.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0445","card":"q-ansible-072-install-httpd-and-manage-nginx","sec":"ansible-s073","q":"On a modern RHEL-family host, what does the Ansible <code>yum</code> module use under the hood?","a":"<p>On systems with DNF (RHEL 8 and later, Fedora), the <code>yum</code> module works through the <code>dnf</code> module, because <code>yum</code> is just a compatibility name there. Use <code>ansible.builtin.dnf</code> explicitly for new playbooks that only target such systems.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0446","card":"q-ansible-072-install-httpd-and-manage-nginx","sec":"ansible-s073","q":"What does this Ansible one-line style mean, and is it equal to the multi-line form?","a":"<p>It is a YAML flow mapping, equal to listing <code>name: httpd</code> and <code>state: present</code> on separate indented lines. Both parse to the same data. The multi-line form is easier to read and diff when there are many options.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible.builtin.yum: { name: httpd, state: present }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0447","card":"q-ansible-072-install-httpd-and-manage-nginx","sec":"ansible-s073","q":"Why does a service task that sets only <code>state: started</code> not survive a reboot in Ansible?","a":"<p><code>state: started</code> starts the service now. Without <code>enabled: yes</code>, nothing registers it to start at boot, so after a reboot it stays down. Set both when you want it running now and on every boot.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0448","card":"q-ansible-073-rolling-restarts-and-reboots","sec":"ansible-s074","q":"Restart <code>webservers</code> sequentially?","a":"<p><code>serial: 1</code> processes one host at a time.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Restart webservers\n  hosts: webservers\n  become: yes\n  serial: 1\n  tasks:\n  - name: Restart\n    ansible.builtin.reboot:</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook reboot_webservers.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0449","card":"q-ansible-073-rolling-restarts-and-reboots","sec":"ansible-s074","q":"Reboot hosts and wait for them to return?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible all -m reboot -a &quot;reboot_timeout=300&quot; --become</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0450","card":"q-ansible-073-rolling-restarts-and-reboots","sec":"ansible-s074","q":"In Ansible, which forms does <code>serial</code> accept, and how do you do a canary rollout with it?","a":"<p>It accepts a number (<code>serial: 2</code>), a percentage (<code>serial: \"25%\"</code>), or a list. A list sets batch sizes in order: <code>serial: [1, \"25%\", \"100%\"]</code> updates one host first as a canary, then a quarter, then all the rest. If the canary fails, the later batches do not run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0451","card":"q-ansible-073-rolling-restarts-and-reboots","sec":"ansible-s074","q":"Does <code>serial: 1</code> with the <code>reboot</code> module keep users from seeing errors in Ansible?","a":"<p>Not by itself. <code>serial: 1</code> only guarantees that one server is down at a time. Requests can still reach the rebooting host until a health check marks it down, so errors are limited rather than prevented.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0452","card":"q-ansible-073-rolling-restarts-and-reboots","sec":"ansible-s074","q":"In Ansible, what does <code>reboot_timeout</code> of the <code>reboot</code> module control, and what else can you tune?","a":"<p><code>reboot_timeout</code> is the maximum time in seconds Ansible waits for the host to come back and answer a test command. Related options are <code>pre_reboot_delay</code>, <code>post_reboot_delay</code> (extra wait after the host returns), and <code>test_command</code> (a command that must succeed before the module returns).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0453","card":"q-ansible-073-rolling-restarts-and-reboots","sec":"ansible-s074","q":"Why is a play with <code>serial: 1</code> slower than the same play without it in Ansible?","a":"<p>Each batch runs all tasks to the end before the next starts, so hosts are processed one after another instead of in parallel. This trades speed for safety, so use it only where availability matters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0454","card":"q-ansible-074-users-facts-and-shell-output","sec":"ansible-s075","q":"Playbook for user <code>devops</code> with UID 2000?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Create devops user\n  hosts: all\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Create user\n    ansible.builtin.user: { name: devops, uid: 2000, state: present }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook create_user.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0455","card":"q-ansible-074-users-facts-and-shell-output","sec":"ansible-s075","q":"Set a fact based on the OS distribution?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Set OS fact\n  hosts: all\n  tasks:\n  - name: Set fact\n    ansible.builtin.set_fact: { os_dist: &quot;{{ ansible_facts[&#x27;distribution&#x27;] }}&quot; }\n  - name: Debug\n    ansible.builtin.debug: { var: os_dist }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0456","card":"q-ansible-074-users-facts-and-shell-output","sec":"ansible-s075","q":"Run a shell command and register the output?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Run shell\n  hosts: all\n  tasks:\n  - name: Get disk space\n    ansible.builtin.shell: &quot;df -h /&quot;\n    register: disk_space\n  - name: Debug\n    ansible.builtin.debug: { var: disk_space.stdout }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook shell_command.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0457","card":"q-ansible-074-users-facts-and-shell-output","sec":"ansible-s075","q":"What happens in Ansible when you create a user with <code>uid: 2000</code> and that UID already belongs to another account?","a":"<p>The task fails, because the system refuses a duplicate UID unless <code>non_unique: yes</code> is set. A fixed UID is useful for matching file ownership across hosts or shared storage. Pick values outside the range used by system and local users.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- ansible.builtin.user: { name: devops, uid: 2000, state: present }</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0458","card":"q-ansible-074-users-facts-and-shell-output","sec":"ansible-s075","q":"In Ansible, a <code>shell</code> task that only runs <code>df -h /</code> reports <code>changed</code> on every run. Why, and how do you make it report truthfully?","a":"<p><code>command</code> and <code>shell</code> cannot tell whether the command changed anything, so they always report <code>changed</code>. For a read-only command, add <code>changed_when: false</code>. Where a module exists for the job, prefer it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- ansible.builtin.shell: &quot;df -h /&quot;\n  register: disk_space</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0459","card":"q-ansible-074-users-facts-and-shell-output","sec":"ansible-s075","q":"What is the difference between <code>disk_space.stdout</code> and <code>disk_space.stdout_lines</code> of a registered task result in Ansible?","a":"<p><code>stdout</code> is the whole output as one string. <code>stdout_lines</code> is the same output split on newlines into a list, which is easier to loop over or index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0460","card":"q-ansible-075-templates-stat-and-handlers","sec":"ansible-s076","q":"Deploy the template <code>app.conf.j2</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Deploy template\n  hosts: all\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Deploy\n    ansible.builtin.template: { src: app.conf.j2, dest: /etc/app.conf, owner: root, mode: &quot;0644&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook deploy_template.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0461","card":"q-ansible-075-templates-stat-and-handlers","sec":"ansible-s076","q":"Check if a file exists and print a debug message?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Check file\n  hosts: all\n  tasks:\n  - name: Check file\n    ansible.builtin.stat: { path: /etc/app.conf }\n    register: file_status\n  - name: Debug\n    ansible.builtin.debug: { msg: &quot;File exists&quot; }\n    when: file_status.stat.exists</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook check_file.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0462","card":"q-ansible-075-templates-stat-and-handlers","sec":"ansible-s076","q":"Playbook with a handler to restart <code>httpd</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Manage httpd\n  hosts: webservers\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Deploy config\n    ansible.builtin.copy: { src: httpd.conf, dest: /etc/httpd/conf/httpd.conf, owner: root }\n    notify: Restart httpd\n  handlers:\n  - name: Restart httpd\n    ansible.builtin.service: { name: httpd, state: restarted }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook httpd_config.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0463","card":"q-ansible-075-templates-stat-and-handlers","sec":"ansible-s076","q":"In Ansible, which attributes of the <code>stat</code> result do you most often use besides <code>exists</code>?","a":"<p><code>isdir</code> and <code>isreg</code> (type checks), <code>mode</code>, <code>pw_name</code> (owner), <code>size</code>, <code>mtime</code>, and <code>checksum</code> (with <code>get_checksum</code>). A common use is checking the state before a destructive or conditional step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0464","card":"q-ansible-075-templates-stat-and-handlers","sec":"ansible-s076","q":"Why does the <code>when</code> in this Ansible play use <code>file_status.stat.exists</code> and not <code>file_status.exists</code>?","a":"<p>The registered variable holds the module's whole result. The <code>stat</code> module puts its data under a key called <code>stat</code>, so the flag is at <code>file_status.stat.exists</code>. Print the variable with <code>debug</code> when you are unsure of its structure.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- ansible.builtin.stat: { path: /etc/app.conf }\n  register: file_status\n- ansible.builtin.debug: { msg: &quot;File exists&quot; }\n  when: file_status.stat.exists</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0465","card":"q-ansible-075-templates-stat-and-handlers","sec":"ansible-s076","q":"Why do you not need a <code>stat</code> check before creating a file with the <code>file</code> or <code>copy</code> module in Ansible?","a":"<p>These modules are idempotent: they compare current and wanted state and change only when different. Checking first adds a task and a race window. Use <code>stat</code> when the decision depends on something the module does not handle, such as skipping a long step when a marker file exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0466","card":"q-ansible-075-templates-stat-and-handlers","sec":"ansible-s076","q":"What happens in Ansible if the name in <code>notify</code> does not match any handler name or <code>listen</code> topic exactly?","a":"<p>The play fails with an error that the handler was not found. Names are case sensitive, so a typo breaks the notification instead of silently doing nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0467","card":"q-ansible-076-loops-conditionals-and-cron","sec":"ansible-s077","q":"Loop over a package list?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install packages\n  hosts: all\n  become: yes\n  vars: { packages: [nginx, git, curl] }\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: &quot;{{ item }}&quot;, state: present }\n    loop: &quot;{{ packages }}&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook install_packages.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0468","card":"q-ansible-076-loops-conditionals-and-cron","sec":"ansible-s077","q":"Run a task only on Debian?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Debian task\n  hosts: all\n  tasks:\n  - name: Update cache\n    ansible.builtin.apt: { update_cache: yes }\n    when: ansible_os_family == &quot;Debian&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook debian_task.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0469","card":"q-ansible-076-loops-conditionals-and-cron","sec":"ansible-s077","q":"Playbook for a daily cron job at midnight?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Setup cron\n  hosts: all\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Create cron\n    ansible.builtin.cron: { name: &quot;Daily backup&quot;, minute: &quot;0&quot;, hour: &quot;0&quot;, job: &quot;/usr/local/bin/backup.sh&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook cron_backup.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0470","card":"q-ansible-076-loops-conditionals-and-cron","sec":"ansible-s077","q":"In Ansible, how do you loop over a list of dictionaries and use their fields?","a":"<p>Use <code>loop</code> with the list, and read fields from <code>item</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- ansible.builtin.user:\n    name: &quot;{{ item.name }}&quot;\n    groups: &quot;{{ item.groups }}&quot;\n  loop:\n    - { name: alice, groups: dev }\n    - { name: bob, groups: ops }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0471","card":"q-ansible-076-loops-conditionals-and-cron","sec":"ansible-s077","q":"In Ansible, what does <code>loop_control</code> with <code>label</code> or <code>loop_var</code> do?","a":"<p><code>label</code> shortens what is printed for each item, so long dictionaries do not flood the output (and secret fields can be hidden). <code>loop_var</code> renames <code>item</code>, which prevents a clash when a looped task file includes another loop.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0472","card":"q-ansible-076-loops-conditionals-and-cron","sec":"ansible-s077","q":"In Ansible, what happens if the <code>cron</code> module task has no <code>name</code>?","a":"<p>With <code>state: present</code> and no <code>name</code>, Ansible cannot recognise its own entry, so it adds a new crontab line on every run. Always set a unique <code>name</code>. The module stores it as a comment that it uses to find the job again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0473","card":"q-ansible-076-loops-conditionals-and-cron","sec":"ansible-s077","q":"In Ansible, a <code>cron</code> task sets only <code>hour: \"0\"</code> and <code>job</code>. When does the job run?","a":"<p>Every minute of hour 0, because unset time fields default to <code>*</code>. Set <code>minute: \"0\"</code> as well for exactly one run at midnight.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0474","card":"q-ansible-076-loops-conditionals-and-cron","sec":"ansible-s077","q":"How do you make an Ansible task retry until a condition holds, for example until a service answers?","a":"<p>Use <code>until</code> with <code>retries</code> and <code>delay</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- ansible.builtin.uri:\n    url: http://localhost:8080/health\n  register: result\n  until: result.status == 200\n  retries: 10\n  delay: 5</code></pre></div>\n<p>The task repeats up to <code>retries</code> times, waiting <code>delay</code> seconds between attempts, and fails if the condition never becomes true.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0475","card":"q-ansible-077-gather-facts-delegate-and-remove-directo","sec":"ansible-s078","q":"Gather facts and save them to a file?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible all -m setup -a &quot;gather_subset=all&quot; --tree /tmp/facts</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0476","card":"q-ansible-077-gather-facts-delegate-and-remove-directo","sec":"ansible-s078","q":"Delegate a task to localhost?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Delegate to localhost\n  hosts: all\n  tasks:\n  - name: Run locally\n    ansible.builtin.command: &quot;echo {{ inventory_hostname }} &gt;&gt; /tmp/host_list&quot;\n    delegate_to: localhost</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook delegate_task.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0477","card":"q-ansible-077-gather-facts-delegate-and-remove-directo","sec":"ansible-s078","q":"Playbook to remove <code>/tmp/old_data</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Remove directory\n  hosts: all\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Remove\n    ansible.builtin.file: { path: /tmp/old_data, state: absent }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook remove_dir.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0478","card":"q-ansible-077-gather-facts-delegate-and-remove-directo","sec":"ansible-s078","q":"What does the <code>--tree</code> option do when you run <code>ansible all -m setup --tree /tmp/facts</code>?","a":"<p>It writes the gathered facts of each host into a separate JSON file in that directory, named after the host. That gives you an offline inventory you can search or compare between runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0479","card":"q-ansible-077-gather-facts-delegate-and-remove-directo","sec":"ansible-s078","q":"Why does this delegated Ansible task not append to a file as intended?","a":"<p>The <code>command</code> module does not use a shell, so <code>&gt;&gt;</code> is passed to <code>echo</code> as a literal argument and nothing is written. Use <code>shell</code>, or better <code>lineinfile</code> with <code>create: yes</code>, which is idempotent. Because every host runs the delegated task, parallel writes to one file can also race, so use <code>throttle: 1</code> or <code>serial</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- ansible.builtin.command: &quot;echo {{ inventory_hostname }} &gt;&gt; /tmp/host_list&quot;\n  delegate_to: localhost</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0480","card":"q-ansible-077-gather-facts-delegate-and-remove-directo","sec":"ansible-s078","q":"In Ansible, what does the <code>file</code> module do with <code>state: absent</code> on a directory that does not exist, and on one that has content?","a":"<p>Missing path: nothing happens and the task reports <code>ok</code>, so it is idempotent. Existing directory: it is deleted recursively, with everything inside. Keep the path explicit and avoid templating it from a variable that could be empty.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0481","card":"q-ansible-078-tags-lineinfile-and-verbose-troubleshoot","sec":"ansible-s079","q":"Run a playbook with the <code>deploy</code> tag?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook playbook.yml --tags &quot;deploy&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0482","card":"q-ansible-078-tags-lineinfile-and-verbose-troubleshoot","sec":"ansible-s079","q":"Ensure a line in <code>/etc/hosts</code>?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Update hosts\n  hosts: all\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Add line\n    ansible.builtin.lineinfile: { path: /etc/hosts, line: &quot;192.168.1.100 app.example.com&quot; }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook update_hosts.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0483","card":"q-ansible-078-tags-lineinfile-and-verbose-troubleshoot","sec":"ansible-s079","q":"Troubleshoot a playbook with verbose output?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook playbook.yml -v</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0484","card":"q-ansible-078-tags-lineinfile-and-verbose-troubleshoot","sec":"ansible-s079","q":"In Ansible, how do you list the tags a playbook defines, and how do you run everything except one tag?","a":"<p><code>ansible-playbook site.yml --list-tags</code> shows the tags. <code>--skip-tags slow</code> runs all tasks except those tagged <code>slow</code>. <code>--tags</code> and <code>--skip-tags</code> can be combined.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0485","card":"q-ansible-078-tags-lineinfile-and-verbose-troubleshoot","sec":"ansible-s079","q":"In Ansible, running a <code>lineinfile</code> task with only <code>line:</code> and no <code>regexp</code>, you change the text of <code>line</code>. What does the next run do?","a":"<p>It adds a second line. Without <code>regexp</code>, the module only looks for an exact match of <code>line</code>, so the old line stays and the new one is appended. Set <code>regexp</code> so the module can find and replace the old line.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0486","card":"q-ansible-079-apply-a-manifest-and-verify-the-pod","sec":"ansible-s080","q":"Ansible to apply a K8s manifest and verify?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Apply K8s\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Apply\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f pod.yaml\n  - name: Verify\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl get pod nginx-pod -o jsonpath=&#x27;{.status.phase}&#x27;\n    register: pod_status\n  - name: Fail if not running\n    ansible.builtin.fail: { msg: &quot;Pod not Running&quot; }\n    when: pod_status.stdout != &quot;Running&quot;</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>pod.yaml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Pod\nmetadata: { name: nginx-pod }\nspec: { containers: [{ name: nginx, image: &quot;nginx:latest&quot; }] }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0487","card":"q-ansible-079-apply-a-manifest-and-verify-the-pod","sec":"ansible-s080","q":"Troubleshoot an Ansible-deployed pod that is not running?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>kubectl get pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl describe pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\nkubectl get pod &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt; -o yaml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0488","card":"q-ansible-079-apply-a-manifest-and-verify-the-pod","sec":"ansible-s080","q":"Why use the <code>kubernetes.core.k8s</code> module instead of <code>command: kubectl apply -f</code> in Ansible?","a":"<p>The module talks to the Kubernetes API, reports <code>changed</code> only when the object differs, supports check mode and diff, takes the definition from a file or inline YAML, and can wait for a condition. The <code>command</code> form always reports <code>changed</code> and depends on <code>kubectl</code> being installed. The module needs the <code>kubernetes</code> Python library and the <code>kubernetes.core</code> collection on the host that runs it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0489","card":"q-ansible-079-apply-a-manifest-and-verify-the-pod","sec":"ansible-s080","q":"Why do Kubernetes tasks in Ansible usually use <code>hosts: localhost</code>?","a":"<p>Kubernetes work goes through the cluster API, not through SSH to the nodes. A task on <code>localhost</code> (the control node, or the CI runner) uses a kubeconfig and calls the API, so the cluster nodes do not need to be in the inventory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0490","card":"q-ansible-079-apply-a-manifest-and-verify-the-pod","sec":"ansible-s080","q":"In Ansible, how do you wait until a pod or deployment is ready?","a":"<p>With <code>kubernetes.core.k8s</code> set <code>wait: yes</code> and <code>wait_condition</code> (for example <code>type: Ready</code>, <code>status: \"True\"</code>), plus <code>wait_timeout</code>. With <code>kubectl</code>, use <code>kubectl wait --for=condition=Ready pod/&lt;name&gt; --timeout=60s</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0491","card":"q-ansible-079-apply-a-manifest-and-verify-the-pod","sec":"ansible-s080","q":"Why is <code>status.phase == Running</code> not enough to say a Kubernetes pod is ready?","a":"<p><code>Running</code> means the containers were started. The pod is ready for traffic only when it has the <code>Ready</code> condition, meaning its readiness probe passes. A pod can be Running and still failing its probes. Check <code>.status.conditions</code> or use <code>kubectl wait</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0492","card":"q-ansible-079-apply-a-manifest-and-verify-the-pod","sec":"ansible-s080","q":"In Ansible, how do you query cluster state and install a chart instead of applying a raw manifest?","a":"<p>Use <code>kubernetes.core.k8s_info</code> to read objects such as pods, with <code>wait</code> and <code>wait_condition</code> to check them, and <code>kubernetes.core.helm</code> to install or upgrade a Helm release.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0493","card":"q-ansible-080-install-kubectl-and-check-the-cluster","sec":"ansible-s081","q":"Ansible to install <code>kubectl</code> and check the cluster?","a":"<p>The <code>creates</code> argument keeps the install step idempotent.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Install kubectl\n  hosts: all\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install\n    ansible.builtin.shell: |\n      curl -LO &quot;https://dl.k8s.io/release/$(curl -L -s https://dl.k8s.io/release/stable.txt)/bin/linux/amd64/kubectl&quot;\n      chmod +x kubectl &amp;&amp; mv kubectl /usr/local/bin/\n    args: { creates: /usr/local/bin/kubectl }\n  - name: Check cluster\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl cluster-info\n    register: cluster_info\n  - name: Debug\n    ansible.builtin.debug: { var: cluster_info.stdout }</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>ansible-playbook install_kubectl.yml</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0494","card":"q-ansible-080-install-kubectl-and-check-the-cluster","sec":"ansible-s081","q":"What is wrong with downloading kubectl from <code>stable.txt</code> in an Ansible task?","a":"<p>The <code>stable</code> marker changes over time, so two runs install different versions, which is not reproducible. The download is not verified. Pin a version, download with <code>get_url</code> and a <code>checksum</code>, and keep kubectl within one minor version of the API server (the Kubernetes version skew policy).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0495","card":"q-ansible-080-install-kubectl-and-check-the-cluster","sec":"ansible-s081","q":"Why can <code>kubectl cluster-info</code> fail in an Ansible task that has <code>become: yes</code>?","a":"<p>With <code>become</code> the task runs as root, whose kubeconfig is <code>/root/.kube/config</code>, not the login user's. Either run without <code>become</code>, set the <code>KUBECONFIG</code> environment variable on the task, or pass <code>--kubeconfig</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0496","card":"q-ansible-080-install-kubectl-and-check-the-cluster","sec":"ansible-s081","q":"Why prefer <code>get_url</code> or <code>unarchive</code> over a <code>shell</code> with <code>curl</code> to install a binary in Ansible?","a":"<p>They are idempotent, support checksums, report <code>changed</code> correctly, and give clear errors. A shell script has none of this and needs <code>creates</code> just to avoid repeating itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0497","card":"q-ansible-081-update-a-configmap-and-restart-pods","sec":"ansible-s082","q":"Ansible to update a ConfigMap and restart pods?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Update ConfigMap\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Apply\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f configmap.yaml\n  - name: Restart\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl rollout restart deployment web-app -n default</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>configmap.yaml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata: { name: app-config }\ndata: { env: &quot;production&quot; }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0498","card":"q-ansible-081-update-a-configmap-and-restart-pods","sec":"ansible-s082","q":"In Kubernetes, why must pods be restarted after a ConfigMap changes, and when is that not needed?","a":"<p>Environment variables from a ConfigMap are read when the container starts, so a running pod keeps old values. A ConfigMap mounted as a volume is refreshed in the pod after a delay, but not when mounted with <code>subPath</code>, and the application must re-read the file. A restart is the dependable way to apply the change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0499","card":"q-ansible-081-update-a-configmap-and-restart-pods","sec":"ansible-s082","q":"What does <code>kubectl rollout restart deployment</code> do and why is it preferred over deleting pods?","a":"<p>It starts a rolling update by changing an annotation in the pod template, so new pods are created and old ones are removed gradually, respecting the update strategy and readiness. Deleting pods by hand ignores that and can cause downtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0500","card":"q-ansible-081-update-a-configmap-and-restart-pods","sec":"ansible-s082","q":"How can an Ansible playbook restart the pods only when the ConfigMap actually changed?","a":"<p>Register the result of the apply task and use a <code>when</code> on the restart, or notify a handler. With <code>kubernetes.core.k8s</code>, the <code>changed</code> flag tells you whether the object changed. With a <code>kubectl apply</code> command, parse the output for <code>configured</code>, or use <code>changed_when</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0501","card":"q-ansible-081-update-a-configmap-and-restart-pods","sec":"ansible-s082","q":"How can Kubernetes roll pods automatically when a ConfigMap changes, with no extra restart step?","a":"<p>Put a hash of the ConfigMap content in an annotation of the pod template (Helm does this with a <code>checksum/config</code> annotation). A content change changes the template, so the Deployment starts a rollout by itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0502","card":"q-ansible-082-check-pod-logs-and-fail-on-errors","sec":"ansible-s083","q":"Ansible task to check pod logs and fail on errors?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Check logs\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Get logs\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl logs &lt;pod-name&gt; -n &lt;namespace&gt;\n    register: pod_logs\n  - name: Fail on error\n    ansible.builtin.fail: { msg: &quot;Errors in logs&quot; }\n    when: &quot;&#x27;error&#x27; in pod_logs.stdout | lower&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0503","card":"q-ansible-082-check-pod-logs-and-fail-on-errors","sec":"ansible-s083","q":"What is the weakness of failing an Ansible task when the pod log contains the word \"error\"?","a":"<p>A text search gives false positives (a line such as <code>error_count=0</code>) and misses real problems that use other words. It reads the whole log, including old entries. Narrow it with <code>--since</code> or <code>--tail</code>, match a clear pattern, and prefer health checks, probes or metrics for real verification.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Fail on error\n  ansible.builtin.fail: { msg: &quot;Errors in logs&quot; }\n  when: &quot;&#x27;error&#x27; in pod_logs.stdout | lower&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0504","card":"q-ansible-082-check-pod-logs-and-fail-on-errors","sec":"ansible-s083","q":"In Ansible, how is <code>'error' in pod_logs.stdout | lower</code> evaluated?","a":"<p>The filter binds tighter than the <code>in</code> operator, so <code>pod_logs.stdout | lower</code> is computed first and then <code>'error'</code> is searched in that lowercase text. It is a case-insensitive substring test.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0505","card":"q-ansible-082-check-pod-logs-and-fail-on-errors","sec":"ansible-s083","q":"How do you get logs of a pod that has crashed and restarted, and of one container in a multi-container pod?","a":"<p><code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous</code> shows the previous container instance. <code>kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c &lt;container&gt;</code> selects a container. To read all pods of a label, use <code>kubectl logs -l app=web</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0506","card":"q-ansible-083-deploy-a-service-and-expose-it-externall","sec":"ansible-s084","q":"Ansible to deploy a K8s Service and expose it externally?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Deploy service\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n  - name: Apply\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl apply -f service.yaml\n  - name: Expose\n    ansible.builtin.command: kubectl expose service web-service --type=LoadBalancer --port=80 --name=web-lb -n default</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>service.yaml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>apiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata: { name: web-service }\nspec:\n  selector: { app: web-app }\n  ports: [{ port: 80, targetPort: 8080 }]\n  type: ClusterIP</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0507","card":"q-ansible-083-deploy-a-service-and-expose-it-externall","sec":"ansible-s084","q":"What is the difference between the Kubernetes Service types ClusterIP, NodePort and LoadBalancer?","a":"<p><code>ClusterIP</code> (default) gives a virtual IP reachable only inside the cluster. <code>NodePort</code> also opens a port on every node (default range 30000-32767). <code>LoadBalancer</code> asks the cloud provider for an external load balancer in front of the NodePort. Each type builds on the previous one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0508","card":"q-ansible-083-deploy-a-service-and-expose-it-externall","sec":"ansible-s084","q":"In a Kubernetes Service, what do <code>port</code>, <code>targetPort</code> and <code>selector</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>selector</code> picks the pods that receive traffic, by label. <code>port</code> is the port the Service listens on. <code>targetPort</code> is the container port the traffic is forwarded to. So clients call port 80 and the pod receives it on 8080.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>spec:\n  selector: { app: web-app }\n  ports: [{ port: 80, targetPort: 8080 }]\n  type: ClusterIP</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0509","card":"q-ansible-083-deploy-a-service-and-expose-it-externall","sec":"ansible-s084","q":"A Kubernetes Service of type LoadBalancer stays at <code>&lt;pending&gt;</code> for its external IP on a bare-metal cluster. Why?","a":"<p>A cloud controller creates the external load balancer, and a bare-metal cluster has none. Install an implementation such as MetalLB, or use NodePort or an Ingress.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0510","card":"q-ansible-083-deploy-a-service-and-expose-it-externall","sec":"ansible-s084","q":"Why create the external Service from a manifest with <code>type: LoadBalancer</code> instead of running <code>kubectl expose</code> after applying a ClusterIP Service?","a":"<p>The manifest keeps the full desired state in Git and is idempotent. <code>kubectl expose</code> creates a second Service imperatively, which Ansible cannot compare, so it reports <code>changed</code> each run or fails on a repeat.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0511","card":"q-ansible-084-run-an-ansible-playbook-from-github-acti","sec":"ansible-s085","q":"GitHub Actions to run an Ansible playbook on push?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Run Ansible\non: { push: { branches: [main] } }\njobs:\n  ansible:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - run: sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt install -y ansible\n    - run: ansible-playbook playbook.yml -i inventory.ini</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>playbook.yml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Test\n  hosts: all\n  tasks:\n  - name: Ping\n    ansible.builtin.ping:</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0512","card":"q-ansible-084-run-an-ansible-playbook-from-github-acti","sec":"ansible-s085","q":"How does a GitHub Actions workflow give Ansible the SSH key for the managed hosts?","a":"<p>Store the private key as an encrypted secret, write it to a file at run time with permissions <code>600</code> (or load it into <code>ssh-agent</code>), and pass it with <code>--private-key</code>. Never commit it, and do not print it. Add the host keys to <code>known_hosts</code> so strict host key checking stays on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0513","card":"q-ansible-084-run-an-ansible-playbook-from-github-acti","sec":"ansible-s085","q":"A GitHub-hosted runner cannot reach the servers Ansible must configure because they are in a private network. What are the options?","a":"<p>Use a self-hosted runner inside the network, connect through a bastion host (<code>ProxyJump</code> in the SSH settings), or use a VPN or private connectivity. Do not open the servers' SSH port to the internet just for CI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0514","card":"q-ansible-084-run-an-ansible-playbook-from-github-acti","sec":"ansible-s085","q":"Why install Ansible with <code>pip</code> and a pinned version rather than <code>apt install ansible</code> in a workflow?","a":"<p>The distribution package gives whatever version the runner image ships, which changes over time and may lack collections. <code>pip install ansible-core==&lt;version&gt;</code> plus a <code>requirements.yml</code> for collections gives repeatable runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0515","card":"q-ansible-084-run-an-ansible-playbook-from-github-acti","sec":"ansible-s085","q":"How do you make sure two Ansible deployments from GitHub Actions never run at the same time?","a":"<p>Add a <code>concurrency</code> group to the workflow or job, for example <code>concurrency: ansible-prod</code>. Runs in the same group queue behind each other, and <code>cancel-in-progress</code> controls whether a newer run cancels an older one. Be careful cancelling a deployment halfway.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0516","card":"q-ansible-084-run-an-ansible-playbook-from-github-acti","sec":"ansible-s085","q":"Which GitHub Actions trigger suits a check of an Ansible change before merge, and which one a deployment?","a":"<p>Use <code>pull_request</code> with <code>--check --diff</code> (and <code>ansible-lint</code>) for review. Use <code>push</code> to the main branch, or <code>workflow_dispatch</code> for a manual run, for the real deployment, optionally with an environment that needs approval.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0517","card":"q-ansible-085-configure-a-k8s-node-with-ansible-from-g","sec":"ansible-s086","q":"GitHub Actions with Ansible to configure a K8s node and verify?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>name: Configure Node\non: { push: { branches: [main] } }\njobs:\n  configure:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n    - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n    - run: sudo apt update &amp;&amp; sudo apt install -y ansible\n    - run: ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini configure_node.yml\n    - uses: azure/setup-kubectl@v3\n    - run: echo &quot;${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }}&quot; &gt; $HOME/.kube/config\n    - run: kubectl get nodes</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>configure_node.yml</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Configure node\n  hosts: k8s_nodes\n  become: yes\n  tasks:\n  - name: Install kubeadm\n    ansible.builtin.package: { name: kubeadm, state: present }</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0518","card":"q-ansible-085-configure-a-k8s-node-with-ansible-from-g","sec":"ansible-s086","q":"What can go wrong with these workflow lines that write a kubeconfig from a secret?","a":"<p>The <code>.kube</code> directory may not exist, so create it with <code>mkdir -p</code>. The file should be <code>chmod 600</code>. Putting <code>${{ secrets.X }}</code> directly into the script text is fragile: quotes or special characters in the value can break the shell command. Pass the secret through <code>env:</code> and use <code>printf '%s' \"$KUBECONFIG_DATA\"</code>. Secrets are masked in logs, but do not echo them in other forms.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- run: echo &quot;${{ secrets.KUBECONFIG }}&quot; &gt; $HOME/.kube/config\n- run: kubectl get nodes</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0519","card":"q-ansible-085-configure-a-k8s-node-with-ansible-from-g","sec":"ansible-s086","q":"Why does a workflow that configures a Kubernetes node with Ansible end with a <code>kubectl get nodes</code> step?","a":"<p>It is a check: it proves the pipeline can reach the cluster and shows whether the node is registered and <code>Ready</code>. If the node does not appear or stays <code>NotReady</code>, the step or a following check fails the job.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0520","card":"q-ansible-085-configure-a-k8s-node-with-ansible-from-g","sec":"ansible-s086","q":"How would you protect a pipeline that changes production nodes with Ansible?","a":"<p>Use an environment with required reviewers, restrict who can modify the workflow, keep secrets scoped to that environment, limit the SSH key to the required hosts, run <code>--check --diff</code> first, and use <code>concurrency</code> to avoid parallel runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0521","card":"q-ansible-086-infrastructure-as-code-and-ansible-vs-te","sec":"ansible-s087","q":"What is Infrastructure as Code (IaC), and how do Terraform and Ansible support it?","a":"<p>IaC manages and provisions infrastructure through machine-readable definition files instead of manual processes, giving automation, consistency and version control.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Terraform</strong>: a declarative tool using HCL; supports many cloud providers and tracks resources in a state file.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ansible</strong>: a procedural tool using YAML playbooks; agentless over SSH, strong at configuration management and application deployment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Terraform focuses on provisioning and Ansible on configuration.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0522","card":"q-ansible-086-infrastructure-as-code-and-ansible-vs-te","sec":"ansible-s087","q":"Key differences between Ansible and Terraform when managing AWS resources?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Approach</strong>: Terraform is declarative (desired state); Ansible is procedural (steps to reach a state).</li>\n  <li><strong>Focus</strong>: Terraform provisions infrastructure (creating EC2 instances); Ansible configures it (installing software on instances).</li>\n  <li><strong>State</strong>: Terraform keeps a state file; Ansible is stateless.</li>\n  <li><strong>Python integration</strong>: Terraform uses <code>python-terraform</code> or <code>subprocess</code>; Ansible uses the <code>ansible</code> Python API, <code>ansible-runner</code> or custom modules.</li>\n</ul>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0523","card":"q-ansible-086-infrastructure-as-code-and-ansible-vs-te","sec":"ansible-s087","q":"What does the Terraform state file hold and where should it be stored?","a":"<p>It maps each resource in the configuration to the real object (IDs and attributes), so Terraform knows what to create, change or destroy. Store it in a remote backend with locking and encryption (for example an object store with a lock mechanism), not in Git, because it can contain secrets and must not be edited by two people at once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0524","card":"q-ansible-086-infrastructure-as-code-and-ansible-vs-te","sec":"ansible-s087","q":"Is Ansible procedural or declarative?","a":"<p>Mixed. A playbook runs tasks in order, which is procedural, but most modules are declarative: you state the wanted result (<code>state: present</code>) and the module works out whether to act. Terraform is fully declarative.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0525","card":"q-ansible-086-infrastructure-as-code-and-ansible-vs-te","sec":"ansible-s087","q":"How does each tool detect drift between the code and reality?","a":"<p>Terraform runs <code>terraform plan</code>, which compares the state and the real infrastructure with the code and shows differences. Ansible runs <code>--check --diff</code>, which reports what would change on hosts, but it cannot detect objects it was not told about.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0526","card":"q-ansible-086-infrastructure-as-code-and-ansible-vs-te","sec":"ansible-s087","q":"How do you hand the servers Terraform created to Ansible?","a":"<p>Read the Terraform outputs (for example IP addresses) with <code>terraform output -json</code> and build an inventory from them, or use a dynamic inventory plugin that queries the cloud API. A <code>local-exec</code> provisioner that runs <code>ansible-playbook</code> also works but couples the two tools.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0527","card":"q-ansible-087-how-ansible-uses-python","sec":"ansible-s088","q":"How does Ansible use Python under the hood, and why is Python a good fit?","a":"<p>Ansible is written in Python and uses it for:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Module execution</strong>: most modules are Python scripts executed on the target hosts.</li>\n  <li><strong>API integration</strong>: the <code>ansible</code> Python package allows programmatic playbook execution.</li>\n  <li><strong>Extensibility</strong>: custom modules can be written in Python.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Python fits because of its simplicity, extensive libraries, cross-platform compatibility, and ability to handle complex automation logic.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0528","card":"q-ansible-087-how-ansible-uses-python","sec":"ansible-s088","q":"How does Python integrate with Ansible for resource management?","a":"<p>Python scripts can use the <code>ansible</code> module to run playbooks programmatically, or create custom modules.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0529","card":"q-ansible-087-how-ansible-uses-python","sec":"ansible-s088","q":"How do you ensure idempotency with Ansible?","a":"<p>Use <code>state</code> parameters (for example <code>state=present</code>) and check conditions.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0530","card":"q-ansible-087-how-ansible-uses-python","sec":"ansible-s088","q":"What is <code>ansible_python_interpreter</code> in Ansible, and when do you set it?","a":"<p>It tells Ansible which Python to use on the managed host. Set it when the default is wrong or missing, for example a host with only <code>python3</code> at a custom path, or a virtualenv that holds the libraries a module needs. Recent versions can discover the interpreter automatically (<code>interpreter_python = auto</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0531","card":"q-ansible-087-how-ansible-uses-python","sec":"ansible-s088","q":"Why do cloud modules such as the AWS ones usually run with <code>hosts: localhost</code>, and where must <code>boto3</code> be installed?","a":"<p>The modules call the cloud API, so the work happens from one place and there is no server to log into. The module runs where the task runs, which is the control node. So <code>boto3</code> and <code>botocore</code> must be installed in the Python environment of the control node (or the interpreter used for <code>localhost</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0532","card":"q-ansible-087-how-ansible-uses-python","sec":"ansible-s088","q":"Where do filter and lookup plugins run in Ansible, and what does that mean for their Python dependencies?","a":"<p>They run on the control node, inside the Ansible process. Their libraries (for example <code>netaddr</code> for IP filters or <code>hvac</code> for HashiCorp Vault) must be installed on the control node, not on the managed hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0533","card":"q-ansible-088-custom-ansible-modules-in-python","sec":"ansible-s089","q":"What is the role of Python in writing custom Ansible modules?","a":"<p>Custom modules are Python scripts that extend Ansible. They accept arguments as JSON input, return JSON output with results or errors, and use the <code>AnsibleModule</code> class for argument parsing and error handling.</p>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0534","card":"q-ansible-088-custom-ansible-modules-in-python","sec":"ansible-s089","q":"How do you create a custom Ansible module in Python?","a":"<p>Use <code>AnsibleModule</code> to handle input parameters and output, and place the module in a <code>library/</code> directory in the Ansible project (for example <code>library/custom_module.py</code>).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>#!/usr/bin/python\nfrom ansible.module_utils.basic import AnsibleModule\n\ndef main():\n    module_args = dict(\n        name=dict(type=&#x27;str&#x27;, required=True),\n        state=dict(type=&#x27;str&#x27;, default=&#x27;present&#x27;, choices=[&#x27;present&#x27;, &#x27;absent&#x27;])\n    )\n\n    module = AnsibleModule(\n        argument_spec=module_args,\n        supports_check_mode=True\n    )\n\n    name = module.params[&#x27;name&#x27;]\n    state = module.params[&#x27;state&#x27;]\n\n    result = dict(changed=False, message=&quot;&quot;)\n\n    if state == &#x27;present&#x27;:\n        result[&#x27;changed&#x27;] = True\n        result[&#x27;message&#x27;] = f&quot;Resource {name} created&quot;\n    else:\n        result[&#x27;changed&#x27;] = True\n        result[&#x27;message&#x27;] = f&quot;Resource {name} deleted&quot;\n\n    module.exit_json(**result)\n\nif __name__ == &#x27;__main__&#x27;:\n    main()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0535","card":"q-ansible-088-custom-ansible-modules-in-python","sec":"ansible-s089","q":"Why write a custom Ansible module instead of calling a script with <code>command</code>?","a":"<p>A module takes validated arguments, can be idempotent, supports check mode, returns structured JSON (<code>changed</code>, <code>failed</code>, your own keys), and appears in the output like built-in modules. A script through <code>command</code> returns only text and always reports <code>changed</code>. Write a module when logic is reused or touches an API; for a one-off, a script is fine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0536","card":"q-ansible-088-custom-ansible-modules-in-python","sec":"ansible-s089","q":"What does <code>supports_check_mode=True</code> in <code>AnsibleModule</code> promise, and how must the module behave?","a":"<p>It declares that the module can run in <code>--check</code> mode without changing anything. Inside, test <code>module.check_mode</code> and skip the real change, but still report the <code>changed</code> value that would result. A module that claims support and changes things anyway is dangerous.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0537","card":"q-ansible-088-custom-ansible-modules-in-python","sec":"ansible-s089","q":"In an Ansible custom module, how do you mark an argument as secret and express rules between arguments?","a":"<p>Set <code>no_log=True</code> on the argument in <code>argument_spec</code>, so its value is hidden in logs and results. Pass <code>required_if</code>, <code>mutually_exclusive</code>, <code>required_together</code> or <code>required_one_of</code> to <code>AnsibleModule</code> to validate combinations. Ansible checks them before your code runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0538","card":"q-ansible-088-custom-ansible-modules-in-python","sec":"ansible-s089","q":"How do you test an Ansible custom module without a playbook?","a":"<p>Run it ad hoc: <code>ANSIBLE_LIBRARY=./library ansible localhost -m my_module -a \"name=x state=present\"</code>. You can also call the Python file directly with a JSON arguments file, or write unit tests that mock <code>AnsibleModule</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0539","card":"q-ansible-088-custom-ansible-modules-in-python","sec":"ansible-s089","q":"How should a custom Ansible module handle a third-party Python library that may be missing on the host?","a":"<p>Import it in a <code>try/except ImportError</code>, remember the traceback, and fail cleanly with <code>module.fail_json(msg=missing_required_lib(\"name\"))</code> (from <code>ansible.module_utils.basic</code>). The user then sees which library to install instead of a stack trace.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0540","card":"q-ansible-089-the-ansible-python-api","sec":"ansible-s090","q":"What is the purpose of the <code>ansible</code> Python API, and how is it used to run playbooks programmatically?","a":"<p>It lets Python scripts run playbooks and tasks, integrate Ansible with other tools, and customize execution parameters.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from ansible import context\nfrom ansible.cli import CLI\nfrom ansible.module_utils.common.collections import ImmutableDict\nfrom ansible.executor.playbook_executor import PlaybookExecutor\n\ncontext.CLIARGS = ImmutableDict(tags={}, listtags=False, listtasks=False, listhosts=False,\n                               syntax=False, connection=&#x27;smart&#x27;, module_path=None,\n                               forks=100, private_key_file=None, ssh_common_args=None,\n                               ssh_extra_args=None, sftp_extra_args=None, scp_extra_args=None,\n                               become=None, become_method=None, become_user=None, verbosity=0,\n                               check=False, diff=False)\n\nplaybook_path = &#x27;playbook.yml&#x27;\nexecutor = PlaybookExecutor(playbooks=[playbook_path], inventory=None,\n                            variable_manager=None, loader=None, passwords=None)\nexecutor.run()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0541","card":"q-ansible-089-the-ansible-python-api","sec":"ansible-s090","q":"In Ansible's internal Python API, why is <code>context.CLIARGS = ImmutableDict(...)</code> set before running a playbook?","a":"<p>The classes behind <code>ansible-playbook</code> read their options from a global context that the command line normally fills. When you call them from Python, you must fill it yourself with the same keys the CLI would set (forks, check mode, become, verbosity and others). Missing keys cause errors. This mirrors the CLI, which is one reason the API is awkward.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>context.CLIARGS = ImmutableDict(forks=100, check=False, diff=False, verbosity=0)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0542","card":"q-ansible-089-the-ansible-python-api","sec":"ansible-s090","q":"In Ansible's Python API, which objects does <code>PlaybookExecutor</code> need to run a playbook correctly?","a":"<p>A <code>DataLoader</code> (reads YAML), an <code>InventoryManager</code> (hosts and groups, built from the loader and the inventory source), and a <code>VariableManager</code> (variables, built from the loader and inventory). Pass them to <code>PlaybookExecutor</code> along with the playbook list and the passwords. Passing <code>None</code> for them is not enough for a real run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0543","card":"q-ansible-090-ad-hoc-command-through-the-python-api","sec":"ansible-s091","q":"Write a Python script to execute an ad-hoc Ansible command using the <code>ansible</code> Python API.","a":"<p>The <code>ansible</code> Python API is internal and less stable than <code>ansible-runner</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from ansible import context\nfrom ansible.cli import CLI\nfrom ansible.module_utils.common.collections import ImmutableDict\nfrom ansible.executor.task_queue_manager import TaskQueueManager\nfrom ansible.inventory.manager import InventoryManager\nfrom ansible.parsing.dataloader import DataLoader\nfrom ansible.playbook.play import Play\nfrom ansible.vars.manager import VariableManager\n\n# Setup context\nloader = DataLoader()\ncontext.CLIARGS = ImmutableDict(tags={}, listtags=False, listtasks=False, listhosts=False,\n                               syntax=False, connection=&#x27;smart&#x27;, module_path=None, forks=10,\n                               remote_user=&#x27;user&#x27;, private_key_file=None, ssh_common_args=None,\n                               ssh_extra_args=None, sftp_extra_args=None, scp_extra_args=None,\n                               become=False, become_method=&#x27;sudo&#x27;, become_user=&#x27;root&#x27;, verbosity=0,\n                               check=False, diff=False)\n\n# Initialize inventory and variables\ninventory = InventoryManager(loader=loader, sources=[&#x27;/path/to/inventory&#x27;])\nvariable_manager = VariableManager(loader=loader, inventory=inventory)\n\n# Define ad-hoc command\nplay_source = dict(\n    name=&quot;Ad-hoc command&quot;,\n    hosts=&quot;all&quot;,\n    gather_facts=&quot;no&quot;,\n    tasks=[\n        dict(action=dict(module=&quot;command&quot;, args=dict(cmd=&quot;uptime&quot;)))\n    ]\n)\n\n# Create play\nplay = Play().load(play_source, variable_manager=variable_manager, loader=loader)\n\n# Run the play\ntqm = None\ntry:\n    tqm = TaskQueueManager(\n        inventory=inventory,\n        variable_manager=variable_manager,\n        loader=loader,\n        passwords=dict(),\n    )\n    result = tqm.run(play)\nfinally:\n    if tqm is not None:\n        tqm.cleanup()</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0544","card":"q-ansible-090-ad-hoc-command-through-the-python-api","sec":"ansible-s091","q":"In Ansible's Python API, what is the role of <code>TaskQueueManager</code> and why is it cleaned up in a <code>finally</code> block?","a":"<p><code>TaskQueueManager</code> starts the worker processes (forks), runs the play on the hosts and passes events to callbacks. <code>cleanup()</code> stops the workers and removes temporary files. Putting it in <code>finally</code> makes sure that happens even if the run raises an exception.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>tqm = None\ntry:\n    tqm = TaskQueueManager(inventory=inventory, variable_manager=variable_manager,\n                           loader=loader, passwords=dict())\n    result = tqm.run(play)\nfinally:\n    if tqm is not None:\n        tqm.cleanup()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0545","card":"q-ansible-090-ad-hoc-command-through-the-python-api","sec":"ansible-s091","q":"In Ansible's Python API, <code>tqm.run(play)</code> returns only an exit code. How do you get the per-host output?","a":"<p>Register a callback plugin by subclassing <code>CallbackBase</code> and passing it as <code>stdout_callback</code> to <code>TaskQueueManager</code>. Override methods such as <code>v2_runner_on_ok</code> and <code>v2_runner_on_failed</code> to collect results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0546","card":"q-ansible-090-ad-hoc-command-through-the-python-api","sec":"ansible-s091","q":"In Ansible's Python API, how is a play built from a dictionary and why is <code>gather_facts</code> set to <code>no</code> for an ad-hoc command?","a":"<p><code>Play().load(play_source, variable_manager=..., loader=...)</code> turns a dict with <code>name</code>, <code>hosts</code> and <code>tasks</code> into a play object, the same structure as YAML. An ad-hoc command does not need facts, so skipping the gather step saves time on every host.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0547","card":"q-ansible-090-ad-hoc-command-through-the-python-api","sec":"ansible-s091","q":"How do you run an ad-hoc module from Python with <code>ansible-runner</code> instead of the internal API?","a":"<p><code>ansible_runner.run(private_data_dir=dir, host_pattern='all', module='command', module_args='uptime')</code>. Runner builds and runs the <code>ansible</code> command for you in a separate process and returns a result object.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0548","card":"q-ansible-091-run-a-playbook-with-ansible-runner","sec":"ansible-s092","q":"How do you run an Ansible playbook programmatically using <code>ansible-runner</code>?","a":"<p>Install it with <code>pip install ansible-runner</code> and use the <code>run</code> function.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import ansible_runner\n\n# Run an Ansible playbook\nr = ansible_runner.run(\n    private_data_dir=&#x27;/path/to/ansible/project&#x27;,\n    playbook=&#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;,\n    inventory=&#x27;/path/to/inventory&#x27;\n)\n\n# Check the result\nif r.rc == 0:\n    print(&quot;Playbook executed successfully&quot;)\nelse:\n    print(f&quot;Playbook failed with status: {r.rc}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0549","card":"q-ansible-091-run-a-playbook-with-ansible-runner","sec":"ansible-s092","q":"Why prefer <code>ansible-runner</code> to the internal Ansible API when running playbooks from Python?","a":"<p>It has a supported, stable interface, runs Ansible in a separate process (so your script is isolated from Ansible internals and crashes), produces structured events and artifacts, and is what AWX uses. The internal API can change between releases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0550","card":"q-ansible-091-run-a-playbook-with-ansible-runner","sec":"ansible-s092","q":"In <code>ansible_runner.run</code>, what is <code>private_data_dir</code>?","a":"<p>The working directory that holds everything for the run: <code>project/</code> (playbooks and roles), <code>inventory/</code>, <code>env/</code> (extra vars, settings, SSH key) and <code>artifacts/</code> (output and events from each run). A relative <code>playbook='deploy.yml'</code> is looked up in <code>project/</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0551","card":"q-ansible-091-run-a-playbook-with-ansible-runner","sec":"ansible-s092","q":"Which attributes of the object returned by <code>ansible_runner.run</code> do you check, and which is better for success: <code>rc</code> or <code>status</code>?","a":"<p><code>rc</code> is the exit code, <code>status</code> is a text such as <code>successful</code>, <code>failed</code>, <code>timeout</code> or <code>canceled</code>, <code>stats</code> holds per-host counts and <code>events</code> yields the events. Use <code>status</code> because it distinguishes failure from timeout and cancellation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0552","card":"q-ansible-091-run-a-playbook-with-ansible-runner","sec":"ansible-s092","q":"What is the difference between <code>ansible_runner.run</code> and <code>ansible_runner.run_async</code>?","a":"<p><code>run</code> blocks until the playbook ends and returns the result. <code>run_async</code> returns right away with a thread and a runner object so your code can do other work and check the result later.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0553","card":"q-ansible-091-run-a-playbook-with-ansible-runner","sec":"ansible-s092","q":"Does <code>pip install ansible-runner</code> install Ansible itself?","a":"<p>No. It is a wrapper that calls <code>ansible-playbook</code> and <code>ansible</code>, so Ansible must already be installed in the environment where the script runs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0554","card":"q-ansible-092-parse-playbook-output-and-log-results","sec":"ansible-s093","q":"How do you parse Ansible playbook output and extract results?","a":"<p>Run the playbook with <code>ansible-runner</code> and parse the JSON events under the <code>job_events</code> folder of the artifacts directory in the <code>private_data_dir</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import ansible_runner\nimport json\nimport os\n\n# Run playbook\nr = ansible_runner.run(\n    private_data_dir=&#x27;/path/to/ansible/project&#x27;,\n    playbook=&#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;\n)\n\n# Parse results\nartifacts_dir = os.path.join(r.config.artifact_dir, &#x27;job_events&#x27;)\nfor event_file in os.listdir(artifacts_dir):\n    with open(os.path.join(artifacts_dir, event_file)) as f:\n        event_data = json.load(f)\n        if &#x27;event_data&#x27; in event_data and &#x27;task&#x27; in event_data[&#x27;event_data&#x27;]:\n            task = event_data[&#x27;event_data&#x27;][&#x27;task&#x27;]\n            result = event_data[&#x27;event_data&#x27;].get(&#x27;res&#x27;, {})\n            print(f&quot;Task: {task}, Result: {result}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0555","card":"q-ansible-092-parse-playbook-output-and-log-results","sec":"ansible-s093","q":"Run a playbook and log the results to a file?","a":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import ansible_runner\nimport json\n\n# Run playbook\nr = ansible_runner.run(\n    private_data_dir=&#x27;/path/to/ansible/project&#x27;,\n    playbook=&#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;\n)\n\n# Log results\nwith open(&#x27;ansible_log.json&#x27;, &#x27;w&#x27;) as f:\n    json.dump({\n        &#x27;status&#x27;: &#x27;success&#x27; if r.rc == 0 else &#x27;failed&#x27;,\n        &#x27;return_code&#x27;: r.rc,\n        &#x27;stats&#x27;: r.stats\n    }, f, indent=2)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0556","card":"q-ansible-092-parse-playbook-output-and-log-results","sec":"ansible-s093","q":"Why parse structured events from ansible-runner instead of the text output of a playbook?","a":"<p>Text output is meant for people and its format can change. Events are JSON with the event type, host, task name and result, so code can rely on the fields.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0557","card":"q-ansible-092-parse-playbook-output-and-log-results","sec":"ansible-s093","q":"What are the main event types in an ansible-runner run?","a":"<p><code>runner_on_ok</code>, <code>runner_on_failed</code>, <code>runner_on_unreachable</code> and <code>runner_on_skipped</code> for each task result per host, <code>playbook_on_task_start</code> for tasks, and <code>playbook_on_stats</code> for the final summary. Filter on <code>event</code> to pick the ones you need.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0558","card":"q-ansible-092-parse-playbook-output-and-log-results","sec":"ansible-s093","q":"What is fragile about this loop over the files in the events directory?","a":"<p><code>os.listdir</code> returns names in arbitrary order, so events may not be chronological. Event file names start with a counter, so sort them. A simpler option is to iterate <code>r.events</code> or pass an <code>event_handler</code> function to <code>run</code>, and avoid reading files.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for event_file in os.listdir(artifacts_dir):\n    with open(os.path.join(artifacts_dir, event_file)) as f:\n        event_data = json.load(f)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0559","card":"q-ansible-092-parse-playbook-output-and-log-results","sec":"ansible-s093","q":"What keys does <code>r.stats</code> contain in ansible-runner and what does <code>dark</code> mean?","a":"<p>It maps counters to per-host numbers: <code>ok</code>, <code>changed</code>, <code>failures</code>, <code>dark</code>, <code>skipped</code>, <code>ignored</code>, <code>rescued</code>, <code>processed</code>. <code>dark</code> means unreachable hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0560","card":"q-ansible-092-parse-playbook-output-and-log-results","sec":"ansible-s093","q":"What should you watch for when logging ansible-runner results to a file?","a":"<p>Results can contain secrets (passwords, tokens, command output). Tasks with <code>no_log: true</code> are censored, but others are not. Do not write raw event data to shared logs, and restrict the file permissions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0561","card":"q-ansible-093-generate-a-dynamic-inventory","sec":"ansible-s094","q":"Write a Python script to dynamically generate an Ansible inventory file.","a":"<p>Write the host and group information to a file in YAML or INI format.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import yaml\n\n# Define inventory data\ninventory = {\n    &#x27;webservers&#x27;: {\n        &#x27;hosts&#x27;: [&#x27;web1.example.com&#x27;, &#x27;web2.example.com&#x27;],\n        &#x27;vars&#x27;: {\n            &#x27;http_port&#x27;: 80\n        }\n    },\n    &#x27;dbservers&#x27;: {\n        &#x27;hosts&#x27;: [&#x27;db1.example.com&#x27;]\n    }\n}\n\n# Write to inventory file\nwith open(&#x27;inventory.yml&#x27;, &#x27;w&#x27;) as f:\n    yaml.dump(inventory, f)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0562","card":"q-ansible-093-generate-a-dynamic-inventory","sec":"ansible-s094","q":"A Python script writes this structure into a YAML inventory file for Ansible. What is wrong with it?","a":"<p>In the YAML inventory format, <code>hosts</code> must be a mapping of host names (each to an optional dictionary of variables), not a list. Write <code>'hosts': {'web1.example.com': {}, ...}</code> and check the file with <code>ansible-inventory -i file --list</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>inventory = {\n    &#x27;webservers&#x27;: {\n        &#x27;hosts&#x27;: [&#x27;web1.example.com&#x27;, &#x27;web2.example.com&#x27;],\n        &#x27;vars&#x27;: {&#x27;http_port&#x27;: 80}\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0563","card":"q-ansible-093-generate-a-dynamic-inventory","sec":"ansible-s094","q":"What must an Ansible dynamic inventory script return when called with <code>--list</code>?","a":"<p>JSON on standard output with groups, each with <code>hosts</code> (and optional <code>vars</code>/<code>children</code>). Add a top-level <code>_meta</code> key with <code>hostvars</code> so Ansible does not call the script again with <code>--host</code> for every host, which is slow at scale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0564","card":"q-ansible-093-generate-a-dynamic-inventory","sec":"ansible-s094","q":"Why use <code>yaml.safe_dump</code> instead of <code>yaml.dump</code> when a script writes an inventory?","a":"<p><code>yaml.dump</code> can write Python-specific tags for some objects that Ansible cannot read. <code>safe_dump</code> writes only standard YAML types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0565","card":"q-ansible-094-handle-playbook-failures-and-pass-variab","sec":"ansible-s095","q":"How do you handle Ansible playbook failures in a Python script?","a":"<p>Check the return code (<code>rc</code>), parse the output or logs from <code>ansible-runner</code>, use try-except, and add retry logic if needed.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import ansible_runner\n\ntry:\n    r = ansible_runner.run(\n        private_data_dir=&#x27;/path/to/ansible/project&#x27;,\n        playbook=&#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;\n    )\n    if r.rc != 0:\n        print(f&quot;Playbook failed with exit code: {r.rc}&quot;)\n        # Retry logic\n        max_attempts = 3\n        for attempt in range(max_attempts):\n            retry_r = ansible_runner.run(\n                private_data_dir=&#x27;/path/to/ansible/project&#x27;,\n                playbook=&#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;\n            )\n            if retry_r.rc == 0:\n                print(&quot;Retry successful&quot;)\n                break\n            print(f&quot;Retry {attempt + 1} failed&quot;)\n    else:\n        print(&quot;Playbook succeeded&quot;)\nexcept Exception as e:\n    print(f&quot;Error running playbook: {e}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0566","card":"q-ansible-094-handle-playbook-failures-and-pass-variab","sec":"ansible-s095","q":"How do you pass variables to a playbook from a Python script?","a":"<p>Use the <code>extravars</code> parameter of <code>ansible-runner</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import ansible_runner\n\n# Define extra variables\nextra_vars = {\n    &#x27;http_port&#x27;: 8080,\n    &#x27;app_version&#x27;: &#x27;1.2.3&#x27;\n}\n\n# Run playbook with extra variables\nr = ansible_runner.run(\n    private_data_dir=&#x27;/path/to/ansible/project&#x27;,\n    playbook=&#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;,\n    extravars=extra_vars\n)\n\nprint(f&quot;Playbook executed with status: {r.rc}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0567","card":"q-ansible-094-handle-playbook-failures-and-pass-variab","sec":"ansible-s095","q":"When a Python script runs a playbook through <code>ansible-runner</code>, why is checking <code>r.rc</code> alone not enough to decide whether to retry?","a":"<p>A non-zero code can mean different things: a task failed on a host, a host was unreachable, or the playbook has a syntax or parse error. Retrying a syntax error or a bad variable never helps, and retrying a non-idempotent task can do damage. Retry only for transient causes (unreachable host, timeout), keep the number of attempts small, and wait between attempts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0568","card":"q-ansible-094-handle-playbook-failures-and-pass-variab","sec":"ansible-s095","q":"When a Python script runs a playbook through <code>ansible-runner</code>, how can it tell hosts that failed from hosts that were unreachable?","a":"<p>Read the run statistics instead of only the return code. In <code>ansible-runner</code>, <code>r.stats</code> is a dict with per-host counters such as <code>failures</code> (task failed) and <code>dark</code> (host unreachable), plus <code>ok</code> and <code>changed</code>, and <code>r.status</code> is <code>successful</code> or <code>failed</code>. Retry or re-queue the unreachable hosts, and alert on failed hosts. Exit codes of <code>ansible-playbook</code> differ between the documentation and the engine (for example, unreachable and parse errors can both return 4), so do not build retry logic on a single code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0569","card":"q-ansible-094-handle-playbook-failures-and-pass-variab","sec":"ansible-s095","q":"In <code>ansible-runner</code>, how do you pass variables to a playbook, and what is their precedence in Ansible?","a":"<p><code>extravars</code> is the equivalent of <code>-e</code> on the command line, and extra vars have the highest precedence in Ansible. They override play vars, inventory vars and role defaults, so use them for run-time overrides, not as the normal place for defaults.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import ansible_runner\nr = ansible_runner.run(\n    private_data_dir=&#x27;/path/to/ansible/project&#x27;,\n    playbook=&#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;,\n    extravars={&#x27;http_port&#x27;: 8080}\n)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0570","card":"q-ansible-094-handle-playbook-failures-and-pass-variab","sec":"ansible-s095","q":"In Ansible, why should you not pass a database password through <code>extravars</code> as plain text in a Python script?","a":"<p>Extra vars can end up in process listings, run artifacts or logs, and in the script's own source or history. Keep secrets in Ansible Vault or an external secrets manager, load them at run time, and set <code>no_log: true</code> on the tasks that use them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0571","card":"q-ansible-094-handle-playbook-failures-and-pass-variab","sec":"ansible-s095","q":"A deployment wrapper retries the whole playbook three times when it fails. What are the drawbacks, and what is a better approach?","a":"<p>A full rerun repeats work on hosts that already succeeded and can hide a real defect. It also behaves badly if tasks are not idempotent. Better: make tasks idempotent, retry only the failing hosts (limit the rerun with <code>--limit</code> to the hosts that failed in the run result), retry individual flaky tasks with <code>retries</code> and <code>until</code>, and give up with a clear alert after a few attempts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0572","card":"q-ansible-095-validate-syntax-before-running","sec":"ansible-s096","q":"How do you validate playbook syntax from Python before execution?","a":"<p>Run <code>ansible-playbook --syntax-check</code> through <code>ansible-runner</code> and confirm there are no errors.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import ansible_runner\n\n# Run syntax check\nr = ansible_runner.run(\n    private_data_dir=&#x27;/path/to/ansible/project&#x27;,\n    playbook=&#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;,\n    cmdline=&#x27;--syntax-check&#x27;\n)\n\nif r.rc == 0:\n    print(&quot;Syntax check passed&quot;)\nelse:\n    print(f&quot;Syntax check failed: {r.rc}&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0573","card":"q-ansible-095-validate-syntax-before-running","sec":"ansible-s096","q":"What does <code>ansible-playbook --syntax-check</code> verify, and what does it not verify?","a":"<p>It parses the playbook and checks that the YAML and the play structure are valid, for example unknown keywords or a missing required task key. It does not connect to hosts, does not render most variables, and does not prove module arguments are correct or that the tasks will succeed. Use <code>--check</code>, <code>ansible-lint</code> and a test run on a staging host for those.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0574","card":"q-ansible-095-validate-syntax-before-running","sec":"ansible-s096","q":"Where in a CI pipeline for Ansible code should syntax checking go, and why?","a":"<p>As an early, cheap stage before linting and before any run against real hosts. It needs no inventory access or credentials, fails in seconds, and stops broken code from reaching later stages that connect to servers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0575","card":"q-ansible-095-validate-syntax-before-running","sec":"ansible-s096","q":"In <code>ansible-runner</code>, the syntax check is run with <code>cmdline='--syntax-check'</code>. How does the script decide pass or fail?","a":"<p>By the return code. <code>ansible-playbook --syntax-check</code> exits with <code>0</code> when the playbook parses and non-zero when it does not, so the script gates the real run on <code>r.rc == 0</code>. The <code>cmdline</code> argument passes extra command-line flags straight to <code>ansible-playbook</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>r = ansible_runner.run(\n    private_data_dir=&#x27;/path/to/ansible/project&#x27;,\n    playbook=&#x27;deploy.yml&#x27;,\n    cmdline=&#x27;--syntax-check&#x27;\n)\nif r.rc == 0:\n    print(&quot;Syntax check passed&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0576","card":"q-ansible-096-integrate-ansible-with-aws-ec2-through-b","sec":"ansible-s097","q":"How can Python integrate Ansible with AWS services (EC2 provisioning)?","a":"<p>Use <code>boto3</code> to read AWS state and pass the result to Ansible as inventory or variables. Here running EC2 instances become an inventory, then a playbook is run against it.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nimport yaml\n\n# Initialize boto3 client\nec2 = boto3.client(&#x27;ec2&#x27;)\n\n# Get EC2 instances\nresponse = ec2.describe_instances()\n\n# Create inventory\ninventory = {&#x27;webservers&#x27;: {&#x27;hosts&#x27;: []}}\nfor reservation in response[&#x27;Reservations&#x27;]:\n    for instance in reservation[&#x27;Instances&#x27;]:\n        if instance[&#x27;State&#x27;][&#x27;Name&#x27;] == &#x27;running&#x27;:\n            inventory[&#x27;webservers&#x27;][&#x27;hosts&#x27;].append(instance[&#x27;PublicDnsName&#x27;])\n\n# Write inventory\nwith open(&#x27;inventory.yml&#x27;, &#x27;w&#x27;) as f:\n    yaml.dump(inventory, f)\n\n# Run Ansible playbook\nimport ansible_runner\nansible_runner.run(\n    private_data_dir=&#x27;/path/to/ansible/project&#x27;,\n    playbook=&#x27;configure_web.yml&#x27;,\n    inventory=&#x27;inventory.yml&#x27;\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0577","card":"q-ansible-096-integrate-ansible-with-aws-ec2-through-b","sec":"ansible-s097","q":"In boto3, why can a loop over <code>describe_instances()</code> miss instances in a large account, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The API returns results in pages. A single call returns one page and a <code>NextToken</code> for the rest. Use <code>ec2.get_paginator('describe_instances')</code> and iterate over its pages, or pass <code>Filters</code> so fewer results come back.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0578","card":"q-ansible-096-integrate-ansible-with-aws-ec2-through-b","sec":"ansible-s097","q":"In boto3 <code>describe_instances</code> output, how are instances nested, and why does a single loop over <code>Reservations</code> not give instances?","a":"<p>The response is a list of <code>Reservations</code>, and each reservation holds a list of <code>Instances</code>. A reservation is the launch request that created the instances, so one reservation can hold several instances. You need two nested loops, as in <code>for r in resp['Reservations']: for i in r['Instances']</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0579","card":"q-ansible-096-integrate-ansible-with-aws-ec2-through-b","sec":"ansible-s097","q":"A script builds an Ansible inventory from <code>PublicDnsName</code> of running EC2 instances. Which instances break it?","a":"<p>Instances in private subnets, which have no public DNS name, so the field is empty or missing. Use <code>PrivateIpAddress</code> or <code>PrivateDnsName</code> when the control node can reach the VPC (VPN, bastion, or control node inside the VPC), and filter on <code>instance-state-name</code> as well.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0580","card":"q-ansible-096-integrate-ansible-with-aws-ec2-through-b","sec":"ansible-s097","q":"When would you use the <code>amazon.aws.aws_ec2</code> inventory plugin instead of a custom boto3 script that writes an inventory file?","a":"<p>Almost always. The plugin is maintained, supports filters, <code>keyed_groups</code>, <code>compose</code> and caching, and builds the inventory at run time so it is never stale. A custom script makes sense only for logic the plugin cannot express, and then a script that follows the dynamic inventory contract is better than a stale static file.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0581","card":"q-ansible-097-validate-aws-credentials-before-a-playbo","sec":"ansible-s098","q":"Scenario: the playbook fails provisioning EC2 because of incorrect credentials. How would Python validate credentials first?","a":"<p>Make a simple API call such as <code>sts.get_caller_identity</code> with <code>boto3</code>, and run the playbook only if it succeeds.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nimport subprocess\nimport botocore\n\ndef validate_aws_credentials():\n    try:\n        sts = boto3.client(&quot;sts&quot;)\n        sts.get_caller_identity()\n        print(&quot;AWS credentials are valid&quot;)\n        return True\n    except botocore.exceptions.ClientError as e:\n        print(f&quot;Invalid AWS credentials: {e}&quot;)\n        return False\n\ndef run_ansible_playbook(playbook_path):\n    if validate_aws_credentials():\n        subprocess.run([&quot;ansible-playbook&quot;, playbook_path], check=True)\n        print(&quot;Ansible playbook executed successfully&quot;)\n    else:\n        print(&quot;Aborting playbook execution due to invalid credentials&quot;)\n\n# Example usage\nrun_ansible_playbook(&quot;provision_ec2.yml&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0582","card":"q-ansible-097-validate-aws-credentials-before-a-playbo","sec":"ansible-s098","q":"Why is <code>sts.get_caller_identity()</code> the usual call for checking AWS credentials in boto3?","a":"<p>It needs no IAM permissions, it works with any valid credentials, and it returns the account ID, user ID and ARN of the caller. So it proves the credentials are valid and shows which identity and account a playbook is about to act on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0583","card":"q-ansible-097-validate-aws-credentials-before-a-playbo","sec":"ansible-s098","q":"In this credential check, what case is missed by <code>except botocore.exceptions.ClientError</code>?","a":"<p>When there are no credentials at all, boto3 raises <code>botocore.exceptions.NoCredentialsError</code>, which is not a <code>ClientError</code>. It would escape the handler and crash the script. Catch <code>NoCredentialsError</code> (and <code>BotoCoreError</code> in general) as well. <code>ClientError</code> covers errors returned by AWS, such as an invalid or expired token.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    sts = boto3.client(&quot;sts&quot;)\n    sts.get_caller_identity()\n    return True\nexcept botocore.exceptions.ClientError as e:\n    return False</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0584","card":"q-ansible-097-validate-aws-credentials-before-a-playbo","sec":"ansible-s098","q":"In which order does boto3 look for credentials?","a":"<p>Explicit parameters passed to the client or session, then environment variables (<code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code> and so on), then the shared credentials file and config file (profiles, including assume-role and SSO), then container credentials (ECS), and finally the EC2 instance profile through the metadata service. Knowing the order explains why a stale environment variable can override a correct profile.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0585","card":"q-ansible-097-validate-aws-credentials-before-a-playbo","sec":"ansible-s098","q":"Why validate credentials before running an Ansible provisioning playbook instead of letting the first AWS task fail?","a":"<p>It fails fast with a clear message, before any partial changes are made, and it makes the wrapper show which account is in use. A failure midway through a provisioning playbook can leave half-created infrastructure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0586","card":"q-ansible-098-speed-up-a-slow-playbook-across-many-ins","sec":"ansible-s099","q":"Scenario: a playbook takes too long across many AWS instances. How would Python optimize it?","a":"<p>Parallelize with <code>concurrent.futures</code> or group instances into batches, and validate instance availability with <code>boto3</code> first.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nimport subprocess\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\n\ndef run_ansible_playbook(instances, playbook_path):\n    ec2 = boto3.client(&quot;ec2&quot;)\n    response = ec2.describe_instances(InstanceIds=instances)\n    valid_instances = [i[&quot;InstanceId&quot;] for r in response[&quot;Reservations&quot;] for i in r[&quot;Instances&quot;] if i[&quot;State&quot;][&quot;Name&quot;] == &quot;running&quot;]\n\n    def execute_playbook(instance_id):\n        subprocess.run([&quot;ansible-playbook&quot;, playbook_path, &quot;-i&quot;, f&quot;{instance_id},&quot;], check=True)\n        print(f&quot;Playbook executed on {instance_id}&quot;)\n\n    with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=5) as executor:\n        executor.map(execute_playbook, valid_instances)\n\n# Example usage\nrun_ansible_playbook([&quot;i-1234567890abcdef0&quot;, &quot;i-0987654321fedcba0&quot;], &quot;deploy.yml&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0587","card":"q-ansible-098-speed-up-a-slow-playbook-across-many-ins","sec":"ansible-s099","q":"In Ansible, what are the first settings you try when a playbook is slow across hundreds of hosts, before writing any Python parallelism?","a":"<p>Raise <code>forks</code> (default 5), enable SSH <code>pipelining</code>, keep <code>ControlPersist</code> connection reuse on, turn off fact gathering when facts are not used (<code>gather_facts: false</code>) or cache facts, use <code>strategy: free</code> where tasks are independent, and run long tasks with <code>async</code>. Also remove tasks that are slow by themselves, such as repeated package cache updates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0588","card":"q-ansible-098-speed-up-a-slow-playbook-across-many-ins","sec":"ansible-s099","q":"This Python code runs one <code>ansible-playbook</code> process per instance with <code>-i f\"{instance_id},\"</code>. What is wrong with it?","a":"<p>The inline inventory host is the EC2 instance ID, which is not an address Ansible can connect to, so it needs <code>ansible_host</code> set to the IP or DNS name. Also, one process per host loses Ansible's own parallelism, repeats startup and fact gathering, and <code>check=True</code> raises on the first failure. A single run with a proper inventory and higher <code>forks</code> is simpler and faster.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>subprocess.run([&quot;ansible-playbook&quot;, playbook_path, &quot;-i&quot;, f&quot;{instance_id},&quot;], check=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0589","card":"q-ansible-098-speed-up-a-slow-playbook-across-many-ins","sec":"ansible-s099","q":"In Python, what happens to exceptions raised inside functions submitted with <code>executor.map(...)</code> if you never read the results?","a":"<p>They are silently lost. <code>map</code> returns an iterator, and an exception is re-raised only when that result is read. Iterate over the result (<code>list(executor.map(...))</code>) or use <code>submit</code> and check <code>future.result()</code> so that failures are noticed.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=5) as executor:\n    executor.map(execute_playbook, valid_instances)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0590","card":"q-ansible-098-speed-up-a-slow-playbook-across-many-ins","sec":"ansible-s099","q":"Why would you use <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> rather than <code>ProcessPoolExecutor</code> when each task just launches a subprocess?","a":"<p>The work is waiting on an external process, which is I/O-bound, and threads release the GIL while waiting. Threads are lighter than processes and are enough here. Use processes for CPU-bound Python code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0591","card":"q-ansible-099-automate-rds-backups-with-python-and-ans","sec":"ansible-s100","q":"Scenario: automate the backup of an AWS RDS database using Python and Ansible. How would you design it?","a":"<p>Use <code>boto3</code> to create the snapshot and wait until it is available, and an Ansible playbook to orchestrate the run.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import boto3\nimport time\n\ndef create_rds_snapshot(db_instance_id, snapshot_id):\n    rds = boto3.client(&quot;rds&quot;)\n    rds.create_db_snapshot(DBInstanceIdentifier=db_instance_id, DBSnapshotIdentifier=snapshot_id)\n    print(f&quot;Creating snapshot {snapshot_id}&quot;)\n\n    while True:\n        response = rds.describe_db_snapshots(DBSnapshotIdentifier=snapshot_id)\n        status = response[&quot;DBSnapshots&quot;][0][&quot;Status&quot;]\n        if status == &quot;available&quot;:\n            print(&quot;Snapshot created successfully&quot;)\n            break\n        time.sleep(30)\n\n# Example usage\ncreate_rds_snapshot(&quot;my-db-instance&quot;, &quot;my-snapshot-2023&quot;)</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code># Ansible playbook: backup_rds.yml\n- name: Backup RDS\n  hosts: localhost\n  tasks:\n    - name: Run Python script to create RDS snapshot\n      command: python create_rds_snapshot.py\n      environment:\n        AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: &quot;{{ lookup(&#x27;env&#x27;, &#x27;AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID&#x27;) }}&quot;\n        AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: &quot;{{ lookup(&#x27;env&#x27;, &#x27;AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY&#x27;) }}&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0592","card":"q-ansible-099-automate-rds-backups-with-python-and-ans","sec":"ansible-s100","q":"In boto3, why is a <code>waiter</code> better than a hand-written <code>while True</code> loop that polls snapshot status?","a":"<p>A waiter such as <code>rds.get_waiter('db_snapshot_available')</code> has built-in polling interval, maximum attempts and error handling. The loop here has no timeout, so a snapshot that ends in a failed state keeps the script spinning forever. If you do write a loop, add a deadline and handle failure states.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>while True:\n    response = rds.describe_db_snapshots(DBSnapshotIdentifier=snapshot_id)\n    status = response[&quot;DBSnapshots&quot;][0][&quot;Status&quot;]\n    if status == &quot;available&quot;:\n        break\n    time.sleep(30)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0593","card":"q-ansible-099-automate-rds-backups-with-python-and-ans","sec":"ansible-s100","q":"In AWS RDS, what is the difference between automated backups and manual snapshots?","a":"<p>Automated backups run in the backup window, support point-in-time recovery within the retention period (0 to 35 days), and are deleted with the instance unless you keep them when deleting. Manual snapshots are taken on demand, have no expiry, and stay until you delete them, so they suit pre-change checkpoints and long-term retention.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0594","card":"q-ansible-099-automate-rds-backups-with-python-and-ans","sec":"ansible-s100","q":"Why is <code>time.sleep(30)</code> polling inside an Ansible <code>command</code> task a poor design compared with a native module?","a":"<p>The task is opaque: Ansible cannot tell whether anything changed, it is not idempotent, and it needs <code>boto3</code> and a script on the control node. A native module (for example the RDS snapshot module in <code>amazon.aws</code>) reports <code>changed</code>, supports check mode and handles waiting with a <code>wait</code> option.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0595","card":"q-ansible-099-automate-rds-backups-with-python-and-ans","sec":"ansible-s100","q":"In this task, why is passing AWS keys through <code>environment</code> with <code>lookup('env', ...)</code> a weak way to give credentials to a script?","a":"<p>It relies on long-lived keys sitting in the control node's environment and can leak into logs. <code>lookup('env', ...)</code> also reads the control node, not the target. Prefer an IAM role (instance profile) on the control node, or short-lived credentials from STS, so no keys need to be handled in the playbook.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>environment:\n  AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: &quot;{{ lookup(&#x27;env&#x27;, &#x27;AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID&#x27;) }}&quot;\n  AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: &quot;{{ lookup(&#x27;env&#x27;, &#x27;AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY&#x27;) }}&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0596","card":"q-ansible-099-automate-rds-backups-with-python-and-ans","sec":"ansible-s100","q":"How would you keep RDS backups safe from a regional failure?","a":"<p>Copy snapshots to another region (<code>copy_db_snapshot</code> with a source region, encrypted with a key in the destination region), and optionally share them with a separate account. Automate it with a scheduled job and test restores regularly, because an untested backup is not a recovery plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0597","card":"q-ansible-100-ansible-failing-on-kubernetes-nodes-with","sec":"ansible-s101","q":"Scenario: a playbook fails on certain Kubernetes nodes due to network issues. How would Python diagnose and resolve it?","a":"<p>Use the <code>kubernetes</code> client to check node status, <code>boto3</code> to verify network configuration (VPC, security groups), then restart or replace the problematic nodes.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from kubernetes import client, config\nimport boto3\n\ndef diagnose_k8s_nodes(namespace):\n    config.load_kube_config()\n    v1 = client.CoreV1Api()\n\n    nodes = v1.list_node()\n    for node in nodes.items:\n        for condition in node.status.conditions:\n            if condition.type == &quot;NetworkUnavailable&quot; and condition.status == &quot;True&quot;:\n                print(f&quot;Node {node.metadata.name} has network issues&quot;)\n                # Check AWS network configuration\n                ec2 = boto3.client(&quot;ec2&quot;)\n                response = ec2.describe_instances(Filters=[{&quot;Name&quot;: &quot;private-dns-name&quot;, &quot;Values&quot;: [node.metadata.name]}])\n                instance_id = response[&quot;Reservations&quot;][0][&quot;Instances&quot;][0][&quot;InstanceId&quot;]\n                ec2.reboot_instances(InstanceIds=[instance_id])\n                print(f&quot;Rebooted node {node.metadata.name}&quot;)\n\n# Example usage\ndiagnose_k8s_nodes(&quot;default&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":false},{"id":"ansible-f0598","card":"q-ansible-100-ansible-failing-on-kubernetes-nodes-with","sec":"ansible-s101","q":"In Kubernetes, which node condition signals a network problem, and how do you read it with the Python client?","a":"<p><code>NetworkUnavailable</code> is a node condition set by the network plugin or cloud provider when the node network is not set up correctly. Conditions are in <code>node.status.conditions</code>, each with a <code>type</code> and a <code>status</code> string of <code>\"True\"</code>, <code>\"False\"</code> or <code>\"Unknown\"</code>, so compare against the string.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>v1 = client.CoreV1Api()\nfor node in v1.list_node().items:\n    for condition in node.status.conditions:\n        if condition.type == &quot;NetworkUnavailable&quot; and condition.status == &quot;True&quot;:\n            ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0599","card":"q-ansible-100-ansible-failing-on-kubernetes-nodes-with","sec":"ansible-s101","q":"In the Kubernetes Python client, what is the difference between <code>config.load_kube_config()</code> and <code>config.load_incluster_config()</code>?","a":"<p><code>load_kube_config()</code> reads credentials from a kubeconfig file (default <code>~/.kube/config</code>), which fits a laptop or CI runner. <code>load_incluster_config()</code> uses the service account token mounted in a pod, which fits code running inside the cluster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0600","card":"q-ansible-100-ansible-failing-on-kubernetes-nodes-with","sec":"ansible-s101","q":"Why is rebooting a Kubernetes node straight from a diagnosis script risky, and what should happen first?","a":"<p>Pods on the node are killed without a graceful shutdown. First cordon the node so no new pods land on it, then drain it so pods are evicted and rescheduled (respecting PodDisruptionBudgets), and only then reboot or replace it. On managed node groups, terminating the instance and letting the Auto Scaling group replace it is cleaner.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0601","card":"q-ansible-100-ansible-failing-on-kubernetes-nodes-with","sec":"ansible-s101","q":"A playbook fails only on a few Kubernetes nodes with timeouts. How do you tell whether the cause is the network or Ansible?","a":"<p>Test from the control node with plain <code>ssh</code> and <code>ping</code> to the failing host, run <code>ansible &lt;host&gt; -m ping -vvv</code> to see where the connection fails, and check security groups, network ACLs, route tables and DNS. If the same hosts also report <code>NetworkUnavailable</code> or fail health checks, the problem is the node network. If plain SSH works but Ansible does not, look at users, keys, Python on the target and <code>ansible_host</code> values.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0602","card":"q-ansible-101-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"ansible-s102","q":"In Ansible, what do you need installed on the control node to use the <code>amazon.aws</code> modules, and why is <code>hosts: localhost</code> used?","a":"<p>The <code>amazon.aws</code> collection and the Python libraries <code>boto3</code> and <code>botocore</code>. The modules call the AWS API from the control node, so there is nothing to connect to over SSH. Running against <code>localhost</code> (with a local connection) keeps the task on the control node, where <code>boto3</code> is installed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0603","card":"q-ansible-101-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"ansible-s102","q":"In <code>amazon.aws.ec2_instance</code>, how do you keep a playbook from creating a new instance each time it runs?","a":"<p>The module is idempotent by the <code>Name</code> tag and the filters you pass: if an instance matching them exists, it is not created again. Use a stable <code>name</code> (or <code>filters</code> and <code>exact_count</code>) so repeated runs find the same instance. Without a stable identity, instance IDs differ and you get duplicates.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Launch EC2 instance\n  amazon.aws.ec2_instance:\n    name: &quot;my-ec2-instance&quot;\n    key_name: &quot;my-key-pair&quot;\n    instance_type: &quot;t2.micro&quot;\n    image_id: &quot;ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0&quot;\n    region: &quot;us-east-1&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0604","card":"q-ansible-101-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"ansible-s102","q":"In <code>amazon.aws.ec2_instance</code>, how do you stop or terminate an instance with the same module?","a":"<p>Set <code>state</code>. Use <code>state: stopped</code> to stop, <code>state: running</code> to start, and <code>state: absent</code> to terminate. The default <code>state: present</code> creates the instance if missing and does not change the running state of an existing one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0605","card":"q-ansible-101-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"ansible-s102","q":"In <code>ec2_instance</code>, what does <code>key_name</code> do, and what happens if the key pair does not exist in that region?","a":"<p>It names the EC2 key pair whose public key is placed on the instance for SSH login. Key pairs are regional, so a name that does not exist in the chosen <code>region</code> makes the launch fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0606","card":"q-ansible-101-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"ansible-s102","q":"Why should the AMI ID not be hard-coded for EC2 provisioning?","a":"<p>AMI IDs differ per region and old images get deprecated or replaced. Look up the latest image with <code>amazon.aws.ec2_ami_info</code> filters or an SSM public parameter, so the same playbook works in any region and uses a current image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0607","card":"q-ansible-101-create-an-ec2-instance","sec":"ansible-s102","q":"How would you wait until a new EC2 instance accepts SSH before configuring it in Ansible?","a":"<p>Register the launch result, take its public or private IP, and use <code>ansible.builtin.wait_for</code> on port 22 (or <code>wait_for_connection</code> after adding the host with <code>add_host</code>). The module's <code>wait: true</code> only waits for the instance state, not for SSH to be ready.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0608","card":"q-ansible-102-create-an-rds-instance","sec":"ansible-s103","q":"In the <code>amazon.aws.rds_instance</code> module, how should the master password be supplied, and what else should be set on the task?","a":"<p>Keep <code>rds_master_password</code> in Ansible Vault (or fetch it from a secrets manager) and set <code>no_log: true</code> on the task so the value does not appear in output. Better still, let RDS manage it through Secrets Manager where supported.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>amazon.aws.rds_instance:\n  identifier: &quot;my-rds-instance&quot;\n  engine: &quot;mysql&quot;\n  username: &quot;admin&quot;\n  password: &quot;{{ rds_master_password }}&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0609","card":"q-ansible-102-create-an-rds-instance","sec":"ansible-s103","q":"In RDS, why do you provide a DB subnet group, and what must it contain?","a":"<p>A DB subnet group tells RDS which subnets in the VPC it may place the instance in. It must contain subnets in at least two Availability Zones, which also enables Multi-AZ. Use private subnets for databases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0610","card":"q-ansible-102-create-an-rds-instance","sec":"ansible-s103","q":"In RDS, what is the difference between Multi-AZ and a read replica?","a":"<p>Multi-AZ keeps a synchronous standby in another AZ for automatic failover and high availability; the standby does not serve reads (for a classic Multi-AZ instance). A read replica is asynchronous, serves read traffic for scaling, and can be promoted manually for disaster recovery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0611","card":"q-ansible-102-create-an-rds-instance","sec":"ansible-s103","q":"In RDS, which settings should you review before putting a database into production besides engine and size?","a":"<p>Multi-AZ, backup retention, storage encryption (<code>storage_encrypted</code>) with a KMS key, deletion protection, a private subnet with <code>publicly_accessible</code> off, a security group that allows only the application, and parameter groups. Encryption cannot be turned on later for an existing unencrypted instance except through a snapshot copy and restore.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0612","card":"q-ansible-103-create-an-s3-bucket","sec":"ansible-s104","q":"In S3, why can bucket creation fail even with a valid name, and what are the naming rules?","a":"<p>Bucket names are globally unique across all AWS accounts, 3 to 63 characters, lowercase letters, numbers, dots and hyphens. If another account already owns the name, creation fails. Do not hard-code common names; add an account ID or environment suffix.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0613","card":"q-ansible-103-create-an-s3-bucket","sec":"ansible-s104","q":"In <code>amazon.aws.s3_bucket</code>, which options would you add to make a bucket production-safe?","a":"<p>Enable <code>versioning</code>, default encryption, and public access blocking (<code>public_access</code> settings), and add tags and a policy that denies insecure transport. Add lifecycle rules for storage cost control. By default new buckets block public ACLs in modern AWS accounts, but state it explicitly in code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0614","card":"q-ansible-103-create-an-s3-bucket","sec":"ansible-s104","q":"In Ansible, how do you delete an S3 bucket with the <code>s3_bucket</code> module and what stops it from working?","a":"<p>Use <code>state: absent</code>. A bucket that still has objects cannot be deleted unless you set <code>force: true</code>, which removes the objects first. A versioned bucket also needs old versions deleted, so be careful with <code>force</code> in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0615","card":"q-ansible-104-create-a-vpc","sec":"ansible-s105","q":"In AWS, how do you choose the CIDR block of a VPC, for example <code>10.0.0.0/16</code>?","a":"<p>Pick a private range (RFC 1918) that is large enough for the subnets you need and does not overlap with other VPCs, on-premises networks or VPNs you may connect to. Overlapping ranges make peering and Transit Gateway routing impossible. A <code>/16</code> gives about 65,000 addresses and is the largest size allowed for a VPC.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0616","card":"q-ansible-104-create-a-vpc","sec":"ansible-s105","q":"In Ansible, what else do you still need after <code>ec2_vpc_net</code> before an instance can reach the internet?","a":"<p>Subnets, an internet gateway attached to the VPC, a route table with a <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> route to that gateway associated with the subnet, and a security group. A VPC on its own has only a default route table and no internet path.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0617","card":"q-ansible-104-create-a-vpc","sec":"ansible-s105","q":"In Ansible, how do you reuse a VPC ID created in one task in later tasks?","a":"<p>Add <code>register: vpc</code> to the task and read the ID from the result, for example <code>{{ vpc.vpc.id }}</code>. Registered results are the usual way to pass IDs between provisioning tasks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0618","card":"q-ansible-105-create-a-security-group","sec":"ansible-s106","q":"In this <code>ec2_security_group</code> task, what is the risk of the rule, and how would you fix it?","a":"<p>SSH is open to the whole internet, so the instance is exposed to scans and brute force. Restrict <code>cidr_ip</code> to an office or VPN range, or avoid SSH completely and use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>rules:\n  - proto: tcp\n    ports: 22\n    cidr_ip: &quot;0.0.0.0/0&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0619","card":"q-ansible-105-create-a-security-group","sec":"ansible-s106","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between a security group and a network ACL?","a":"<p>A security group is attached to an instance or network interface, is stateful (return traffic is allowed automatically), and has allow rules only. A network ACL is attached to a subnet, is stateless (you must allow return traffic), supports allow and deny rules, and is evaluated in rule number order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0620","card":"q-ansible-105-create-a-security-group","sec":"ansible-s106","q":"In <code>amazon.aws.ec2_security_group</code>, what happens to existing rules that are not listed in <code>rules</code>?","a":"<p>By default <code>purge_rules</code> is true, so the module removes rules that are not in your list and the group matches your definition exactly. Set <code>purge_rules: false</code> to keep other rules. Knowing this prevents surprises when someone has added a rule by hand.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0621","card":"q-ansible-105-create-a-security-group","sec":"ansible-s106","q":"In AWS security groups, how can one group allow traffic from another group instead of an IP range?","a":"<p>A rule can use another security group as the source (<code>group_id</code> in the module). Then any instance with that group is allowed, even as IPs change. This is common for app to database rules: the database group allows port 3306 from the app group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0622","card":"q-ansible-106-create-an-iam-role","sec":"ansible-s107","q":"In an IAM role, what is the difference between the trust policy and the permission policy?","a":"<p>The trust policy (<code>assume_role_policy_document</code>) says who may assume the role, for example the <code>ec2.amazonaws.com</code> service. The permission policies say what the role may do once assumed. Both are needed; a role with permissions but a wrong trust policy cannot be used.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0623","card":"q-ansible-106-create-an-iam-role","sec":"ansible-s107","q":"Why do IAM roles beat access keys for applications running on AWS?","a":"<p>Roles give temporary credentials that are rotated automatically, so nothing long-lived is stored on disk or in code. Keys can leak, need manual rotation, and are hard to scope per workload.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0624","card":"q-ansible-106-create-an-iam-role","sec":"ansible-s107","q":"In IAM, what does <code>\"Action\": \"sts:AssumeRole\"</code> with <code>\"Principal\": {\"Service\": \"ec2.amazonaws.com\"}</code> mean?","a":"<p>It lets the EC2 service assume the role on behalf of instances. The role is then attached to an instance through an instance profile.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0625","card":"q-ansible-106-create-an-iam-role","sec":"ansible-s107","q":"Why attach <code>AmazonS3ReadOnlyAccess</code> through <code>managed_policy</code> rather than writing broad permissions inline, and what is the better practice for production?","a":"<p>AWS managed policies are quick but often broader than needed. Production roles should follow least privilege with a customer-managed policy limited to the specific buckets and actions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0626","card":"q-ansible-107-create-an-ebs-volume-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"ansible-s108","q":"In AWS, why must an EBS volume be in the same Availability Zone as the instance it attaches to?","a":"<p>EBS volumes live in a single AZ and can be attached only to instances in the same AZ. To move data across AZs, take a snapshot and create a new volume from it in the other AZ.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0627","card":"q-ansible-107-create-an-ebs-volume-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"ansible-s108","q":"In Ansible, after attaching an EBS volume to an instance, what else must happen before an application can use it?","a":"<p>The volume is a raw block device. You must create a filesystem (for example with <code>community.general.filesystem</code>), mount it (<code>ansible.posix.mount</code>), and add an entry to <code>/etc/fstab</code> so it mounts after a reboot. Format it only if it has no filesystem yet, or the data is lost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0628","card":"q-ansible-107-create-an-ebs-volume-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"ansible-s108","q":"In EBS, why use <code>gp3</code> rather than <code>gp2</code>?","a":"<p><code>gp3</code> has a lower price per GB and a baseline of 3,000 IOPS and 125 MiB/s that does not depend on size, with IOPS and throughput you can raise separately. With <code>gp2</code> performance scales with volume size.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0629","card":"q-ansible-108-create-a-lambda-function","sec":"ansible-s109","q":"In AWS Lambda, what are <code>handler</code> and <code>runtime</code> in the function definition?","a":"<p><code>runtime</code> is the language environment the function runs in (for example Python 3.12 or Node.js 20). <code>handler</code> is the entry point inside the code, in the form <code>file.function</code> for Python and Node, such as <code>index.handler</code>, which Lambda calls with the event and context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0630","card":"q-ansible-108-create-a-lambda-function","sec":"ansible-s109","q":"In AWS Lambda, why does a function need an execution role, and what is the minimum it should have?","a":"<p>The role gives the function permissions to call AWS services and write logs. At minimum it needs permission to write to CloudWatch Logs (for example the <code>AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole</code> managed policy), plus only the access to other services that the code uses. Lambda must be trusted in the role's trust policy (<code>lambda.amazonaws.com</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0631","card":"q-ansible-108-create-a-lambda-function","sec":"ansible-s109","q":"In AWS Lambda, what are the ways to deliver code, and when do you pick each?","a":"<p>A zip uploaded directly (small packages), a zip in S3 (larger, and the usual way for automation), or a container image in ECR (up to 10 GB, for large dependencies or custom runtimes). Deploying from S3 lets a pipeline build an artifact once and reference it by key.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0632","card":"q-ansible-108-create-a-lambda-function","sec":"ansible-s109","q":"In Ansible, how does updating Lambda code from S3 behave if the S3 key is unchanged?","a":"<p>The module may not detect the change because the key is the same, so the function is not updated. Use a new key per build (a version or hash in the name) or a versioned object, so the deployment is traceable and rollbacks are possible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0633","card":"q-ansible-108-create-a-lambda-function","sec":"ansible-s109","q":"In AWS Lambda, what are cold starts and how do you reduce them?","a":"<p>A cold start is the extra latency when Lambda must create a new execution environment: download code, start the runtime and run initialization. Reduce it with smaller packages, lazy imports, avoiding heavy init work, provisioned concurrency, or SnapStart where supported.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0634","card":"q-ansible-109-create-an-sns-topic","sec":"ansible-s110","q":"In AWS, when do you use SNS and when SQS?","a":"<p>SNS is publish and subscribe: one message is pushed to many subscribers (Lambda, SQS, HTTP, email). SQS is a queue: consumers pull messages, each message is handled by one consumer, with buffering and retries. A common pattern is SNS fan-out to several SQS queues.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0635","card":"q-ansible-109-create-an-sns-topic","sec":"ansible-s110","q":"In SNS, which protocols can a topic deliver to?","a":"<p>SQS, Lambda, HTTP and HTTPS endpoints, email, SMS, mobile push and Kinesis Data Firehose.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0636","card":"q-ansible-109-create-an-sns-topic","sec":"ansible-s110","q":"In Ansible, why is the SNS topic task safe to run repeatedly?","a":"<p>Topic creation is idempotent by name: creating a topic that already exists returns the existing topic, so the task reports no change. That is why provisioning playbooks can be rerun.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0637","card":"q-ansible-109-create-an-sns-topic","sec":"ansible-s110","q":"In SNS, what is the difference between a standard topic and a FIFO topic?","a":"<p>A standard topic offers very high throughput, at-least-once delivery and best-effort ordering. A FIFO topic keeps strict ordering per message group and supports deduplication, with lower throughput, and delivers to a limited set of subscriber types (SQS queues in particular).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0638","card":"q-ansible-110-create-an-auto-scaling-group","sec":"ansible-s111","q":"In an EC2 Auto Scaling group, what do <code>min_size</code>, <code>max_size</code> and <code>desired_capacity</code> mean?","a":"<p><code>min_size</code> is the smallest number of instances the group will keep, <code>max_size</code> is the largest it may grow to, and <code>desired_capacity</code> is the number it tries to keep now. Scaling policies change the desired number between min and max.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>min_size: 1\nmax_size: 3\ndesired_capacity: 2</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0639","card":"q-ansible-110-create-an-auto-scaling-group","sec":"ansible-s111","q":"In an Auto Scaling group, why spread <code>vpc_zone_identifier</code> across subnets in different AZs?","a":"<p>The group balances instances over the listed subnets, so losing one AZ leaves instances running in the others and the group launches replacements. A single subnet is a single point of failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0640","card":"q-ansible-110-create-an-auto-scaling-group","sec":"ansible-s111","q":"In an Auto Scaling group, why use a launch template instead of a launch configuration?","a":"<p>Launch configurations are legacy and no longer receive new features, and AWS recommends launch templates. Templates are versioned, support multiple instance types, Spot and newer instance features. Pointing at <code>$Latest</code> or <code>$Default</code> also lets you change the template without recreating the group.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0641","card":"q-ansible-110-create-an-auto-scaling-group","sec":"ansible-s111","q":"In an Auto Scaling group, what is the difference between EC2 and ELB health checks?","a":"<p>EC2 health checks only detect instances that are not running or are impaired. ELB health checks also replace instances that the load balancer marks unhealthy because the application stopped answering. Use ELB checks for groups behind a load balancer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0642","card":"q-ansible-110-create-an-auto-scaling-group","sec":"ansible-s111","q":"In an Auto Scaling group, what is the difference between target tracking and step scaling?","a":"<p>Target tracking keeps a metric near a target (for example average CPU at 50%), and AWS computes the scaling steps itself. Step scaling lets you define alarm thresholds and how many instances to add or remove at each step. Target tracking is simpler and usually the first choice.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0643","card":"q-ansible-111-attach-a-security-group-to-an-ec2-instan","sec":"ansible-s112","q":"In AWS, how many security groups can an instance have, and how are their rules combined?","a":"<p>Each network interface can have several security groups (the default limit is 5, which can be raised). Their rules are merged as a union: traffic is allowed if any attached group allows it. There are no deny rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0644","card":"q-ansible-111-attach-a-security-group-to-an-ec2-instan","sec":"ansible-s112","q":"In AWS, if you change the security group of a running instance, does it restart or drop existing connections?","a":"<p>No restart is needed. Changes to the groups and rules apply almost immediately. Existing tracked connections stay open even if a new rule would no longer allow them, while new connections follow the new rules.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0645","card":"q-ansible-112-attach-an-iam-role-to-an-ec2-instance","sec":"ansible-s113","q":"In AWS, why does an EC2 instance need an instance profile and not just an IAM role?","a":"<p>An instance profile is the container that passes a role to an EC2 instance. The console creates one automatically with the same name, but the API and Ansible need it created explicitly. An instance profile holds one role.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0646","card":"q-ansible-112-attach-an-iam-role-to-an-ec2-instance","sec":"ansible-s113","q":"In EC2, how do applications on an instance obtain credentials from its role?","a":"<p>The SDK reads temporary credentials from the instance metadata service, which are rotated automatically before they expire. Use IMDSv2 (session-token based) and limit the hop count to protect against SSRF-based credential theft.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0647","card":"q-ansible-112-attach-an-iam-role-to-an-ec2-instance","sec":"ansible-s113","q":"In the Ansible task that attaches the instance profile, why must the profile be created before the EC2 task, and what happens if you change the role later?","a":"<p>The EC2 task refers to the profile by name, so it must exist first. Changing the role's permissions takes effect for running instances after the credential refresh, with no restart. Swapping the role inside the profile also applies without a restart.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>- name: Create instance profile and attach role\n  amazon.aws.iam_instance_profile:\n    name: &quot;my-instance-profile&quot;\n    roles: &quot;my-iam-role&quot;\n- name: Attach instance profile to EC2\n  amazon.aws.ec2_instance:\n    instance_ids: &quot;i-1234567890abcdef0&quot;\n    iam_instance_profile: &quot;my-instance-profile&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0648","card":"q-ansible-113-attach-a-subnet-to-a-vpc","sec":"ansible-s114","q":"In AWS, what makes a subnet public rather than private?","a":"<p>Its route table has a route to an internet gateway for <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> (and instances have public IPs). A private subnet has no such route and reaches the internet, if needed, through a NAT gateway placed in a public subnet.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0649","card":"q-ansible-113-attach-a-subnet-to-a-vpc","sec":"ansible-s114","q":"In AWS, why does each subnet live in one Availability Zone, and how does that affect design?","a":"<p>A subnet cannot span AZs. For high availability, create one subnet per tier in each of at least two AZs, and spread instances across them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0650","card":"q-ansible-113-attach-a-subnet-to-a-vpc","sec":"ansible-s114","q":"In AWS, how many IP addresses in a <code>/24</code> subnet can you actually use?","a":"<ol start=\"251\">\n  <li>A <code>/24</code> has 256 addresses and AWS reserves five in every subnet (network address, VPC router, DNS, reserved for future use, and broadcast).</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0651","card":"q-ansible-114-attach-an-internet-gateway-to-a-vpc","sec":"ansible-s115","q":"In AWS, what does an internet gateway do, and does it cost anything?","a":"<p>It provides a route for traffic between the VPC and the internet and performs one-to-one NAT for instances with public IPs. It is horizontally scaled and highly available, and has no hourly charge. Data transfer is charged.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0652","card":"q-ansible-114-attach-an-internet-gateway-to-a-vpc","sec":"ansible-s115","q":"In AWS, how many internet gateways can a VPC have?","a":"<p>One. An internet gateway attaches to a single VPC and a VPC can have only one.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0653","card":"q-ansible-114-attach-an-internet-gateway-to-a-vpc","sec":"ansible-s115","q":"In AWS, why is an internet gateway not enough for an instance in a public subnet to reach the internet?","a":"<p>The subnet route table needs a default route to the gateway, the instance needs a public IPv4 address (or an Elastic IP), and the security group and network ACL must allow the traffic.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0654","card":"q-ansible-114-attach-an-internet-gateway-to-a-vpc","sec":"ansible-s115","q":"In AWS, what is the difference between an internet gateway and a NAT gateway?","a":"<p>An internet gateway allows two-way connections for instances with public IPs. A NAT gateway lets instances in private subnets start outbound connections to the internet while blocking inbound connections started from outside. A NAT gateway has an hourly and per-GB cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0655","card":"q-ansible-115-attach-a-route-table-to-a-subnet","sec":"ansible-s116","q":"In AWS, what is a route table and how is a destination matched when several routes fit?","a":"<p>A route table is a list of rules sending traffic for a destination CIDR to a target such as a gateway, NAT or peering connection. The most specific route wins (longest prefix match), and every table has a local route for traffic inside the VPC.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0656","card":"q-ansible-115-attach-a-route-table-to-a-subnet","sec":"ansible-s116","q":"In AWS, what happens to a subnet that is not associated with any route table?","a":"<p>It uses the VPC's main route table. Associating a custom table with a subnet explicitly is safer, because changing the main table changes every subnet that relies on it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0657","card":"q-ansible-116-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"ansible-s117","q":"In a CloudWatch alarm, how do <code>period</code>, <code>evaluation_periods</code> and <code>threshold</code> combine?","a":"<p>The metric is aggregated over each <code>period</code> (300 seconds). The alarm goes to ALARM when the value breaches the threshold for the number of <code>evaluation_periods</code> in a row (here two periods, about 10 minutes of average CPU over 80%). Longer windows reduce false alarms but slow detection.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>period: 300\nevaluation_periods: 2\nthreshold: 80\ncomparison_operator: &quot;GreaterThanThreshold&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0658","card":"q-ansible-116-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"ansible-s117","q":"In CloudWatch, why does the alarm need <code>dimensions</code> with <code>InstanceId</code>?","a":"<p>Metrics are identified by name, namespace and dimensions. <code>CPUUtilization</code> exists for every instance, so the dimension picks the instance. Without it the alarm would not find the metric.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0659","card":"q-ansible-116-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"ansible-s117","q":"In CloudWatch, what are the three alarm states, and what is <code>alarm_actions</code> for?","a":"<p><code>OK</code>, <code>ALARM</code> and <code>INSUFFICIENT_DATA</code>. <code>alarm_actions</code> lists what to trigger on moving to ALARM, such as an SNS topic, an Auto Scaling policy or an EC2 action.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0660","card":"q-ansible-116-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"ansible-s117","q":"In CloudWatch, why is CPU alone not enough to judge an EC2 instance, and what must be installed for memory or disk metrics?","a":"<p>Default EC2 metrics cover CPU, network and disk I/O, but not memory or filesystem usage, because they are visible only inside the operating system. Install the CloudWatch agent to publish them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0661","card":"q-ansible-116-create-a-cloudwatch-alarm-for-an-ec2-ins","sec":"ansible-s117","q":"In CloudWatch, what is the difference between basic and detailed monitoring for EC2?","a":"<p>Basic monitoring publishes metrics every 5 minutes at no cost. Detailed monitoring publishes every 1 minute for an additional charge, which allows faster alarms and scaling reactions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0662","card":"q-ansible-117-create-a-dynamodb-table","sec":"ansible-s118","q":"In DynamoDB, how do you choose a partition (hash) key?","a":"<p>Pick an attribute with many distinct values that spreads requests evenly, such as a user ID. A key with few values or one dominant value (a date, a status) creates hot partitions and throttling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0663","card":"q-ansible-117-create-a-dynamodb-table","sec":"ansible-s118","q":"In DynamoDB, what is the difference between provisioned and on-demand capacity?","a":"<p>Provisioned mode, the mode in this config, sets fixed read and write units per second; it is cheaper for steady, predictable traffic, can use auto scaling, and throttles over the limit. On-demand mode charges per request and scales automatically, which suits spiky or unknown traffic.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>read_capacity: 5\nwrite_capacity: 5</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0664","card":"q-ansible-117-create-a-dynamodb-table","sec":"ansible-s118","q":"In DynamoDB, what is the difference between a partition key alone and a partition key with a sort key?","a":"<p>With only a partition key, each item is found by its unique key. With a sort key, several items share a partition key and are ordered by the sort key, so you can query ranges (<code>begins_with</code>, <code>between</code>) within one partition.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0665","card":"q-ansible-117-create-a-dynamodb-table","sec":"ansible-s118","q":"In DynamoDB, when do you use a Query and when a Scan?","a":"<p>Use Query with the key condition to read items efficiently from one partition. Scan reads the whole table, uses a lot of capacity, and should be avoided in application paths. If you need other access patterns, add a global secondary index.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0666","card":"q-ansible-117-create-a-dynamodb-table","sec":"ansible-s118","q":"In DynamoDB, why does the module need <code>hash_key_type: \"S\"</code>?","a":"<p>DynamoDB requires an attribute type for key attributes: <code>S</code> for string, <code>N</code> for number and <code>B</code> for binary. Other attributes are schemaless and typed when written.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0667","card":"q-ansible-118-create-an-elastic-ip-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"ansible-s119","q":"In AWS, why use an Elastic IP instead of the instance's normal public IP?","a":"<p>A normal public IP is released when the instance stops and changes after restart. An Elastic IP is a static address owned by your account that you can remap to another instance, which suits allow-lists and failover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0668","card":"q-ansible-118-create-an-elastic-ip-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"ansible-s119","q":"In AWS, is an Elastic IP free, and what is the pitfall?","a":"<p>Since 2024, AWS charges for all public IPv4 addresses, including Elastic IPs, whether attached or not. Before that, an unattached or extra Elastic IP was charged and one attached to a running instance was free. Release addresses you do not use.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0669","card":"q-ansible-118-create-an-elastic-ip-and-attach-it-to-an","sec":"ansible-s119","q":"In AWS, what are better alternatives to an Elastic IP for reaching a service?","a":"<p>A DNS name in front of a load balancer, or Route 53 records. Load balancers give a stable name that survives instance replacement, spread load, and avoid tying clients to one instance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0670","card":"q-ansible-119-create-a-load-balancer","sec":"ansible-s120","q":"In AWS, when do you use an Application Load Balancer and when a Network Load Balancer?","a":"<p>An Application Load Balancer works at layer 7 (HTTP and HTTPS), with host and path routing, redirects and WebSocket support. A Network Load Balancer works at layer 4 (TCP, UDP, TLS), handles very high throughput with low latency, and provides static IPs per AZ.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0671","card":"q-ansible-119-create-a-load-balancer","sec":"ansible-s120","q":"In an ALB, what is the difference between a listener, a rule and a target group?","a":"<p>A listener checks for connections on a port and protocol. Rules in the listener match conditions (host, path, headers) and forward to a target group. A target group is the set of targets (instances, IPs, Lambda) with health checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0672","card":"q-ansible-119-create-a-load-balancer","sec":"ansible-s120","q":"In an ALB, what does <code>scheme: \"internet-facing\"</code> change compared with <code>internal</code>?","a":"<p>An internet-facing load balancer has public IPs and DNS that resolves to them, and must sit in public subnets. An internal one has only private IPs and serves traffic inside the VPC or over private links.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0673","card":"q-ansible-119-create-a-load-balancer","sec":"ansible-s120","q":"In an ALB, why must you list subnets in at least two Availability Zones?","a":"<p>The load balancer needs nodes in multiple AZs for availability, and AWS requires at least two for an Application Load Balancer. It spreads traffic across healthy targets in the enabled zones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0674","card":"q-ansible-119-create-a-load-balancer","sec":"ansible-s120","q":"In an ALB, how do health checks affect traffic?","a":"<p>The load balancer probes each target on a path and port. Unhealthy targets are taken out of rotation until they pass again, which is also what lets an Auto Scaling group replace failed instances.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0675","card":"q-ansible-120-attach-a-security-group-to-an-rds-instan","sec":"ansible-s121","q":"In AWS, what should the inbound rule of a database security group look like?","a":"<p>Allow the database port (for example 3306 or 5432) only from the application's security group, not from <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>. The database stays in private subnets and is not publicly accessible.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0676","card":"q-ansible-120-attach-a-security-group-to-an-rds-instan","sec":"ansible-s121","q":"In <code>rds_instance</code>, what does <code>apply_immediately: true</code> change?","a":"<p>RDS applies the modification now instead of waiting for the next maintenance window. For a security group change this is usually harmless, but for changes that cause a reboot or downtime, for example some instance class changes, an immediate apply causes an outage, so choose it deliberately.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>vpc_security_group_ids: [&quot;sg-12345678&quot;]\napply_immediately: true</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0677","card":"q-ansible-120-attach-a-security-group-to-an-rds-instan","sec":"ansible-s121","q":"In AWS, why can an application fail to connect to RDS after a security group change, and how do you debug it?","a":"<p>The rule may point at the wrong source, the port may be wrong, or the instance may not be in the VPC or subnet you expect. Check the group rules, route tables and network ACLs, confirm the endpoint DNS name resolves to a private IP, and test from the app host with a TCP check to the port.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0678","card":"q-ansible-121-create-an-rds-snapshot","sec":"ansible-s122","q":"In RDS, how do you restore from a snapshot, and does it overwrite the original database?","a":"<p>Restoring creates a new DB instance with a new endpoint from the snapshot. It never overwrites the original. Then you point the application to the new endpoint or rename instances to switch over.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0679","card":"q-ansible-121-create-an-rds-snapshot","sec":"ansible-s122","q":"In RDS, why take a manual snapshot before a risky change such as a major version upgrade?","a":"<p>A manual snapshot is a restore point that stays until deleted and is not removed by the retention window. If the change fails, you can restore to the state before it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0680","card":"q-ansible-121-create-an-rds-snapshot","sec":"ansible-s122","q":"In Ansible, how can you give snapshots unique names when a playbook runs on a schedule?","a":"<p>Add a timestamp or build number to the identifier, for example <code>\"{{ db }}-{{ ansible_date_time.iso8601_basic_short }}\"</code> (fact gathering required) or a <code>lookup('pipe', 'date +%Y%m%d%H%M')</code>. A fixed identifier fails on the second run because the snapshot name already exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0681","card":"q-ansible-121-create-an-rds-snapshot","sec":"ansible-s122","q":"In RDS, how do you stop snapshot storage costs from growing, and why can't a snapshot of an encrypted instance be unencrypted?","a":"<p>Delete old manual snapshots with a retention job or use AWS Backup lifecycle rules. A snapshot of an encrypted instance is always encrypted with the same KMS key; copying lets you re-encrypt with another key, but not decrypt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0682","card":"q-ansible-122-create-a-route-53-record","sec":"ansible-s123","q":"In Route 53, what is an alias record and why use it instead of a CNAME?","a":"<p>An alias is a Route 53 record type that points to an AWS resource such as an ALB, CloudFront distribution or S3 website. Unlike a CNAME it can be used at the zone apex (<code>example.com</code>), queries to it are free, and it follows the resource's changing IPs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0683","card":"q-ansible-122-create-a-route-53-record","sec":"ansible-s123","q":"In DNS, why can you not create a CNAME at the zone apex?","a":"<p>A CNAME means the name has no other records, but the apex must hold SOA and NS records. The DNS rules forbid the combination, which is why Route 53 provides alias records for the apex.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0684","card":"q-ansible-122-create-a-route-53-record","sec":"ansible-s123","q":"In Route 53, what are the main routing policies?","a":"<p>Simple, weighted (traffic split by percentage), latency-based, failover (primary and secondary with health checks), geolocation, geoproximity and multivalue answer. Use weighted for canary releases and failover for disaster recovery.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0685","card":"q-ansible-122-create-a-route-53-record","sec":"ansible-s123","q":"In DNS, what is TTL and how does it affect a change to a record?","a":"<p>TTL is how long resolvers cache an answer, in seconds. A low TTL makes changes spread faster but increases query volume; a high TTL reduces load but delays changes. Lower the TTL before a planned migration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0686","card":"q-ansible-122-create-a-route-53-record","sec":"ansible-s123","q":"In the <code>route53</code> module, what does <code>state: present</code> do when a record already exists with a different value?","a":"<p>With <code>present</code>, the module fails if the record exists with a different value unless you set <code>overwrite: true</code>. Use <code>state: absent</code> to delete a record.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0687","card":"q-ansible-123-attach-a-lambda-function-to-an-sns-topic","sec":"ansible-s124","q":"In AWS, what two things are needed for an SNS topic to invoke a Lambda function?","a":"<p>A subscription (protocol <code>lambda</code>, endpoint the function ARN) and a resource-based permission on the function that lets <code>sns.amazonaws.com</code> invoke it, restricted to the topic ARN. Creating the subscription from the console adds the permission; in code you add it yourself with <code>lambda_policy</code> or the API.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0688","card":"q-ansible-123-attach-a-lambda-function-to-an-sns-topic","sec":"ansible-s124","q":"In AWS, how does SNS invoke Lambda: synchronously or asynchronously, and what does that mean for failures?","a":"<p>Asynchronously. Lambda queues the event and retries failed invocations twice by default, and you can send final failures to a dead-letter queue or an on-failure destination. Functions should be idempotent because messages can be delivered more than once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0689","card":"q-ansible-123-attach-a-lambda-function-to-an-sns-topic","sec":"ansible-s124","q":"In AWS, when should an SQS queue sit between SNS and Lambda?","a":"<p>When you need buffering, retries with control over batch size, backpressure for a slow consumer, or a place to inspect failed messages with a dead-letter queue. Direct SNS to Lambda is simpler for light, fast processing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0690","card":"q-ansible-123-attach-a-lambda-function-to-an-sns-topic","sec":"ansible-s124","q":"In an SNS subscription with Lambda, how can you deliver only some messages to a function?","a":"<p>Set a subscription filter policy on message attributes (or payload for supported modes). SNS then delivers only matching messages, which saves invocations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0691","card":"q-ansible-124-create-an-ecs-cluster","sec":"ansible-s125","q":"In AWS, what is an ECS cluster and does it cost anything by itself?","a":"<p>It is a logical grouping of tasks and services and the capacity they run on. The cluster has no charge; you pay for the compute (Fargate tasks or EC2 instances) used by it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0692","card":"q-ansible-124-create-an-ecs-cluster","sec":"ansible-s125","q":"In ECS, when do you use Fargate and when EC2 launch type?","a":"<p>Fargate is serverless: no instances to patch or scale, and you pay per task vCPU and memory. Use the EC2 launch type when you need specific instance types (GPU), daemon-style tasks, cheaper capacity at high steady load, or full control of the hosts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0693","card":"q-ansible-124-create-an-ecs-cluster","sec":"ansible-s125","q":"In AWS, how do you choose between ECS and EKS?","a":"<p>ECS is simpler, AWS-specific, and has less to operate. EKS runs Kubernetes, which is portable, has a large ecosystem and more flexibility, at the cost of more complexity. Pick EKS when you already use Kubernetes tooling or need portability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0694","card":"q-ansible-125-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"ansible-s126","q":"In ECS, what is a task definition and how does it differ from a service?","a":"<p>A task definition is the blueprint for the containers: image, CPU and memory, ports, environment, roles and logging, versioned in revisions. A task is a running copy of it. A service keeps a desired number of tasks running, replaces failed ones and connects them to a load balancer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0695","card":"q-ansible-125-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"ansible-s126","q":"In an ECS task definition, what is the difference between <code>execution_role_arn</code> and the task role?","a":"<p>The execution role is used by ECS itself to pull images from ECR, fetch secrets and write logs. The task role is used by the application code in the container to call AWS services such as S3 or DynamoDB. Keep them separate for least privilege.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0696","card":"q-ansible-125-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"ansible-s126","q":"In ECS Fargate, what does <code>network_mode: \"awsvpc\"</code> mean?","a":"<p>Each task gets its own elastic network interface and private IP in the VPC, with its own security group. Fargate requires <code>awsvpc</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0697","card":"q-ansible-125-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"ansible-s126","q":"In ECS, why does <code>run_task</code> differ from creating a service for a web application?","a":"<p>Running a task starts it once and does nothing if it stops, which suits batch and one-off jobs. A web app needs a service so ECS restarts failed tasks, keeps the count, registers them with a load balancer and does rolling deployments.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">YAML</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>amazon.aws.ecs_task:\n  cluster: &quot;my-ecs-cluster&quot;\n  task_definition: &quot;my-task&quot;\n  launch_type: &quot;FARGATE&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"ansible-f0698","card":"q-ansible-125-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"ansible-s126","q":"In ECS on Fargate, what does <code>assign_public_ip: \"ENABLED\"</code> do, and when is it needed?","a":"<p>It gives the task a public IP so it can reach the internet, for example to pull an image from a public registry, when it sits in a public subnet. In private subnets use a NAT gateway or VPC endpoints and leave it disabled.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"ansible-f0699","card":"q-ansible-125-create-a-task-definition-and-attach-it-t","sec":"ansible-s126","q":"In ECS, why must Fargate <code>cpu</code> and <code>memory</code> be a valid combination?","a":"<p>Fargate accepts only fixed pairs, for example 256 CPU units (0.25 vCPU) with 512 MiB, 1024 MiB or 2048 MiB. An unsupported pair makes registration fail.</p>","hand":true}];
